      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>HMIS OT THE MODERN WOIEAN.</p>
        <p>Lord Meath, who may be described as th• Specialist on the British Empire, is asking pet tinently if we are not, as a nation, losin, "grit" ? The ladies, it appears, especially do servo censure, for if, as he avers, they "ahem courage and endurance in sport and eociety.' they are sadly wanting in "discipline and self- control in daily duties." Lord Meath, it ir obvious, declares Ella Hepworth Dixon in the Lady's Pictorial, knows little of the habits of the modern woman in society if ho thinks she tacks discipline and self-control. He has not heard of the Swedish exercises and calisthenics. the rubbings and drubbings, and the streams o' cold water which begin milady's day. Nor Lae he counted her abstinence from alcohol, her rigidly performed outdoor exercise, her self-con- trol in the matter of food, or the strict, hygiene to which she submits her person. This twentieth- century censor ,thinks the modern woman extra- vagant—but extravagance is the note of the day, and if she dresses sumptuously it is usually because her husband and her adorer will not ecuntenanco her joining the army of frumps. Then the multitudinous interests and duties to which the modern woman is thrall are not pos- sible withaut mothod, order, and punctuality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SCARF REGNANT.</head>
        <p>THE SCARF REGNANT.</p>
        <p>We are in the way to return to the perennial fichu, a mode which is never very long out of fashion, says the Globe. It is heralded by the latest draping of the fashionable scarves. They are no longer a separate item of a costume, bur are an incorporate part of the dress, and are spreading over the shoulders in a way which would render them fichus at once if they were frilled. In lace this resemblance is particularly marked. A pretty grey frock, for instance, in soft material, has for its only trimming, boyond a little vest of net, a wide scarf of lace, draped over the shoulders, and crossing in front, thus forming the whole 'of the front of the bodice. It is drawn round the waist, knotted at the back under a paste buckle, and allowed to fall in even ends to the hem. At the back of the shoulders it is arranged in a square. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>SMART NEW SKIRT.</head>
        <p>A SMART NEW SKIRT.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our illustration is an attractive skirt of new and very becoming shape. This skirt is a two-piece sheath model, and promises to become quite one of the most fashionable shapes for the comiiig winter. The seams are arranged down the middle of • the back and the middle of the front, and the front seam is made to button from top to bottom, the buttons being covered with material to match the skirt, and</p>
        <p>the buttonholes practical affairs. The opening is in front, and entrance is made into the skirt by. undoing a few buttons at the top. At a point some little distance above the knees the buttoned seam is sewn firmly down to prevent its being accidentally pulled undone. -.Round the bottom come three very wide tucks, a short spite° being left between each tuck. This skirt would look well carried out in tweed, serge, cashmere, cloth, or frieze. THE VALUE OF APPEARANCE&amp;</p>
        <p>Do we women think too much of our appear- ance? Someone asked me this question the other day, writes Mrs. Neish in M.' A. P., and I promptly replied; "You may; I don't." This : was hardly polite, but I was not intentionally , rude. I own quite frankly that I consider my appearance a good deal, and, moreover, I think everyone should consider not only how she looks, but how nice she cau look. In this grey old world, which we could make so much more rosy, we are apt to consider too little how to adorn and brighten not only our surroundings, but ourselves. There are, of course, plenty of women who give too much time to their appear- ance and spend too muc,h adorning themselves, but these are merely thd over-vain and extrava- gant few. In alluding to the average woman, I think it is woman's solemn duty to make the very best of herself and her surroundings; to Andeavour, if plain, to become as pretty as she can, and, if pretty, to preserve her beauty as long as possible.</p>
        <p>, .•61 ill 1 liiiiii . di 1 ' • 1 / • 1 lbw. I, .111 ......,1"2 ...(""___l.„,.'„.,____,lL. !, • , ...,,: i . !lif. Il i! ,4„._ 1 #lllO-1111S .-1/:' - ....-10110111Pille _ --,-,-....... • ..,..,,, ..----- , -;th; • r - - II 111114 Mike_ , , • -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>NEAT WINTER SHIRT.</head>
        <p>A NEAT WINTER SHIRT.</p>
        <p>The subject of our sketch is a simple and very neat little morning shirt, suitable for wear right through the autumn and winter. Down the front runs an unusually wide box-pleat, which is machined on either edge and taken right into the shoulder. This pleat is flanked on either side by two very wide tucks, both of which are machined at the edges, and one of which overlaps the other. Tho outer tuck pro-</p>
        <p>jects over the top of the sleeve, which is set on underneath it. The sleeves are neat little bishop affairs, and are taken at the wrist into small stitched cuffs, that open well up the arm to allow of the sleeve being rolled up at plea- sure. A plain linen collar finishes the blouse in the sketch, but if preferred this collar may be replaced by a soft turn-down collar of the mate- rial. This blouse would realise well in cash- mere, delaine, nun's-veiling, wincey, viyella, or flannel. _ _</p>
        <p>--17., cel":"*. 0.0 V .. . . . . # J 1? i,liftmltill 'hi 0 V_=----1- i 't . ---- t 1V..4 r, .g</p>
