      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>PASSING MODES.</head>
        <p>PASSING MODES.</p>
        <p>Looking round at a country house among a bevy of pretty women one sees everything to at- tract, remarks the Queen. At tea-time many are wearing the full fluffy Charlotte Corday caps ovr beautifully-dressed hair, and the princess gowns are supplemented by coats which very nearly resemble a man's dress ccat, only the back flap comes a little forward and the fronts are richly embroidered. The sleeves, cut in one, are entirely covered with the same embroidery; or, maybe, a loose coat is slipped over them, with hanging sleeves of large proportions. TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>COLD VEAL PlE.—Cut about lib. of veal cutlet into thin slices, and put into a pie-dish with half a pound of cooked ham, oeatly sliced, and two eggs boiled hard. Scatter over the meat chopped parsley, a little mixed herbs. some very finely. minced lemon-peel, pepper, salt, and a very little powdered mace. Put a little gravy over aIL newer with a rood nuff naste. ornament with pastry leaves, anct bat:o in a good steady oven for one hour and a-half. When removed from the oven, add a little dissolved gelatine in some gravy, pouring it into the pie through a funnel. Serve cold with salad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>NIGHT AT MRS. OLD CASTLE'S.</head>
        <p>A NIGHT AT MRS. OLD CASTLE'S.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Their Majesties' Xmas Cards.—Tt will be some weeks yet before we begin to think about Christnvs cards, even for sending abroad, but as far as the card manufacturers are concerned, Christmas 1908 is a past event, and they are now at work on the productions for Christmas 1107. The cards we shall be buying were all completed before midsummer, including those for Royalties. The King's Christmas card this year," A Royal Greeting," depicts King Edward 111. and the Countess of Salisbury, the scene representing the origin of the Order of the Garter 1319. " For a Happy Christmas and a Bright New Year" is the wish it bears. The Queen's card is that of a picture by Mr. J. H.Bacon —" Roman Maidens listening to the Christmas Story," 'with the verse</p>
        <p>.....-- ......_ . Still the old, old Christmas story, To faiths ear as fresh as when Angel hosts in ranks of glory Sang of God's goodwill to men. Many specially prepared Royal greeting cards are repro- duced in subsequent seasons for stle to the public. The University Trained llousewife.—A new scheme of education has been inaugurated at the Women's Department of King's College, which is nothing less than the production of the University trained house- wife. It is a new course, extending from one to three . years in " Home Science and Household Economies," finder which students will be required to know all about , domestic arts, and the whole organisation of home life. Tlie syllabus includes a wild range of studies both of a practical and sclentific nature, and the girl student must ! , not only know all about scrubbing, cleaning silver, and 1 brasses, laundry work, washing, and the elementary pro- I ! ceases of cooking, but she must understand all about ! ', the fitness of water for drinking, or cooking, relative 1 food values adulteration, chemical preservatives, every 1 phase of domestic hygiene, the application of heat,' light, sound, and electricity, in relation to the home, the proportion of income to be paid in food, rent, !, clothing, servants wages, the law as between landlord and tenant, and in fact she is to thoroughly comprehend 1 every conceivable operation of the question that can possibly come up in household management. The I ! practical domesticated woman may perhaps be inclined I to scoff at the scientific housewife preparing the mid- ! day meal with the aid of a microscope nod test tubes, I ; but the King's College authorities have decided to 1; make the range of studies as exhaustive as possible, and 1 the fees amount to about .C3O. The University trained 11 housewife will run some risk of belay, classed as a i ' "Know-All," a nickname often applied to the person . who is ready with a solution for every problem, but at ' any rate it will be useful knowledge even if theory and practice are not in such matters always one and the 1 name thing. The Wile's Effects.—The complex state of the law , 1 with regard to the personal property of married women is a frequent cause of worry to tradespeople who give ! i credit, and it is also degrading to womanhood gener- I I ally. For instance, a married woman has only n life I interest in her wardrobe, and her dresses, furs, lace,eto.,, belong not really to her, but to her husband, and even I . the presents of jewellery she may have received from her husband, are only leased to her. If that state of things induced husbands generally to be more generous , in giving presents to their wives, seeing that they are , only lent, the married woman would have less reason to be vexed with the law which declares that nothing she receives in the ordinary way from her husband, Tvally belongs to her. Of course the wife may have an income of her own, and her personal effects may be . secured to her by deed of gift from her husband, but I there is clearly need of acme amendment of the law, I which is reminiscent of the old times, when not only 1 the wife's belongings but she herself was not infre- quently classed as a chattel. Amendment of the Tarr Needed.—There is the greater , need of some amendment of the law relating to the I property and liability of husband and wife, because the I I present state of things enables dishonest people to cheat tradespecple. For instance the wife obtains</p>
        <p>all beef</p>
        <p>gocds from the dressmaker she has no intention of pay- ing. If the dressmaker proceeds against the husband he may say that he makes a proper dress allowance to his wile who has no authority to pledge his credit. If the dressmaker proceeds against the wife, she may sn'y she has no separate estate, and the dresses or goods supplied are privileged against seizure by her creditors, because they legally belong to her husband. It would almost seem as if the law in this respect was expres- sively framed to enable dishonest people to cheat with impunity, and it behoves tradesmen who give credit to beware of the pitfalls that may be prepared for them.</p>
        <p>Matrimonial Advertisements.—The State has done its best to discourage resort to matrimonial agencies, by regarding as illegal all marriage brokage contracts, or promises made upon consideration of the procuring or bringing about of marriage. But although such con- tracts are declared to be contrary to public policy, yet there are still found many people who are prepared to stake their happiness upon a matrimonial advertise- ment ; and they do not appear to be deterred by such warnings as that afforded by a case which has been before the Courts this week. It is generally the woman who is the victim in such cases, and women particularly need to beware of selecting their partners for life by such means. When a woman marries, she risks every- thing, and Heaven help her if the man proves to be a scoundrel, as he often does when the marriage has been arranged through an advertisement. Fashionable Hairdressing.—Tbe waved and parted coiffure is in favour again, a state of affairs for which no doubt Mrs. Winston Churchill is responsible, her beautifully arrayed hair having been so much admired during the past season. Not every woman however, even if she possesses a quantity of pretty Lair, can boast as perfectly a shaped head as Mrs. Churchill, and when contemplating this particular mode of hairdressing, it would surely be wise to study the face and shape of the head, before embarking upon it. A glance round a fashionable theatre in the evening will convince observers that generally speaking the waved and parted coiffure is not a success, and that it is likely to have only a passing vogue.</p>
        <p>Difficult Sleeves.—The very long and tightly-fltting sleeve, rucked or pleated, as the fabric dictates, is likely to be worn throughout the winter, nnd the home worker would do well, if she desires to achieve success in this direction, to search diligently for a really good pattern, and then follow it closely. There are few more difficult tasks one can set the amateur dressmaker than the closely-fitting rucked or pleated sleeve, for not only has the fullness to be equally distributed from shoulder to wrist, hut the sleeve must fit perfectly, any super- fluous or" bagginess " being fatal to a good effect.</p>
        <p>Autumn Leaves and Berries.—Skilfully-wired wreaths for the hair, and " trails "for the corsage, of autumn leaves, with their lovely flaming tints, are being supplied by many leading dressmakers with evening gowns, and when these go‘vns happen to be in white or cream the effect is particularly charming. Milliners too, are taking up autumn foliage and berries,especially in conjunction with fur-trimmed headgear, a knot of leaves and berries to correspond being tucked into the front of the gown. Concerning Gloves.—For knockabout purposes the kid gauntlet glove is with us, once more, gathered on elastic r,t the wrist. Soft greys and mole tints kad, tan and white being for the time out of favour. For dressy wear, suede is the vogue, and in the old-woild lavender shades which recall the days of our grand- mothers when lavender, and straw-coloured kid gloves were greatly prized and worn by all who could afford them.</p>
        <p>" Miss Charity."—A book from the pen of Mr. Keble Howard is always eagerly welcomed, and in " Miss Charity " (Hodden and Stoughton) we shall find as much to hold and interest us as we did in that delight- ful work" The Smiths of Surbiton" Mr. Koble Howard has returned to pastoral warwicks hire for the setting of his new book, and his heroine is one of the most charming he has yet portrayed.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>10 yeast</p>
