        <p>TIM PHOTOGRAPHIC ANNUAL. INCORPORATING "THE FIGURES, FACTS. &amp; FORMULE OF PHOTOGRAPHY." A CONCISE COLLECTION OF FORMUL,E, PRACTICAL WORKING METHODS, USEFUL FIGURES AND TABLES' GATHERED FROM THE EXPERIENCE OF EXPERTS IN ALL BRANCHES OF PHOTOGRAPHY. THE MOST USEFUL REFERENCE BOOK FOR AMATEUR AND PRO- FESSIONAL PHOTOGRAPHERS. is. NET, Is. 4. POST FREE. CLOTH BOUND &amp; INTERLEAVED 25. DAW BARN &amp; WARD, LTD., 6, FARRiNGDON AVENUE, LONDON, B.C.</p>
        <p>she recalled the fluttereepride With ivhich she had at first received the Prince's attentions, flattered his advances, teased him into uncompromising declaration, and laughed at him for his pains. Honest John was a man of few words : the Prince had a limitless vocabulary of fine phrases. John was sober and many called him sullen ; Frederick William was a brilliant creature—a bird of prey with the colours of a bird of Paradise. She knew that she liked the simple country gentleman, but she knew that he did not greatly amuse her. She knew that the Prince amused her, but she did not know if she liked him or the flaming name he carried. She was a high-spirited, romantic baggage., and she had told herself a thousand times that shl. would marry no man whom she did not love, and love no man who did not deserve her homage. So in her high-spirited, romantic way she had thought of a plan to test her lovers. Both of them had, in their different ways vowed a changeless fidelity, not to be tempted ways, one fleeting moment from its steadfast allegiance. Yet his Royal Highness had hinted pretty plainly that John Russ:4l could be led by the nose by any woman ; and his grace of Bedford had roundly asserted, being in a rage one day, that the son of his King was no better than a bad philanderer. Then Dye had set her snare, and the net was trailed to trip the feet of two fine gentlemen at Mrs. Oldcastle's that very night. She knew that both her suitors were going to the masquerade, she knew the colour of their dominoes, she knew the favours they would wear. At Spencer House she had pleaded the megrims as an excuse for early bed; but her con- federate maid had a chair wait ing,and when all was quiet she had slipped away, masked and hooded, to the great noisy house in Golden-square, had made her terms with its mistress, and bad entrusted her with the two letters whose composi- tion had cost her infinite pains. Now she sat wait- ing the result with a beating heart and a roguish delight at the shock it would cause some of the old court Tabbies if they knew that the pride of the great family was alone at midnight in the dancing- shop in Golden-square. As Lady Dye sat thoughtfully tumbling about her trees': e of pleasant memories, and malting merry guesses as to the result of her imbroglio, the gilt clock on the Mantelpiece struck twelve little silver notes that stirred her from her reverie. Her quick ears caught the sound of a distant footstep in the corridor, and in a second she had leaped to her feet, caught up her mask and domino, and looked about her for concealment. The Oriental screen enticed her for a second, only to be abandoned as too obvious a lurking-place, and she ran quickly to the window and ensconced herself behind its heavy curtains just as she heard the click of a key in the lock. Muffled in folds of crimson velvet, Lady Dye could peep unseen upon the room and witness all that took place in it. The door opened softly, and a young man entered the room, and closed-the door behind him. The new-comer was portly, florid, richly-dressed, with a distinguished carriage and a commanding glance, and Lady Dye laughed silently as she made a hidden reverence to the Prince of Wales. His mask swung on his wrist, his domino floated open from his shoulders ; in one hand he carried an open letter, in the m her a small golden key ; and tl e expression On his face was one of amused svrp he. Lady Dye knew' very well what he had been nmdine• ; the lines of liar letter danced before her, with its foreign hand and its feigned im- perfect English, impudently calculated to persuade the Prince that it came from a French nymph of the opera. It protested a sudden passion; it entreat:o its recipient to use the golden key ; it directed his steps to the dim corridor and tho distant parlour it g0i,,,„,,,ed imn to rumenr at ths precise stroke of midnight. Lady Dye, chuckling to herself at the success of her strategy, had no idea whae a naughty girl she was, and what a foolish adventure sho had embarked upon,</p>
        <p>UNIQUE GATHERING OF TEMPERANCE WORKERS TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>remarliable interest has been aroused in Temperance circles of the Lancashire, Cheshire and District by the arrangements for the Great Rally of Counties Band of Hope Workers, to be held in the Free Trade Hall, Manchester, this afternoon. Over 1,200 societies have appointed delegates of whom more than 3,000 base intimated intention to be present. The Railway Com- panies ere giving special facilities. Contingents will attend from every town and almost every village from Barrow-in-Furness in the north to Whitchurch in the south. Everything points to an attendance that will tax even the accommodation of the Free Trade Hall. The delegates will be grouned, according to the districts from which they come, in 22 sections% Each section will be distinguished by a special badge which is also the form of admission. The gathering is " unique " as the first at which individual societies from so large an area have been directly represented. Its special purpose is to launch the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union's Great Forward Movement to increase membership and funds. The Rally will be followed in the evening by a demonstration, open to the public, when the President of the Irish Board of Agriculture Right Hon. T. W. Russell, M.P., will preside, and the Rev. Canon Hicks, Rev. J. Cre*an, Mr. Philip Snowden, M.P., and 1\ Tr.J.S. igham, 11. P., will speak, and a counties choir of COO voices will cchtribute special music.</p>
