      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>The Prince read the letter again, and then looked about him. So far as he could see, the room was empty, though he made at once for the screen arta looked into the vacant room beyond. "Where in the world is the jade ?"laiisid to himself, with a era:ling impatience, as he placed his large body languidly upon the settee. Lady Dye, masked and hooded, was about to emerge from her concealment and begin her audacious trickeries, when a simple sound made her draw back again, and brought the young Prince swiftly to his feet. It was the sound of a key in the doorlock, and Lady Dye bit her lip with vexation, for she knew that part of her plan had miscarried. The missive to John Russell had summoned him to the mysterious assignation for half-an-hour after midnight, by which time she had counted that her game with the Prince would have been played to an end. Yet here, no doubt, !was John Russell at the door. The Prince, how- ever, merely accepted the sound at the door as the , sign that the fair unknown was keeping her I appointment, and lie immediately resolved to take Iher unawares. Rapidly masking, ho stepped behind the Chinese screen and waited for the door to open. The moment it did so, he saw, to his intense surprise and disgust, that the intruder was a man, masked and cloaked, who carried, as he had carried, a letter in one hand and a little gilded key in the other. "Dear me," Lady Dye thfright, in her corner, "this is a, predicament !" ff r she knew very well that the black domino with the large Ifavour of white ribbon conveale4 the sturdy form of her simple lover; and she fen, sure that he was cudgelling his honest brains to understand why an • anonymous lady who wrote in bad English implored . him so earnestly to be in that room at half-an-hour after midnight. What she did not know was that Ihonest John's watch, having stopped, lie had made a guess at the time ; and so had come upon the t' scene unexpected and too soon. . John Russell looked about ,the room, saw that it was empty, and, with an impatient shrug of his broad shoulders, was about to seat , himself when, to his intense surprise, lie was confronted by an apparition that emerged from the shelter of the Chinese screen, the apparition of a man masked like himself, and, like himself, enveloped in a voluminous black domino, which differed from his own only in the fact that ,it sported for its favour a knot of orange ribbon. Dimly suspicious that he was the victim of a hoax, John Russell stiffened himself irritably as die masked figure advanced towards him ;, and, with a. slightly- imperious gesture of salutation, requested to know the reason of his presence. Russell replied stiffly that he was present by request; the Prince asserted his prior claim, and for a few seconds Lady Dye, nestled in I her nook, heard, with mingled amusement and annoyance, a steady interchange of angry interro- gation and indignant response, which ended by each of the disputants producing his letter, and waving it in the masked face of the other. For a moment Lady Dye feared that the argument might end in an appeal to the swords which both gentle- men wore, in Which case she would have been obliged, sorely against her will, to • quit -her concealment and interfere. Before things came to this desperate pass, however, the Prince pro- posed a way out of the difficulty. He had noticed a brace of dice-boxes on the mantelpiece, and he proposed to his antagonist that they should settle their question of precedence in their interview with the unknown by casting 'a main. To such a suggestion there was only one possible answer, and in another moment the adversaries were bending over the table, and the dice were rattling in the Royal fingers. The disguised Duke called and won, and the Prince, witlia good-humoured laugh, surrendered his advantage. "There is a room yonder," he said ; "'twill be more cheerful for me to linger there than to kick my heels in the corridor. He took up a decanter of wine and a glass as he spoke. "Don't hurry yourself," he said, pleasantly, to Russell; "the time won't hang heavy in this good company," and, heedless of John's aesurunce that he had little to, say to the strange lady, the Prince disappeared into the further room, shutting the door noisily behind him. John Russell, left thus to himself, surveyed his loneliness somewhat ruefully. Ile had wrangled for his right to remain, because it was not in his nature to.budge an inc/i for any man; but he had no interest in the adventure, and heartily wished himself out of it. He was asking himself sulkily what all the pother was about, when be felt a light tap on his shoulder; and, turning, found himself face to face with a woman so closely masked and amply dominoed, that it was impossible for him to do more than guess at her age and seeming. "Where did you spring from ? " he asked, astonished, for he had heard no sound of any entrance.</p>
        <p>No was answered by .1) musical voice that seasoned. broken English with a strong French accent. "Perhaps, I dropped from the clouds," said Lady Dye, savouring maliciously her lover's bawilderment. "Perhaps I rose from the (firth. Perhaps I am a witch, and can come and go as I please, though every door be shut against me." She patted John's arm archly with her fan as she spoke, and shot mocking glances at him through the eye-holes. of her mask; but John Russell was not to be wheedled.</p>
        <p>"Madam," he said, bluntly, "what do you want of me?"</p>
        <p>The syren voice made answer with an audacity that amazed Lady Dye herself, trembling not a little behind the brazen security of her envelope. Lady Dye, deliciously inexperienced, believed that she could simulate boldness to the life, and she told Sohn as plainly as her determination to keep up her juggle of broken English permitted, that she thought him a pretty fellow, that she had taken quite a fancy to him, and was well prepared to adventure in his company on a fast and furious flirtation.</p>
        <p>John Russell listened to her stolidly, and stolidly shaok his head. lie thanked the lady, awkwardly, enough ; but Dye ndored his awkward- ness, for her good opinion of his merits—a good opinion which it would not be modest of him to admit nor courteous to gainsay; but he begged to be excused from pursuing the mysterious acquaint- ance further. She expostulated; he persisted. Honest John_was not to be shaken. "If you must know," he said, "I am sworn servant to the prettiest maid in Engand, and so long as there is a chance of winning her heart, there is no other woman in the world for me."</p>
        <p>Lady Dady Dye could have hugged him for•bis stubbornness; but she professed- extreme discom- Mare and disappointment. "Madam," said honest John, bluntly, "you have another gallant in yonder chamber; you may find him of a more affable disposition." He made as if to take his leave, but the mask detained him. "If you are so cold," she queried, you come at alt?" "Madam," John answered, "the letter might have come from one who needed help; I had no right to assume otherwise." - -</p>
        <p>"why did</p>
        <p>he mask shrugged her shoulders. "If you are en, loyal a gentleman," she said, "then I will make this much claim upon your loyalty. Will you wait awhile in yonder corridor? Lose a dance or two for my pleasure? I am something of a witch, indeed, and may tell you a wonder."</p>
        <p>Ruv•ell was for excusing himself, but the mask • pleaded with such a pretty imperiousness that he consented to stay for a time in the corridor. "You make me something of a gate-keeper," he grumbled. The mask shook her head. "It is never ill to serve a woman," she said.</p>
        <p>John Russell saluted her gravely, and went out into the corridor. The distant dance music rippled about him through the open door, and died away as it closed behind him.</p>
        <p>I Lady Dye looked after him a moment, touched and pleased. "Honest John has an honest heart," she tvhispered to herself. "I could find it in my own to hold him very dear if only there were not another wooer in the field. Now for my Royal lover." And on the thought she ran lightly across the floor, threw open the door of the further room, and called out "Entre: done 211onsetvteur." She was answered by a joyous laugh, and in another moment Frederick William stood framed in the doorway. He had cast aside his domino ; his rich suit glowed in the candlelight ; his face was flushed behind the mask which he still wore, end his gait was ever so slightly unsteady. Evidently he had not been idle in plying the flagon during his brief period of imprisonment. But he made a handsome, commanding figure, and Dye felt that there was a great deal to be said for her Royal lover as well as the fact of his royalty. "Hey I" said the young Prince. "What has become of my predecessor ?" and Lady Dye answered him in French, which she spoke very well, that he and she had not been of a mind, and so had parted company. The Prince laughed vinously. "He was lucky at the dice." he chuckled : "'tie only fair that he should be unlucky in love. He won the wager, I the wench," and he advanced towards Lady Dye with his arms affectionately extended. Lady Dye shrank back a little. "I was looking for a true lover," she stammered, and Frederick William shouted back at her boisterously that We had found one now or never.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>AWN</p>
