      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>WANTAGE HORSES IN GRAND FORM. Gentlemen that will pay well for an occasional good thing from Wantaire, please sen ct stamped address.—F. CTfßfl MIII at.,Wantage.</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>.. , , . . . -, 1 ..' , .1' . - :,E:.;,. . _ -..... . 1111 to - . '.'... t- A, ;I°. .7.'. 4 . , . -, , . - , .. , ..3. . , ~, , ..; f . , . . , : , , - , . i 1 1 i il , , . 1 . % s '-- .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>Lady Dye, finding the Prince in so playful a con— dition, began to parley; but the Prince whiffed her speech aside impatiently. "I am ever ready for adventure," he protested; you have taken is. fancy to me, or you would not have made this• assignation. Well, I have taken a fancy to you, or will take, when I see your pretty face plainly. Uncover, mistress, uncover !" and the Prince, whose movements were somewhat unsteady, moved a few paces nearer to his hostess. Lady Dye began to find that she was getting more than she had bargained for; the possible consequences of her escapade had never propheti- cally asserted themselves ; she had pictured herself as teasing two reluctant gentlemen, and finally laughing at their discomfiture, and it had never occurred to her that she might be met very much more than half-way in this fashion. But she was scarcely frightened yet; she did not believe that Royal Fred could be unmannerly; she felt a, ,wholly unreasonable sense of security in the- homage always accorded to Lady Dye, and was resolute to persevere in her enterprise. "Nay," she answered, "I am under a vow not to- unmask before midnight." The Prince's gaze travelled to the gilded Cupid— clock, and saw that tho minute hand was not far from the hour. "It is not worth while to waste precious time in punctilio," he said. "Unmask, girl, unmask," and as the lady did not obey him, he suddenly removed his own mask, saying: "Do as I tell you, child i I absolve you from your vow by my Royal prerogative. lam the Prince of. Wales. '</p>
        <p>Lady Dye dropped him a profound curtsey. "The young lion of England," she said, reveren- tially; and Frederick William laughed again. "If you choose so to call me," he said. "Well, if I am the lion of England, you are in the lion's den. Come hither, pretty prey, that I may devour you," and he began to make a roaring noise, as if he were, indeed, a lion, and to laugh lustily at his conceit in so doing. He looked very .handsome, very insolent, merry with his wine and bright with the pride of his youth; and Lady Dye thought that he might well make a better King to look at than his father. '</p>
        <p>"Hither to me, humming-bird," he cried. "Kiss me quick and *kiss me close; the night will be ashes soon if we do not blow upon the fire," and he moved again to seize her. Lady Dye slipped behind the bulwark of a table. "Stay, air, "she pleaded. "Have you the right to love me ? "</p>
        <p>.‘ivThe- right and the might," he laughed back. "Are you bound in honour to no lady?" she called desperately ; and he answered: "Not a bit," and tried to seize her. She dodged him dexter- ously, and an angry look darkened his face. "Would you play me and gainsay me?"' he grunted, and springing to the door he quickly locked it. "You are mine," he cried in triumph, but the next second he rapped out a red-hot oath.. for Lady Dyo had darted to the table where the candles stood, and with a swift succession of eager little breaths had puffed all the lights out and the room was in thick darkness. _</p>
        <p>Then the chase began, around the settee, around the harpischord. "I can hear you breathe,' growled the Prince. "I will make you squeak in a minute," and as he spoke he stumbled, and stumbling gave tongue exultingly, for he had caught at the heavy window curtains. "Let there be light," be yelled, as he dragged them back, flooding the room through the high window with the bright June moonlight and revealing Lady Dye where she crouched -panting behind a chair. Instantly he was upon. her, had caught her An his strong arms, and was dragging at her when the struggling girl startled him by,crying shrilly at the top of her natural voice:</p>
        <p>"Help, John Russell, help'" "What the plague is this ? " cried the Prince, releasing her as there came a thundering at the doors, then the angry thrust of a strong shoulder, and the frail wood yielding John Russell almost fell into the room. The Prince was masked in a moment as Bedford, unmasked and raging, glared from him to the almost fainting girl who leant against the table. Then his drawn sword gleamed in the moonlight, and he advanced dubiously upon the Prince.</p>
        <p>"You forgot yourself, my lord Duke," said Frederick, unmasking. At the sight of the Prince's face Bedford's point dropped. "if you were not my Sovereign's son I should kill you, sir 1" John Russell said. The Prince shrugged his shoulders and turned to Lady Dye. "So this was a trap!" he sneered. And the girl gasped back: "A test, sir.* "Do you think you have done well ? " asked the Prince.</p>
        <p>And the girl answered, breathlessly, "Well." The Prince made her a profound salutation. "There are other women in the world," he said, coldly. ""I hear the fiddles tuning. I shall dance again ere I sleep," and so he passed out of the door and walked briskly down the corridor humming a tune. John Russell turned to the girl. "Lady Dye, Lady Dye," he whispered, "was this Well done?" _</p>
        <p>And Lady Dye answered him defiantly: "Of course it was well done, John Russell, if I did it," and then broke into a storm of sobbing while the. young Duke caught her in his arms and soothed her tenderly. Lady Dye looked up in her lover's honest face and smiled through her tears. "I am a naughty girl," she said. "I deserve to be whipped for my' folly; but my prank has had its blessing, for it has taught me the worth of a true man.' And putting her arms round honest John's neck she kissed him gently on the lips. [Tim Esc.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>[ALL RICILITS RESERyED.]</p>
        <p>BREAK IN THE MONOTONY.</p>
        <p>BY DOROTHY BAIRD, :Author of "My Beautiful Boy,l "The Im•</p>
        <p>- Pertinence of Mr. Scroggins," "The Story 01 a Look," "The Girl with the Sweet Face," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"Hew I envy you your quiet retreat, your simple country life. Here, in the midst of Lon- don's rush and bustle, I pant for a corner such us falls to your lot, where one can draw apart for a while and breathe. But I cannot get away to see you—you live • too far away from the beaten track which lam destined to follow. Be. Sides, next week lam off to Marienbad. Aftet a season; society needs curing, and so does- -Yours devotedly, NITA OINCLAIII." . Christina Stratwe laid aside the letter with at. impatient gesture. What did Nita know of Het life? Nita. the belle of three seasons, who only remembered her existence •at intervals few and ter between. The little clock On the mantelpiece chimed nine. and Christina rose and went to the Ivindow. Outside, a dewy, bowery garden basked ic the early summer sun; beyond the garden a belt of dewy meadows, where the cows grazed ,P.PgeclV before the heat of the day should drive 'uern to the shade; beyond the meadows, hills-. zange upon range, stony, bleak, desolate. There was no sign of human habitation, and Christina, Fazing at the familiar view, felt hatred rise hot In her breast. " Flowers! yes, a wealth of them; but the ean't telk. 'Cows—hateful, languorous, stupid *things! And beyond, utter desolation. One old maid in a fossilised condition, three servants. and -it man on the 'estate'; a hamlet a mile off, and a silly provincial town eight miles away! And Nita envies me! Bah!" Yet there had been a time when Christina her- sielf had reckoned Hillstead a welcome haven— when she gloried in the flowers, the wild scenery. the soft colpurings, the ever-changing light and Shade that surrounded her. Left an orphan at the age of twenty-five, with the inadequate in come of £2O per annum, and with no education 'or capabilities to render her fit for teaching or 'nursing—the two refuges for homeless or dis- 'contented women—she was thankful to come and loelc after the aged aunt who offered her a home. For a time all went well with her. She threw -herself into country life: with zest. Her aunt was harmless, amiable, and well-meaning, and the change from her anxieties brought rest. But after a while the hopeless monotony of it got Upon her nerves. Aunt Mirfield grew bed-ridden and childish, and there was absolutely no society for the girl in the neighbourhood. She 'had not live enough to make her own interests, or to in an intellectual or artistic world of her ewn• She was only clever enough to realise the led. Sometimes the stultifying dulness, of the life she Sometimes she fancied she might grow accustomed to it and not feel it so much; at 'others she felt as if she must go insane sooner 'Or later. Perhaps she would merely drift into edrivelling old-age as Aunt Afirfield had done. tither contingency was hateful to contemplate. "If I had less brains I could grow domesti- f_ated," she had once written to a friend. "If I V.ad more I could fled engrossing interests in literature, or something of the sort—l might „Yen blossom out as an author or poetess. Being „Verde a medium person. I am simply discore eented, and nothing more." e.OrI that particular morning she was violently utscontented, for Nita's letter 'had stirred up ootne of the depths of dissatisfaction which she ordinarily preferred to. leave undisturbed. , "If only 'something would happen!" she ex- claimed to herself, as she walked down the gar- len to the gate opening upon the track—called .3- courtesy a road—which led ultimately to "entilisation. "I don't care what--birth, death, Marriage, elopement, only something, some- thing e She leant her arms on the gate and looked uown the track, stretching away, green and „ruttY, between the meadows. About half-a-mile " waY it joined an old highway. Christina spent -a good deal of her time watching that road on the chance of seeing some travellers from the nutside world. Bo it was hilly, and, as a rule, tteserted. The weekly market. carts on their letinds, a drove of cattle from some outlying 'arm, a tramp in search of, or in flight from work—these were almost the only objects that tewarded her vigilance. , Presently her eyes became riveted on a mov- ing speck on the .further reaches of the road; a Mint of the sun on polished steel told Christina that it was someone on a bicycle. 'The speck vanished behind a belt of trees, and *he would not see it again till it flashed past "the end of the Hillstead track: Eagerly she -*etched the opening. but a long time elapsed and yet the bicycle did not pass—so long a time that at last she began to wonder if- she had wtissed it. But at last it appeared, not flashing by as she Nxeted, but slowly, being pushed by its rider, a Young girl. Opposite the green lane she Dieused, hesitated, -and then turned boldly and 4,onimeneed walking towards Hillstead. Chris' "na opened 'the gate, and advanced to meet the waYfarer. Something was going to' happen—at last. Even if the lady only asked the way, or begged a drink of water before passing on, she Would have spoken to someone from the outside 'world, and life at Hillstead was one which incul- cated the duty of being thankful for the very *Mallest of mercies. e Christina's heart rose as she neared the way- rec. Her clothes looked good and well-cut. e-4oreover, she limped slightly, end it was pro- fuable that she had hurt herself, and was coming e'Dc shelter. Very likely she would stay for a few 'lours, at least. The girl lifted her head as Christina ap- v,,retteheel, shewing a bewilderingly pretty face, ~ Present rather pale, while lips and beow were contracted, evidently in pain. 'Do yoa live up at that white house?" sho *eked, Christina nodded. "Yes. Are you hurt? Will -37°11 not come up and rest a while?" The girl smiled. "I should be so glad," she and her fresh young voice was full of "i'ucf• " I managed to fall somehow at the foot (4 the hill, and I've hurt my ankle a bit. I can walk, but it is painful, and I should be so glad to rest." Christina looked at her critically. "You had Phetter let me wheel your machine," she said, a little sharply. "And take my arm. You are ieadY to drop with the pain." ThThe girl assented, reluctantly, and they slowly -s-ade their way to the house, where Christina Deedity ensconced hers new acquaintance on the</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>• e.</p>
