      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>sr;:a and examined the .Idlrt</p>
        <p>it Wil9 a</p>
        <p>nasty strain, but not serious. "Will it lay me up for long?" asked the girl, eagerly. "Two or thred days at least," replied Chris- tina. "Perhaps longer. You need not worry. We shall be so glad to keep you and look after you. We can post to your people this afternoon --or, if you would rather, Jake can ride to your home and fetch what you require. Indeed, indeed, there is no need for you to be so dis- tressed." .</p>
        <p>For the girl seemed on the verge of tears. "It isn't that," she said. " You are so very kind—only I am dreadfully disappointed."</p>
        <p>"Were you going anywhere in partimlar?" asked Christina, kindly, drawing the bandage she was fixing a little tighter. Yes, I was gbing to watch the manceuvrea between here and Pennitown. Didn't you know about them?" noticing Christina's look of -sur. prise. "No—do you mean to say that real, live soh diets are going to manceuvre in this benighted land?" .</p>
        <p>—..— "On real, live horses, with real guns and real. powder," laughed the girl, amused at her vehemence. ' The local papers have been full of it." Christina remembered some chance words she had overheard among the servants, and then put aside as, mere rumour. She sternly repressed any tendency to gossip with her domestizti. It seemed a step down the path leading to tremb- ling superstition and petty-mindedness.</p>
        <p>"This place is so deadly dull that even tho local papers savour of monotony. I grew sick of them; I never rend them," she said.</p>
        <p>"What a pity. I should advise a perusal of tha headlines at least," counselled the girl. "Perhaps my strained ankle is not an unmixed nuisance. At any rate you know of the fun in time to see something of it." But Christina shook her head.</p>
        <p>---------- to-day," she said. " Because of me," cried the girl, impetuously. "Look here, you mustn't lose your chance of gaiety like this. Oh, my dear girl, I understand. My name is Gladys Delamere, and I live in a place like this, six miles from here. I know what deadly dulness and monotony are." She sank • back a little exhausted by her vehemence. Christina felt almost bewildered. An earthquake could not have produced more commotion in her world than the advent of this girl and her news. But although she panted %3 be out in the quivering sunshine, she knew her duty lay at home with her guest. She must not be greedy of excitements. "perhaps, to-morrow--- " she began. "To-morrow they will be kr away over Leck- ington Hill oh! listen," sitting up again. "The fight must have begun,".</p>
        <p>Christina ran to the window and pulled, back the curtain. Faintly, over the sun-baked hiKs, came the fierce rattle of quick-firing, guns. The cows lifted their heads for a brief moment in languid amazement, and then returned to their grazing, imagining, no doubt, , that Dame Nature had decided on a new order of things. "How rapturottily exciting," said Christina. "Oh, go, do go," cried Gladys, noting the eagerness of her face.</p>
        <p>"No; they are coming nearer. moment the sounds grow louder" Gladys clapped her hands. "Don't you think—could you help mat—if only I could be nearer the window—" No sooner said than done. Christina wheeled the couch to the French window. By this time the household was aroused, and scurrying maids flew out to range them:wives against the fence. "Do you think," asked Christina—" is it pos- sible that they will come here?" • Her question was answered without words. Flying figures—men and horses and guns ap- peared down the for reaches of the green road, —seemed to swami up from nowhere into meadows and underwood.</p>
        <p>hark! every</p>
        <p>"Something's been altered," said. Gladys. "It was to have been right away by Pennitown." "Battles move quicliy," kaid Christina, as if in a dream,</p>
        <p>Indeed. they move more quickly than sho sup- posed. The flying squadrons were advancing towards the house at an incredible rate. Pre- sently they formed up, horses champing and impatient. "They're going to charge," screamed Chris- tina. " How exciting—how perfectly enchant- ingly exciting I" "The enemy must be somem here near. Is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>there a dell anywhere about here?"</head>
        <p>there a dell anywhere about here?"</p>
        <p>Yes, just over there—this side of that wood. They must be there—yes, I see them, infantry, just forming up to meet the charge." - -</p>
        <p>The thunder of hoofs on the turf drowned the rest of her speech. The excitement of it all entered her blood and possessed her. Forgetting her duties as hostess, she stepped from the win- dow. and ran out into the meadow. Ah! it was glorious. Oh to be a man—a man—to take part in wars, excursions, and alarms! all the exhilarating things that go to make up the life of a man who takes an active part in the doings of the world. • .</p>
        <p>With a roar the horses thundered by her. She watched them, half. dazed, so fast they crossed her lino of vision. And then a horse caught its foot on a stump almost at her feet, and in an instant horse and rider weie rolling over in a confused jumble.. Still that thunder of hoofs went on—awe • inspiring, continuous, while Christina - watched as if petrified, motionless with fear. It seemed incredible that the flying hoofs coald miss that form now lying huddled and motionless upon the meadow grass.</p>
        <p>-- - It was over at last, and Christina, trembling, knelt down beside the injured man. "Is he dead?" she whispered, pantingly, to the men who rode up. "No," said one, apparently a doctor. "Not dead, but badly hurt. Is there any place near here to which we can take him?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, the white house yonder," replied Chris- tina, eagerly. "My Aunt would be pleased." And she ran off to send the maids scurrying about their preparations. It was some little time before she could leave doctor and patient, and then she ,went down to Gladys. "We are having more than our share of excite- ment to-day," she said. " Upstairs is one of the, officers, suffering with a broken arm, and other , injuries unknown." `More exciting for us than for him," replied Gladys, laughing. "Who is he?" "I have not heard the name," replied Chris- tina, ringing for Jakes. fie was to 'fetch •neces- saries for the invalid from the town, and to call at the Delameres' for Gladys's requirements. Late in the afternoon a carriage came for Gladys. Her parents would not hear of her tres- passing on Miss Mirfield's kind hospitality. She must come home ,at once. Very reluctantly the girl obeyed. • ,</p>
        <p>MI&amp;</p>
        <p>snatr tome end see you"dire:By I are about again," she said. "Two lonely stragglers in this desert of monotony should hang together. Be- sides. I shall want to inquire after the wounded hero."</p>
        <p>And so, regretfully, yet laughing, Christina _bade adieu to the girl for whom she had con- ceived a sudden warm friendship. After all, it was, perhaps, as well that. she should go. Two invalids to tend, and a querulous aunt to occupy every spare momMt, would have been a little too much for her. With a sigh she turned from watching the disappearing carria"e to return to her duties as sick nurse.</p>
        <p>It was a long time befere Gladys redeemed her promise of calling. Her foot was slow in mend- ing, and her parents over careful. But on the first day of liberty she ordered her pony and cart and set out for her drive acioss the hills. It was a glorious summer afternoon, with a fresh wind blowing to temper the heat, and plenty of sun- shine peeping through scudding clouds—a day to rejdke the heart and send young blood coursing joyously throir.,ll the veins. But Gladys did not look particularly ha-my. The set of her features in repose was sad 'for so young a girl. Spring and summer brought back memories to her that she would fain forget. and this afternoon they were importunate. "Two years and not a word; two years and not a word!" The phrase re- peated itself. over and over in her mind, until her brain was weary. Christina greeted her at the gate. She was looking more coOtented, more conscious of a sense of well-being than on the day that she had met Gladys in the green lane, ana her welyome was hearty. "You do look jolly!" cried Gladys. "Life seems to agree with you." "I have been so fully occupied," replied Chris- tina, as if occupation necessarily meant fulness of joy. "Also, I believe, you are glad to see me," went on the girl, playfully. "Very glad. I am not often drawn to people, or wish to make friends; but I should be glarif you would be my friend, Gladys," said Christina, simply. Gladys held up her pretty face for a kiss. "Our desires jump together," she cried. "And I am glad. Dwelleics in the wilderness should help to enliven one another's dreariness."</p>
        <p>"And how long," asked Christina, smiling down on the other's beauty—" how long sdo you suppose you will be left in .the wilderness? Faces like yours are not generally left to waste their sweetness on the desert air."</p>
        <p>But Gladys turned epquickly away to hide her food. "Don't!" she said. "I, shall stay here all my life long, I think." "Is it a sad story?" asked Christina, softly. "I am sorry. I stumbled unaware.s:":...</p>
        <p>"It is all right. And—l should like you to know. I have never told anybody. It would make it easier, I think, if I could talk to someone about it." .</p>
        <p>Christina was silent, and presently Gladys con- tinued: "It was someone I met when I was stav- ing in London three years ago. Ho wasn't rich, and he asked me to wait. Well, I am just wait- ing—only it is hard, because for two years I have never had a line, and I think he must have —changed his mind." \ "Chanced his mind—with you in the question! He would have to be blind as a bat if he did that." Gladys laughed. "It seems as if I had annexed a staurAl admirer," she said. "It was because of this that I was so anxious to go to the man- ceuvres. He is a soldier, and so I take a keen in- terest in anything military. Perhaps it is foolish. His regiment is in India." "He might have exchanged," suggested Christina. "Yes; I have thought of that. And so, lam always hoping. But enough of my INN'S. Now tell me, how wags the world with you?", • -</p>
        <p>"Pleasantly," replied Christina. y hands have been 'full with my patient. Men, you must know. are not easy to nurse—though I am told that this particular specimen is better than most. lam sorry for the most! He seems very friendless, too. None of his belongings have offered to come and help." "Nursing seems to suit you, any way," re. torted Gladys,- surveying her critically. "The ogre does not appear to have used you very ill." Christina, with some vexation, felt herself blushing up to the roots of her hair. Yet, after all, why be vexed? Why not tell Gladys that in these weeks of nursing she had found new heaven and new earth? That thel long hours of watching, the alternations of hope and fear, had taught her something so wonderful that she thought monotony must be a thing of the past. Perhaps better not. She had no guarantee that her new heaven and earth were shared by its creator. If he rode away without saying a word she would be glad she had kept secrecy. "I daresay I shall survive his treatment," she answered, lightly. "He is much better. Soon he will depart, I expect." As she said the words she wondered what life would bo when he went, thinking how long and dreary the days would be without the snatches of merriment with which her charge enlivened the weeks of convalescence, how deserted the pink room would seem without the stalwart frame stretched on the couch by the window, the bright smile to greet her entrance. But perhaps in part incr he micht leave with her a bond which shoula keep them together up spite ot thriaing eartn and sea—perhaps----perhaps— "By the way," said Gladys, "you have neier told me his name. It is too absurd the way we talk of him as 'he,' as if he were the only man in the—" A step in the hall interrupted her, and the door was pushed open. "Congratulate me, Miss Strange! Downstairs all by myself! Oh, I know I've done wrong, but you were such a tyrant." The tall, fair young man came to a sudden embarrassed stop as he halted in the doorway. His thin face, pale enough before, became posi- tively ashen, and his blue eyes fixed themselves as if fascinated on the visitor s face.</p>
        <p>"You have done too much," cried Christina, springing forward. And you were getting on so well." _</p>
        <p>But a look of intense gladness was creeping into his eyes and changing his whole face, whilo the colour was flushing back into his cheeks. "Gladys!" he said. His voice told the whole story, but Christina flashed back a look at the girl for corroboration. Then she slipped quietly out of the room. . .</p>
        <p>"I can only thank Heaven I did not tell her!" she whispered to herself. But her lips were trembling. IP •</p>
        <p>• • I •</p>
        <p>"And so my little new-found friend is married, and I have lost her again," wrote Christina to Nita Sinclair, some weeks later. "Hr parents have supplied a liberal dowry, in order to render the marriage possible, for Captain Johnson is not rich, and has needy relations. He had been keeping away from her because of his poverty. Hard as it was to lose her, it would have been harder to see her fret. "And so she is gone. and another happy couple is added to the world, and my excitements are at an end; the deadly monotony of this place is closing in upon me again. And you envy me! Come and take my place for a year—six months —and you will learn contentment with your own lot. Even a break in the monotony such as I have just experienced passes with incredible swiftness into the prevailing dulness." Christina sealed her letter with a sigh, and went through the French- windows to the garden. The cows were just back from milking, and the spirit of peace was over all the land. It was ai though that which had taken place had never been. "Vat." whispered Christina to herself, "mom.. tony,. dulness—even sorrow—are easier to bear when one haa once looked joy in the face end known her.". - .</p>
        <p>(TIIE END.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
