      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY CRIIRCII.</p>
        <p>The harvest services were continued at this church on Sunday, the preachers being the Rev. J. J. Sandys, vicar of Gatacre : the Rev. J. R. Jones. vicar of Great Sankey; and the Rev. Hon. C. F. Cross, vicar of Stretton. There were large congregations. The collections were in aid of the curacy fund and realized £6l 4s. 10d. ST. THOMAS'S, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Atthis chinch on Sunday afternoon the men's Bible class (section B) held their harvest service, when there was a good attendance. Mr. Gibson gave the address. Several harvest hymns were heartily sung, Mr. Smith presiding at the organ. A collection was taken on behalf of the Sunday school funds. Sympathetic refer- ence was made to one of the members, Mr. Starkey,who was admitted to the Infirmary on Saturday evening, owing to a serious accident. At thetoonclusion of the service the flowers which had been used for decorative purposes were taken to the Infirmary by members and placed in his ward. RAGGED SCHOOL MISSION.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services were held at this mission on Sunday, and was very well attended. In the afternoon Mr. James Dodds presided and spoke in eulogistic terms of the work done at the mission amongst the very poor. Mr. R. Cameron, M.P., gave a powerful address, in which he urged upon the children the ditty of persevere:ice, and gave instances of what might be achieved by it He addressed valuable advice to the teachers. Solos were effectively rendered by Miss Leyland and Miss Jones, and the singing of the hymns was accompanied by ah orchestral band. In the evening a large congregation assembled to listen to Mrs. Moore, who gave an address on "Prepare to meet thy God."—On Monday evening a fruit banquet and entertainment took place. A capital programme was submitted, consisting of songs by Messrs. Firth and W. Wright, duets by Miss Bennett and Mr. W. Wright. and action songs by the Misses Lawless's Concert Party; The accompanists were Mrs. Firth and Miss Bennett.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. There were large congregations at this church on Sunday when the harvest thanksgiving services were continued. The preacher morning and evening was the Rev. A. S. Cameron, vicar of Weston St. Mary, Spalding, and formerly senior curate here. The words of the morning text were" Greater love bath no manthan this, that a man lay down his life for his friends," and the speaker referred to numerous instances, as in colliery and shipping disasters, and great fires, where people had come forward and willingly apd gladly risked their lives to save the lives of others. He was certain, how- ever, that our Lord, when He made use of the beautiful saying in the text, meant something infinitely harder and deeper than coming forward in a moment of excite- ment and enthusiasm and doing some heroic deed. He referred to the deliberate laying down of the whole of the life. Their Lord's life was one concentrated act of self-sacri2ce.. He bad no end of his own to serve at all, and the conclusion appeared to him (the preacher) to be perfectly clear and straightforward—that there must be in their own lives something of the self-sacrifice of Christ.—The choir rendered special music at each service, Mr. F. M. Darby, Mus. Bac., presiding at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services were held at Cairo- street Unitarian Chapel on Sunday, and were largely attended. The church had been handsomely decorated with flowers, fruit and corn. The Pastor (Rev. C. , Harvey Cook) preached in the morning on "The social meaning of harvest." He said that England's local harvest might be small, but let us not be morbid or melancholy. Giving all sympathy and help to our English brothers for the bad times they bad had, let us not forget that the sun bad shone elsewhere, that harvests had ripened in other fields, that other men speaking our tongue and other men speaking not our tongue had been thinking of and preparing for us, and were packing up and dispatching of their abundance for our necessity. One did not forget that England ought to grow more of her own food supplies, but we must be on our guard lest in advocating such measures we shut ourselves up vrithin-our insular walls, thereby making the great outside world foreigners and strangers to us. The call of the harvest, as the nations grew and became conscious of the vicinity of each other to each other, was to beget the sense of all belong- ing to all—" everyone" to use the Apostolic phrase "members one of another." We needed to be great Englanders, but more than that we needed to be greet human Catholics, men and women in whose hearts and consciousness there should he the sense of belonging to all the world, whose spiritual instinct of fellowship should be superior to all languages, geographical boundaries and political economy, in ' whose being should throb the sense of belonging to universal humanity. This thing was on the way and every harvest time breught it nearer, despite the doings of the men on the Corn Exchange or in Wall-street. There were two great reciprocal duties concerning industry constantly to be kept in mind : (Ist), we who were living and working were to be thinking of those who were to come after us (2nd), that we took up the good that had been handed to us from the past and worked it into the present as far as we could, so that when we died, the common store of noble things and principles in the world might be larger. We were not to plead in extenuation of Bulling and Bearing that it was business—had been business any way since Joseph's time. Our's it was to ask Is this the right ? Is it the test ? Ts it social? Is it in the largest interests of humanity. At the evening service Mr. Cook spoke on "The joy of harvest." The choir rendered special music in a highly creditable manner.</p>
        <p>POACHER, AND HIS GUN. Michael Dowd, a Newton collier, was flned 20s. and costs at the Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday for trespassing in pursuit of game on Lord Newton's land. Mr. A. Browne prosecuted and said defendant was seen on the land by the gamekeeper and his son, and heard shots, and on approaching defendant the latter raised his gun at the younger gamekeeper, as though to shoot, but, the father shouting out, defendant ran away. Defendant denied the offence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The Sankey Bridge Question.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The Sankey Bridge Question.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington' Rural Dis- trict Council was held-at Bewsey Chambers on Wednes- day afternoon, under the presidency of Dr. Murray. There were also present : —Messrs. G. Mather, A. Brooks, J. Bill, W. Furness, R. Forshaw, J. Backhouse, J. C. Sutton (clerk), Dr. Sephton (medical ofucer of health), B. S. Newcombe (surveyor), H. H. Burnes° (assistant surveyor), and F. Kerfoot (inspector of nuisances).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>TRAM LABOUR CONTRACTS.</head>
        <p>TRAM LABOUR CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>The Govern-Me-1;i :itTdit7Or W. Hicks) wrote as follows in reply to a letter from the Clerk comrdunioating the views of the Council re team labour —" I am obliged by yours of the 7th inst. I think : see the practical difficulties in regard to team labourl contracts, but perhaps we can arrive at some satisfactory COnclusion when I see you. Please allow me to con- gratulate you upon the well-deserved presentation made you by the Guardians." GREA.T SANKEY SANITARY ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>In ;;;;;se- to an advertisement only one tender was received fornight soil oolleotion in the township of Great Sankey. It was from Mr. Thomas Whitfield, of Penketh, who offered to do the work for £lOO per annum. He is the contractor under the present system whereby the property owners pay him for his services. Mr. FURNESS said they had had a meeting of the Parish Council and their idea in asking the Rural Dis- trict Council to advertise was not so much to get a hard and fast tender with a view to placing the charge directly on the rates but in order that they might have an opportunity of asking the tenderer if he would accept the position under the same conditions as Mr. N'Vhitfield had hitherto done the work. They were extremely awkwardly fixed In Sankey as there were so many farmers as compared with the number of house- holders and it would be very hard on the farming class that they should be called upon to pay a special rate for the collection of night soil just because a limited ' few did not pay for the collection as readily as they might do. Before accepting the tender and throwing 6d. on the rates he suggested that a parish meeting should be held. I The CHAIRMAN: You ought to do it on a smeler rate than Gd. Mr. FURNESS : A sixpenny rate wonld realize £l3O. The CLERK: About fourpence would be needed. Mr. FURNESS : We have to pay £55 out of that for</p>
        <p>lamps. On the proposition of Mr. FURNESS seconded by Mr. MATHER it was decided to refer the matter to the Parish Council. Mr. FURNESS said be was inclined to think that if a rate of sixpence bad to be levied on Sankey and Penketh was already paying eightpence for the purpose, it would be more economical to combine the two districts and have their own man and cart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Sammts-reported that dnring the month there had been 32 births and 29 deaths. The death rate was 27.9 per . .</p>
        <p>1,000 against 27, the average for the past three monihs and 813 for the corresponding month last year. Fourteen deaths occurred in the County Asylum at Winwick, so that the death rate proper for the district was 14.1 per 1,000. The folloviing cases of infectious disease had been notified :—Scarlet fever, 13 cases • typhoid, one case ; diphtheria, 3 cases, Ile would like to have the opinion of the Council as to whether all infectious cases should be sent into hospital. If so, be thought they would have to levy a rate of sixpence for that purpose only. The CLERK Hitherto it has always been left to the discretion of the doctor. Mr. MATitmt : The matter should be left in the hands of the doctor and inspector without Interference. Dr. SEPHTON said it had always been his practice where a case could not be isolated or have proper attention, to order the patient into hospital. Where a child could be thoroughly isolated he did not think there was any necessity to send it into hospital. He thought it would be as well to continue that practice, although offensive remarks might sometimes be made by people who thought they should remove them. 'I he CLERK said that when the people who were complaining got the demand for a larger rate they would probably complain more. Mr. MATHER : If they pay any rates at all. Dr. SEPHTON remarked that the sooner they got a sewage scheme for Padgate the better it would be. The report was adopted. SANKEY BRIDGE'. THE COUNTY COUNCIL'S ATTITUDE..</p>
        <p>The SuivFvoi submitted correspondence on the question of the proposed new bridge at Sankey Bridges. On September 3rd Mr. Compton Hall, the County Bridgemaster, wrote stating that he was desired by the Chairman of the Main Roads and Bridges Committee to suggest alternative dates for the joint conference to further consider the proposal for improving the bridge. He asked Mr. Newcombe to confer with Mr. Longdin and let him know the result as early as possible. On September 10th Mr. Newcombe replied "In conversation Mr. Longdin tells me that he would not be able to attend further meetings of the joint committee, in face of a resolution of the Borough Council not to help the scheme financially. I certainly think the question should not be allowed to drop on this account, our Council feeling very strongly that it is more a matter for careful examination of the legal responsibilities of the railway company and thorough discussion We should do all in our power to find out the exact legal position and if necessary bring pressure to bear upon the company." Mr. Compton Hall replied on September 15th in the follow- ing terms: "I have conferred with my Chairman upon your letter and the general situation arising out of the refusal of the Warrington Corporation to attend any further conferences on the ground that they do not intend to give any financial assistance to the scheme for a new bridge. My Chairman therefore considers it would,he quite useless to hold the conference suggested. At the same time I think we might usefully obtain legal opinion as to the extent of the liability of the railway company for the pro- vision and maintenance of an adequate bridge. I hope you will make it es widely known as possible that the Main Roads and Bridges Committee was prepared to do everything reasonable in support of the scheme, the</p>
        <p>lIIL</p>
        <p>importance of which they fully appreciate, and that the onus of dropping the matter must rest open the Cor- poration." Mr. FURNESS : That is an absurdity. It is ridiculous to talk like that. Do I understand the County Council do not intend to call us together or take any action in the matter until they have had legal advice as to the position the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway Company occupy with respect to the bridge? The SURVEYOR: That is so, according to the last letter (raw Mr. Hall, but they will have enough pressure brought to bear by Warrington independently of us. Mr. FURNESS : Ido not think we should rely on the Warrington Corporation. We should push the thing ourselves as a District Council. As a matter of fact, from my knowledge of the interviews we have had with the committee, the Warrington Corporation have done us more harm than good. They have stood but dog in the manger sort of position. The SURVEYOR said the Corporation considered it was a matter of the legal responsibility of the County Council. It affected the borough very much and they thought they had a right to urge the Oouncil to act as speedily as possible. Mr. FURNESS : The position is perfectly clear. If the London and North-Western Railway Company are not responsible for putting the bridge in a condition to meet the present traffic,then it is a standing disgrace to the County Council to allow the matter to lie dormant and take no drastic action. They should either do the work or force the company to do it. The CHAIRMAN : When it first cropped up I was under the impression that the County Council was going to take legal opinion. It was ultimately decided that the Clerk write the County Council asking them to take legal advice as soon as possible as to the liability of the railway company with a view to bringing the matter to an early conclusion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
