      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>INFIRMARY CONCERT.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The annual concert promoted by the Workingmen's I Committee with the praiseworthy object of assisting the funds of the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary was held in the Parr Hall on Saturday evening and proved interesting from a musical point of view as well as financially successful. The crowded attend- ance in the popular paits of the hall was a striking testimony to the enthusiastic exertions of members of the committee in the disposal of tickets. As usual, Mr. T. J. Down's orchestra was responsible for a large part of the programme. We understand this was the twentieth occasion on which either the orchestra or the military band formed by the late Mr. T. J. Down had appeared at the Infirmary concert,and it would be impossible to speak too highly of the public spirit of these instrumentalists in annually giving their servioes on behalf of such a deserving institution. On Saturday, under Ur. Arthur Down's conductorship, they submitted a number of light pieces with a good measure of success, special interest attazh ing to the dainty "Nell Gwyn" dances, written in Edward German's characteristic "Old English" style. The overture "Agnes" (Paer) was another bright and tuneful item, pleasingly performed. The vocal part of the enter- tainment was sustained by Miss Marie Houghton (soprano), Mhos Hilda Nicholas (contralto), Mr. John Willett (tenor), and Mr. Cuthbert Allen (bass) and afforded considerable enjoyment. Miss Marie Houghton, although suffAt ing from a sore throat, sang i " When the heart s young" (Dudley Bach) and the waltz song from .‘ Tom Jones, ' (German) with artistic taste and the appreciation of the audience was so cordially expressed that she had to bow her acknowledgments several times. Miss Hilda Nicholas in her interpretations of "Husheen " (Needham) and " Aberfoyle ' evidenced true artistic feeling and con- siderable refinement of expression, receiving therefor well-merited applause. The singing of Mr. John Willett, the tenor, in the two wings, "I hear you calling me" (Maraud° and "My last treasure " (Nicholls) marked a great advance upon his recent performance in the Palace and Hippodrome, and one is thus lead to the conclusion that, sandwiched as he then was between heavy pieces by Crosfield's brass instrumentalists, he was necessarily heard under very unfavourable conditions. But, be this as it may, it is due to Mr. Willett to say that his voice and method seemed on Saturday evening quite happily attuned to the spirit of the two songs just mentioned —the firet of uhich is of a particularly. dainty _ . .</p>
        <p>character—and for his tceteful rendiring of them he was loudly acclaimed by the audience. Mr. Cuthbert Allan has a pleasant, robust voice and he is especially at home in breezy,swinging songs like " Lighterman Tom " ISquire), and The Drum- Major" (Newton) which he gave with a naturalness and gusto suggesting that he enjoyed them quite as much as his hearers, who encored him most warmly. Another of his contributions was '4 The Bedouin love song' (Pinsati) a more exacting song in which he was not heard to quite the same advantage. The humorous selections by Mr, Bromley Carter had the great merit of originality and pleased the audience immensely. Mr. W. IT. Payton, Mug. Bac, played the pianoforte accompar.iments with taste and skill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WHEN THIEVES FALL OUT.</head>
        <p>WHEN THIEVES FALL OUT.</p>
        <p>Alban Stirrup, of Friars' Green, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Saturday with stealing two suits of clothes from a sale-room in Queen-street, the of property Harry Comley. He pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that the first witness he would cull via William Norbury who was charged with a similar offence on the previous Wednes- day. It appeared that Norbury was in the employ of Harry Comley and so had the key of the sale-room. He went in company with Stirrup during the latter part of last week, and they both took one suit each. They decamped to Southport, and Norbury Waa arrested and brought back to Warrington and charged on the Wed- nesday, with the result that he was bound over under the Probation of Offenders' Act. Stirrup was afterwards arrested and now appeared in the dock.—Norbury stated that both he and Stirrup were drunk when they went to the saleroom and took the suits Stirrup hid them in a refuse plhce and said they would be all right until the next morning. Later a woman pawned the suits for us. and Gs. 6d., and gave the money to witness. Prisoner gave witness a blow in the face and tool( 2s. Id. from him.—Prisoner said he was not with Norbury when the suits were taken. Norbury met him in a public-house and brought the goods to him. Prisoner had seven previous convictions against nim and he WES fined 10s and costs (£3 2.3.) with the alternative of one month's. imprisonment.</p>
        <p>Miss Braddon, the novelist, who is Mrs. John Max- well in private life, celebrated her seventy-first birthday at her Richmond borne on Saturday. Her first novel was published in 1860.</p>
      </div>
