        <p>1714 OR SALH two Chestnut SIAHHS, Trap, Harness, and L Stable Fittings.—Apply Dr. Straw. Hallway rd., Leigh.</p>
        <p>Sir EDWARD GREY'S speech SIR EDWARD on the problem in the Near GREY'S SPEECH. East is one of those far-</p>
        <p>seeing, lucid, and states- manlike utterances which Britons of all parties have learnt to expect from him. The attitude of this country,as set forth by our Foreign Minister. is that treaties are sacred obligations, and cannot be broken at will by one of the parties without the consent of the othar signatories. To have taken any other line at this moment would have been to admit that treaties are of no use whatever in international relationships, that two States or more might sign a treaty to-day and that any one of them the moment it finds the treaty obligations irksome might repudiate them to-morrow. If th e Powers of Europe are to retain real control over international relationships their first duty is to Bee that treaty rights are respected. The action of Bulgaria is much more understandable than that of Austria. There can be no doubt that Austria has determined, so fer as she is concerned to repudiate the restrictions ol the Treaty of Berlin and to take a step forward in that expan- sion of her territory which has been the dream of her statesman.</p>
        <p>Austria has a settled policy in this respect. She means, in the end, to control the Danube to the Black Sea. She wants more seaboard, and the known desire other statesmen to get right through</p>
        <p>the Balkan Peninsula should be borne in mind in estimating her present motives. Probably etas is prepared that Servia, whose governors know her designs, should fight. The end might be the annexation of Servia and the final pirpose which Austro-Hungarian statesmen have in view would be co much the nearer. That part of Europe is such a veritable powder magazine that the present flourishing of torches is bound to cause anxiety. The bright spot in the history of these last few days is to be. found in the admirable restraint of the Young Turks. They were placed in a very awkward position, and their attitude has been most correct. It is, in face of the facts which have come to light, good news indeed that Sir EDWARD GREY does not find any reason to think that what has trans- pired so far will lead to any disturbance of thwe peace. England will refuse to recognise Bulgaria as an independent kingdom or Bosnia and Herze- govina as integral parts of Austria until the other signatories to the Treaty of Berlin have been consulted. If that means anything, it means that the Powers are to be asked their view on the breach of international law which has take place. It may mean a conference, or it may mean much more. If the Concert of Europe declines to recognise the breaches of the Treaty of Berlin by which we have been so surprised within the last few days it will be their duty to insist that both Austria and Bulgaria shall retrace their steps. Toe moral pressure of the rest of Europe ought to be strong enough to do this without resort to arms ; but, of course, a good deal depends on whether the Soncert is horrnonious. How for is Germany behind Austria? If the Concert of Europe should prove unable to secure respect for its own treaties, then we are in a very serious situation indeed. After what has happened, however, the Treaty of Berlin is bound to come up for reconsideration, I and mayhap for revision. It is here, as Sir EDWARD GREY points out, that new complications may arise. It may be found to be necessary to release Austria and Bulgaria from their treaty obligations, but our Foreign Minister assures us that in such a case the interests and status of Turkey shall receive full consideration and be adequately safeguarded. That assurance will be heartily welcomed by the present rulers of Turkey. All of us in England heal rejoiced in the new era which had commenced there. We had great hopes from the adoption by Turkey of Liberal principles in the government of the country, and our great fear was that outside events might have a disastrous effect on the work of internal reform. It is our duty to endeavour, so far as lies in our power, to give the reformers of Turkey a fair chance in the work they have taken in hand. All they desire, and all we desire for them, is that they should be left in peace to pursue the goad rule and government of their own country.</p>
        <p>Alderman Smethurst has</p>
        <p>THE MAYORALTY, declined to accept the Mayoralty for a third year. We cannot blame him. He has had an arduous two years and acquitted himself iu a fashion which does him lasting credit. No man could have worked harder or been more mindful of the dignity of his position. It is unfortunate that Mr. Smethurst's retirement leaves the Council in the same difficulty they were in when he fi,rst took office as Claief Magistrate. For some years the Mayoralty has practically gone begging, and it is to be had at the same cheap rate to-day. A successor to Alderman Smethurst is not available, and the General Purposes Committee after discussing the question has taken a remarkable and, in our view' not very dignified course. A deputation has been appointed to wait upon no less than seven gentlemen in the order of municipal seniority and offer them the vacant Mayoral chair. The seven who are thus to have greatness thrust upon them are Alderman Pier. point, Alderman Bennett, Alderman Burton, Alderman Forshaw, Councillor Fulton-Smith Councillor Dr. Joseph, and Councillor W. B. Roberts. It is impossible to avoid reflecting on the likely alternatives. If the first named gentle.. man were to accept the honour—as we think he ought to have done long ago—then the other six might reasonably consider that they had been thrust into unnecessary prominence. Take the contrary possibility, if the first s'x gentlemen all decline the offer in succession, then numb.er seven I will hardly feel honoured by the proffer of a position which his fellow-councillors evidently do not regard very highly. The Committee raight as well have put the Mayoralty up for auction and let it go to the highest bidder, as adopt such tactless and undignified procedure. There is an honour in being Mayor of a County Borough like Warrington, and the Council should hasten slowly lest they cause the position to be held in light esteem.</p>
        <p>tit Last night we learned that the deputation had already got to work, and that the result of trheir labours did act make the position much less embarrassing. Three of the seven gentlemen waited upon have expressed willingness to step into the breach, whin a fourth is giving the Latter his earnest consideration. Tile remaining ree have, of course, declined. Apparently the General Purposes Committee has an eve for ellect. An interesting denouement seems assured.</p>
        <p>There is a prospect of a</p>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL contest in only three of the ELECTIONS.—TontEs nine Wards up-to-date. AND PENSIONS• Orford Ward has three</p>
        <p>candidates seeking the honour of representation—Liberal, Labour, and Conservative—so that the olectors there are likely to be spoiled with choice unless they decide straightway to vote for the Liberal. Ia Town Hall Ward two well-known tradesmen will be in opposition, Mr. T. Birtles coming out against the retiring member, Mr. W. Benson. The Conserva- tives have, so far, got two new candidates in the field, one of whom possesses the bar sinister of asscciation with the liquor trade. There are already six members of the Council directly connected with the Trade, and we hope the Orford electors will think twice before they increase the number by returning Mr. Rushworth., He may be an excel- lent candidate in other respects, but ratepayers, if they really consider their best interests, will set their faces sternly against the growing influence of the Trade in Municipal Government. Mr. Rushworth does not advance any very substantial' claims for support in his election address. He preaches economy with efficiency, which is a stock text, and shows a considerable amount of pre- sumption as a Conservative candidate in trying to make use of Old Age Pensions. Mr. Rushworth says in his address : " I undertake to do all I can to provide that the granting of these pensions to the aged poor may be carried out in a spirit of courtesy and consideration and not be spoilt by the harsh callousness of a cold officialism." Seeing that his party cold-shouldered the Bill in its passage through the House of Commons, the Conservative candidate's concern for the aged poor is rather belated. It is worth while for the electors to remember that Tories promised old-age pensions and Liberals provided them. On the third reading of the Bill 12 Tories voted against, 11 for, and 140 Tories did not vote at all. If there is any " harsh callousness" about, it is easily traceable to the Tory party.</p>
        <p>There can be no doubt of it, ImraovEmENT or there is a growing tendency THE STREETS, towards brightness, shape- liness, beauty in the streets of London and other large towns. It expresses itself in the desire to clean up and re-face old</p>
