      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>buildings, and in the many quaint and bizarre attempts to evolve a new style of architecture. The mid-Victorian age, the age of which the Great Exhibition was the centre, worshipped utility and imagined that rapid mechanical development represented human progress. The best its architects were capable of was scholarly imitation, the worst is still to be seen every where, drab and ugly. A business firm saw no purpose likely to be served by expense upon sentimental ideas of form and ornateness, but was satisfied with the lowest tender that promised solid walls and respectable appearance. It cannot be that architects and others in such short while have so educated the world as to have produced a new desire for beauty. A 'bus ride from St. Paul's to the Marble Arch will give an observant person excellent opportunities to see, if only by comparison of the old with the new, that there is such a desire. Can it be that it is a reawakening of a long-dormant love; a quickening of lingering traces of emotion that primitive man felt, as lie gaz3d upon Nature and found its mysterious loveliness console his grow- ing sense of oarticulateness ? It must be so; a few dilettanti, a few connoisseurs, a few artists, a few Ruskins could never stir the vast,slow-thinking mind of the crowd. We wonder can there be a renaissance at hand, that will stir the people's hearts,and produce an architecture of the twentieth centpry that may outrival the glory of the twellth ? Can it ba that, in part, the dream of William Morris begins already to take shape ?</p>
        <p>The seems to be a strang's BRIDGES AND fatality about the various BOTCHES. bridge schemes in Warring-</p>
        <p>ton and district. Die delay in the widening of Warrington Bridge has become a standing joke to the townspeople, excepting those who have to cross it several times a day. Probably it never will be widened. If somebody would kindly blow it up in the night there might be an assured prospect of a new bridge. At present there is no prospect of anything but muddling on in the same old way. The Howley Foot-bridge, which we thought would have been in use by now, seems to have fallen under the same evil spell. Meeting after meeting is held,and the Council cannot agree upon a site. Tney are to try, try again! Then there is the interesting affair at Sankey Bridges. It is quite a long time since it was announced that a new bridge was to be built in place of the ridiculous wooden structure on the Liverpool main road at Sankey Bridges, and everybody rejoiced correspondingly. Tho kind of bridge, and its dimensions, were agreed upon. It was to be a bridge in keeping with the importance of its position, and sanguine people rejoiced. But the bridge is not a bit nearer materialising out of thq mess, muddle and make-believe than it was a year ago. Warrington will not share the cost because the County Council would not help in providing a new Warrington Bridge. The County Anthori- ties threaten to abandon the scheme altogether. Then the Rural Council steps in. They think the responsibility of the Railway Company should be tested and urge the County Council to take legal advice with a view to bringing the matter to an early conclusion.By the time the County Council,the Town Council,the Rural Council,the Railway Com- pany, and a few King's Counsel have settled their differences road-traffic will probably be a thing of the past, and aerial flights the order of the day. Therein lies the only solution, so far as we can see. The local authorities seem foredoomed to make more gaps than can ever be bridged.</p>
        <p>The rights or Englishmen are still being coafiscatesi —on the walls !</p>
        <p>* * * We hear that a gentleman of Jewish persua- sion entered the shop of a local chemist the other day and demanded six-pennyworth of laudanum. The dispenser suspected suicrde, and in stern tones said : " What do you want it for?" The reply was prompt: " Threepence." The chemist is reported to be recovering nicely!</p>
        <p>*s* The Borough Member found himself in rather strange company on Wednesday evening when he attended a smoking concert and prize distribution under the auspices of the Warrington district of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds. The gathering was held at the Conservative Club, and Ur. Crosfield was surrounded at the cross-table by such distinguished political foes as Sir Gilbert Greenall, Councillor Fulton-Smitlr, Councillor Rogers, Captain Potter, and Mr. H. Hayes. But as all Shepherds are not of Tory persuation, Mr. Crosfield had plenty of supporters in the room. His presence acted as a useful antidote to the rather strong odour of Conservatism which has emanated from the tents of the Shepherds of late. Members of both kinds of political persuasion listened eagerly to what Mr. Crosfield had to say on the subject of Old Age Pensions —which is one which closely concerns all friendly societies. I _ *s* . _</p>
        <p>In another column we pullish a letter to the Mayor from the War Office, giving particulars as to the enlistment in the Special Reserve of men aged fro= 17 to 30 years. This is the scheme devised by Mr Haldane as one way of dealing with the unemploy- ent which may reach an acute form during the winter months. The recruits will put in six months training .for the first year, and be liable to serve for 15 to 21 days during the five following years. The rate of pay will vary between is. and 10s. 2.1 d. per week according to the branch of the service the men serve in, and each man will receive a bounty of £1 a year each year he remains in the Special Reserve. To the unemployed at any rate these terms, including as they do free food, clothes, bedding, fuel and light for the winter should prove decidedly attractive. The War Minister expects to take 15,000 men off the streets by means of this scheme, and we hope his anticipations may be fully realised. * * *</p>
        <p>"Budget Night" in our -local Parliament provided an interesting debate and gave the financial geniuses in the House an opportunity to shine. The main principles of the Budget are such as will commend themselves to all progressives and we heartily con- gratulate the Chancellor of the Exchequer (Mr. E. England) upon his courageous proposals. At one sweep he abolishes the duties on food, provides for old age pensions and a substantial grant to the unemployed. His method of raising the needful will, as he puts it, be simple in practice and effective in purpose. Death duties are to be increased on a graduated scale commencing with estates of z£lo,ooo, there is to be a heavier income tax for those who are well able to bear it, and a fourth of the mining royalties are to go into the National Exchequer. Of course, the Tories raised the usual hullablaloo about persecuting the capitalist and driving him from the country, but such arguments are to be taken with a grain of salt. There is little doubt that the proposals would work admirably to the betterment of the -working classes, without pressing unduly upon the very well-to-do.</p>
        <p>[Fnou OUR LONDON CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>The crisis in the Near East, threatening grave dis- turbance of the peace of Europe, has, for the moment, thrown into the shade qiestions of domestic policy numerous and important as these are. But in spite of the uncertainty whether the lines of demarcation in Eastern Europe laid down by the Treaty of Berlin may not be obliterated through the pressure of rival ambitions for a fresh demaication to he accomplished only by the arbitramant of war, the urgency of many of our problems of social reform resumes again, after the finst shock of surprise, the first place in the public mind. There has been an exceptional activity of political oratory, from which Mr. Lloyd-George's speech at Swansea on Thursday, October Ist, and Mr. Asquith's speech at Earlston on the 3rd, stand out with especial prominence. It is noticeable that recent speech-making has been almost entirely carried on by Liberals. With the exception of Sir John Gorst's protest against the attitude of his party to the Licensing question, a single speech by Mr. Balfour has had to do duty in relieving the feelings or consciences of the Opposition. SCOTLAND'S ASPIRATIONS THWARTED.</p>
        <p>Speaking to a Scottish audience, Mr. Asquith naturally addressed himself in great measure to the land question. While the people of England are beginning to enjoy the fruits of a system which gives freer access to tbe land, tie people of Scotland have for the second time been cheated of their hope for " a new charter which will give room and occupation for the men who are bred upon the soil to iive on it and to live by it." The House of Lords, which blocks the way for the realisation in Scotland of a scheme which would revive the best traditions of her rural life, may justify to themselves the fairness of such a course ; they will never be able to make any justification that will satisfy the aggrieved nation. To the predictions of every kind of evil and disaster with which the representatives of the landlord class assailed the pro- posals to create a fresh body of small holders in Scotland and give them security of tenure, a close parallel may be found, as Mr. Asquith pointed out, in the opposition to the Crofters Act twenty years ago. All these prophecies have been falsified by the event. Class prejudice, such as that which has animated the Lords' hostility to the Scottish Land Bills, is most often absurdly short-sighed, as well as unjust and harsh.</p>
        <p>THE WIDER SOCIAL PROBLEM A large part of Hr. Asquith's speech was devoted to a careful examination of the Bill which the Lords destroyed. Such an examination, setting all the essential features in their right perspective, was the best and most complete defence. But there was one part of the speech which travelled far beyond the particular circumstances of the Scottish land question and took into its survey the whole field of constructive social reform. There is always a temptation to regard social problems as if they existed in water-tight com- partments, each by itself ; as if temperance had no 1 connection with the remedies for poverty, and the I land question had no bearing on unemployment. Ifr. Asquith reminded his audience, as we all require to be reminded very frequently, that each of these questions is a fragment of one wider and more com- plex social problem, and that is "how to mitigate, by a process not of levelling down, but of levelling up— by means which will stimulate,and not stifie,individual I energy and initiative—how to mitigate in that fashion the glaring anomalies, moral and material, of our modern society." It is all-important, as Hr. Asquith says, to recognise "the interdependence,the reciprocal relations between the various evils which cry aloud fora remedy." CUTTING OFF THE SOURCES OF SOCIAL EVILS. The Government has directed its movement towards social amelioration by a steady recognition of this inter-dependence of social evils. Inpatient critics find fault with it for not applying to one of the most striking of these evils—unemployment—some rough- and-ready treatment of the nature of the "Right to Work " Bill. That would only be to attempt to drain off, as it were, the temporary accumulation of unem- ployment, while the amount displaced was being per- petually renewed from the sources that feed it. The aim of the Government is to cut off the sources. Temperance legislation will do this to some extent, and land reform, by checking the process of depletion in the country and congestion in the town, will do even more. A system of Old Age Pensions which provide without humiliating conditions and associations for necessitous old age, is a conspicuous advance in "the mitigation of the glaring material anomalies of modern society." But it is an advance which the Government regards as only the first step, though a very important step, "towards a comprehensive and complete dealing with the allied problems of the worker who is disabled before his time and the com- plete re-classification of the subjects of our so-called Poor Law, and of the consequent readjustment of its machinery." If, as we believe, the present Govern- ment is destined to leave a deeper mark on the social problem than any Government which has preceded it, the chief reason will be that it has taken a connected view; though the exigencies of our political machinery compel it, as Mr. Asquith says, to proceed piecemeal, it still takes care not to proceed hanhazard. NATIONAL RESOURCES AND SOCIAL</p>
        <p>REFORM. But, it is vehemently proclaimed on the other hand, by its ambitions programme of social reform the Government is laying too great a strain on the resources of the community. Where is the revenue</p>
        <p>L"? h'. , • - ..1&gt; , _ _ -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. S. Windell's system for breaking a bank was an advance on Lord Rossiva's,says " London Opinion." * *</p>
        <p>The elevation of Bulgaria to the status of an independent kingdom will bring the number of kings in Europe up to twenty. Of these kings two established their dynasties but yesterday—namely, the King of Servia, who began to reign in 1903, and King Haakon of Norway, who began to reign in 1905. To which it may be added that the majority reign by right of titles which go no further back than the nineteenth century. ** * In view of Mr. Asquith's statements on land policy on Saturday, it is interesting to learn that the Small Holdings Association are going to renew their cam- paign all over England for the purpose of explaining the English Small Holdings Act. The society is prepared to send lecturers to all parts of the country on condition generally that their railway and hotel expenses are paid. In certain parts of England the county Councils are rather apathetic on the subject of small holdings, and the labourers are not too well informed as to what can be done under the English Act. Meetings and addresses held under the direction of the Small Holdings Association are likely to do much good. * * * _ _</p>
        <p>So long as you are innocent, fear nothing.— Long fellow. Want of luck generally means want of pluck.— F. E. Manning. Home is the girl's prison and the woman's workhouse.—G. B. Shaw. Howe'er it be, it seems to me, 'Tis only noble to be Rood.—Tennyson. We have to live in a provisional state, while we dream of, and work for, a better one.—H. G. Wells. Money may not be able to buy happiness, but it can buy off a great deal of unhappiness.—Ro(leriek Lyndon. The reason why professions seem to be overcrowded is because there is a crowd of loafers in them.—Dean of Peterborough The happiest woman is the one who does not marry the man she loves: then she can go on loving him. —G. B. Burgin. * * * On Tuesday Manchester received the forty-eighth of ithe series of Church Congresses which have been held lin most parts of England since 1861. This is the ' third visit to Cottonopolis —the others having been in 1863 and 1888 —and the third time the Congress has been in Lancashire for four years (Liverpool 1001, Barrow-in-Furness 1006). English Churchmen fore- gathered this week under the presidency of Bishop Knox, whose vigorous personality has given him a hold on the strenuous workers chiefly forming the people of the diocese. Th 3 regular Congress-goers among the clergy are, for the most part, well-matured men—a fact which led a barber in one town visited, who was asked his opinion about the assembly, to say (with true professional instinct) that "he thought there were a lot of baldheads among them." * _ „ .</p>
        <p>Some Lancashire Churchmen " large mass of clerical and lay opinion in Lancashire and Cheshire") have written to the Archbishop of Canterbury to " ginger" him up against any educational compromise. These Churchman are " determined to accept" no settlement not based upon the right of the parent and absolute equality of treatment as between the various denominations. If they accepted anything less than this it would be untrue to" the charge of the young souls committed to us." We desire (says the " West- minster Gazette ") to treat this intimation with all respect, but what we have to remember is that Lanca- shire is not typical of the country as a whole so far as Church schools are concerned. What the gichbishop has to consider is the whole question of the Church school, and when he does that he finds that every year the number of children in the Council schools grows absolutely and relatively larger. The ".young souls " for whom the Church cares in her day-schools I grow fewer, and what a prudent Church leader has to ponder is whether some friendly settlement is not possible, as the result of which the Church might get something by consent that she seems in some danger of losing under present conditions. * * * In his speech at Earleston, Mr. Ascluith had some pleasant things to say about the dignity of local service: It is easy to sneer, as some superfine critics do, at petty interests which are only worthy of the attention and the activity of parochial minds. It is easy to scoff, as even our great poet does, at those who think "the rustic cackle of their burgh the murmur of the world" ; but just as com- munities—the smaller communities—are made up of individuals and of families, so in the same way the State—the nation as a whole—is but an aggregate of those communities themselves, and the mainten- ance of an effective and of an ever-wakeful public interest in the local concerns of your own parish and of your own burgh is the best security in the long run for the maintenance of a high standard both of character and intelligence in the larger arena of national work.</p>
      </div>
