ll now cease for some little time but will ultimately Pensions and better provision for the poor." It was a be re-started, and hopes are entertained that more lisritorious act merely to bring this need before the Prosperous days are in store for the denomination here. fyr!hlic, without moving a finger to satisfy it; but St. John's Church has had a long and interesting e.en the Liberal Government performed, without history. It was established as a Lady Huntingdon I,oraising, what the Conservative Party promised, Church and afterwards taken over by the I.7'r a ithout .performing, they appear to Mr. Balfour an Presbyterians. The Synod gave permission for it to iPsl?ropnate object for a sneer, as if they had been be sold about twenty years ago. •n !egated by no other motive than the desire to obtain Bandmaster Receives Meritorious Service 4 little immediate popularity." Medal. Bandmaster W. Jones, late 2nd Bittalion " The King's " (Liverpool Regiment), and now serving with the sth Battalion (Territorial) of that regiment was on Sunday presented with the meritorious service medal. The ceremony took place at Orford Barracks in the presence of the whole of the troops, under the com- mand of Major C. S. Edridge, " The King's" Regiment. Major Edridge congratulated Bandmaster Jones and took the opportunity of briefly outlining to those present (chiefly Special Reserve recruits) the grand old veteran's services. He pointed out that it was within the scope of every one of them to follow the noble example set by Mr. Jones. It may be interesting to note that Mr. Jones is already in possession of other medals. He served under Lora Roberts in Afghanistan in 1879, and was present at the taking of Peiwar Ketal. He saw active service in Burmah in 1885-1887. He holds the medal for long service and good conduct. On the occasion of the visit of his Majesty the King to Ireland in 1904 his Majesty decorated him with the Royal Victorian Order. The band of the 2nd Battaliop, under his leadership, attained considerable distinction, and at the Corona- tion they wore selected for the position of Guard of Honour at Buckingham. In 1903, during the King's visit to Ireland, the band was under his direction, and. received the Royal command to play during his Majesty's lunch at the Cork Exhibition. He recently retired from the Regular Forces, after 42 years' ser- vice, a number of which were spent abroad. University Extension Lectures. The first of a series of six lectures on "Plant Life" was given by, Mr. F. E. Weiss, Mc. (ProPessor of Botany in Manchester University) on Wednesday evening in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street. Lantern illustrations enabled the Lecturer to define more clearly the processes of a plant growth. Last year the preliminary comments on this subject aroused considerable interest, and judging by the number present on this the beginning of another series, it will be well maintained. Canon Morley Stevenson in introducing Professor Weiss said he thought it was a very hopeful sign that in a town like this there should be a number of people who took an interest in the subject of the lectures. They did not always associate plants with a town of this discription, but it seemed to him that on this occasion they had a professor from Manchester and an audience from Warrington—neither of them places where particular interest was shown in the subject. They looked forward with great pleasure to this series of lectures.— Professor Weiss said it was a great pleasure to him to be asked to come a second time. People sampled'a round of the various sciences, therefore not getting a true knowledge of one subject. There was apparently a widespread desire to know more of the subject of "Plant Life." The roots and leaves were of the most vital importance; the stem performed a mechanical purpose. In a vegetable marrow or water melon there was 95 or 98 per cent. of water. To grow plants they had to find out how much salt, potash and sulyhur were required. As a field lay fallowed, a process took place •, innumerable bacteria made its way into the field. The alder, which grew along the side of a ricer, was greatly helped to grow by the absence of bacteria. The root- cap was important because it afforded a protection to the parts which would otherwise be injured. In the lily they found that the seed termin- ated two-thirds up the stem. The storage function was exhibited in the beetroot. Dealing with the function of the leaf, the lecturer said that if they exposed a leaf they got, in a form, a certain amount of starch. If they pinned a bit of cork on the leaf as shown in the illustration, they would find that the place on which the cork had been pinned did not form starch. The leaf was studded with innumerable hairs. This was to prevent the leaf from being hurt ; it must be protected from being attacked by various animal organisms. In conclusion Mr. Weiss said that the first book published in England on plant anatomy was in 1862, and the earliest plant anatomist was Nehemiah Groome.</p>
