      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, October 10, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, October 10, 1908.</p>
        <p>[struck the iron railings on the bridge which spans the road leading to Messrs. Quirk, Barton, and Burn's lead ' works. Luckily, neither of the ladies fell in the road- way beneath. Railway officials found Miss Alice Pilkington very badly hurt, and her sister in a dazed condition, on the side of the line. After first-aid treat- had been rendered, the unfortunate young ladies were conveyed to Dr. Jackson's surgery. Here it was found that, besides extensive injuries to her face and the loss of several teeth, Miss Alice Pilkington had fractured her thumb near the wrist, and badly hurt her knee. Her sister escaped with minor injuries and a severe shock</p>
        <p>_ HIGH FALUTIN' pouncs. ST. HELENS MUNIcIPAL STRUGGLE.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens municipal battle is now being carried on vigorously all over the town. Mr. R. Bruce Davidson has commenced his campaign, in Hardshaw, against the sitting Labour Councillor, Mr. Pugh, and Mr. 'N. J. Ellison has been definitely accepted as Conservative candidate for North Eccleston' the most fickle and uncertain ward in the town. In the two Sutton Wards electioneering of the most keen description will be seen during this raonth. Dr. O'Keeffe (Lab.) is attacking Councillor Dr. Bates (C.) in East Sutton, the latter being known as the " King of Sutton," while Dr. O'lieeffe has long been the " King of Parr." Mr. J. H. Bottomley, the Conservative agent for the Newton Parliamentary Division, has taken the field against Councillor Rudd, of \Vest Sutton, and the battle will be far Irons the kid-glove variety. On Monday night, in Scholes-lane School, Mr. Bottomley used metaphoric and satirical rhetoric of the most high falutin' description. He defended himself as a" paid politician " by pointing out that the Labour party's agent, Mr. Richard Waring, had become a candidate for Parr Ward. Dealing with the question of Socialism, he discussed ideals, and said that in his dreams of an ideal State they had more thinking and less drinking, smaller towns and larger villages, better homes and fewer workhouses, plenty of money, and lots of babies, happier wives and no under-fed children, cheap food and low rents, regular employment and shorter hours, greater pleasures and more excursions, cheap trains and comfortable libraries, more holidays and fewer worries, spacious playgrounds and well- equipped hospitals, gymnasiums for boys and cooking classes for girls, wider streets and effective sanitation, cleaner rivers and purer skies, religious education and industrial activity, luxury everywhere and poverty nowhere, Socialism abolished and Tariff Reform triumphant. Concluding, he said the had come out to avenge an insult to his dead employer (Colonel Richard Pilkington) by defeating Councillor Rudd. Mr. Bottomley described himself as a democratic Tory, a progressive Conservative, and a strenuous opponent of Socialism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>CYCLISTS SODDEN DEATH.</p>
        <p>A fatal accident, attributed to the heat, occurred at Halton on Saturday evening. Mr. Jacob Holland, 57, a Northwich stonemason and the oldest chorister in the Northwich Parish Church, had cycled with his son to Widnes and Runcorn, and at Halton got off his machine to light the lamp. Remarking "I feel ill," he fell on the ground dead</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>RUNCORN MAN'S SAD END.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN MAN'S SAD END.</p>
        <p>John William Dockery, who for fifteen years has been choirmaster at St. Edward's Roman Catholic Church, Irwell-lane, Runcorn, was about to retire to bed on Saturday night when he missed his footing arid fell downstairs. At the time he was smoking a pipe, and its stem penetrated the roof of his mouth. Despite the able attentions of Dr. Delaney, Mr. Dockery died on Senday evening. He leaves a wife and four children.</p>
        <p>WIDNES MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. Speculation is rife as to how many contests are likely to take place in Widnes. Victoria Ward will have a stiff fight between the retiring member, Coun- cillor Peters,and Mr. Dan Garghan. In llalton View it is not likely that Covinalor David Welding will be opposed, although rumour had it that Mr. It. Allen sought the honour. Mr. Allen has no idea of opposing Councillor Welding. So far Councillor Paul Caldwell's seat in Simms Cross has not been threatened. RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. The monthly meeting of Runcorn Urban District Council N 51,9 held on Wednesday under the presidency of Mr. T. Dean. The medical ofiicer (Dr. M`Dougall) reported that during the past month fifteen deaths and forty-two births were registered, as again.st twenty-six deaths and forty-five births in the corresponding period last year. Oe the recommendation of the Highways CJmntittee plans of a number of dwelling-houses were passed. A proposal to reduce the rents of the stalls of the market was referred back to committee. Mr. Carroll in moving the minutes of the Finance Committee, sa;d it was highly satisfactory that the takiegs at the baths or the first month were £ll6 6s. HELSBY MILK DEALER HEAVILY FINED. At Prescot,on Tuesday, George H. White, wholesale milk dealer, Itelsby, Cheshire, was charged in five instances, with selling milk to the Prescot Board of Guardians which was not of the quality and substance demanded by them. Mr. Wilson (Preston), prosecuted on behalf of the County Council, and Mr. Rudd (Liver- pool) defended.—Mr. Wilson said the contract was for three years, the price being 71d. per gallor and the annual charge for the milk ittn abaut £1,225 15s. 10d. Samples of the contents of five cans sent on one day were tested, with the following result :-1, 10 per cent. added water and 13 grains boracic acid ; 2, 14 per cent. and 12 grains • 3, 12 grains; 4, 2.77 fats, bat cream deficient ; 5, 2:35 fats, but cream deficient.—Defendant suggested that the milk, which arrived at the railway station overnight, might have been the subject of tampering tiers by some other milkman.—A fine of £5 and costs in each case was inflicted. WATER POLO MATCEI.—WIDNES v. EVERTON.</p>
        <p>The Widnes Baths on Monday night were crowded with swimming enthusiasts to witness the series of engagements to be contested. The first, confined to girls under 15 years, distance one length, was won by Miss Kerby, Miss Penny finishing second. The squadron race semi-finals created a de.il of excitement. Deacon's No. 1 was opposed to the Private Traders'. Deacon led throughout and were easy victors. Widnes Alkali and Pilkington's were opponents. The latter got off very badly and suffered defeat by fully three-fourths of a length. In the polo game against Evorton, Widnes first defended the deep end. The visitors notched the lead after a few secopds' play, but Peers equalised with a terrific shot and eventually added another from a short range. The visitors were successful in scoring t,,wo more goals before half-time arrived. The second portion was very even. Fouls were frequent. The game terminated in a draw of five goals each. The flying squadron handicap was won by Everton by two yards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the above Council held at Earlestown on Monday, Mr. W. Thompson presided, and there were also present :—Messrs. L. E. Pilkington, G. F. Clarke, W. Collingwood, A. Tunstall, M. W. Seton, T. Ritchie, with C. Cole (clerk), A. Bowes (surveyor), and R. T. Surties (water engineer.) The proceedings were of a routine character, and only lasted about three minutes.—lt was stated in the minutes that at a meet- ing of the Water Committee on September 21st, the Chairman of the Committee (Mr. C. B. F. Borrcu) and Mr. T. Ormand (chairman of the Finance Committee) reported that they had had an interview at the Water- works with Mr. Hannen (of the firm of Messrs. W. Cubitt and Co.) with reference to Messrs. Cubitt's account for the erection of the water tower, end that they had agreed on behalf of the Council to settle the contractor's claim of £2,342 11s. id. for the balance of their contract and extras by a payment of £1,300, this settlement not in any way relieving the contractors of their obligations under their contract.—lt was resolved that the action of Councillors Borron and Ormond be approved and adopted, and that they be thanked for arranging a satisfactory settlement of the matter.— The Gas Committee had considered a letter from the National Union of Gas Workers and General Labourers with reference to the conditions imposed upon the Gas Works men who are occasionally transferred to the Water Department, and applyiag for the coal wheelers and fire bearer to be paid wages at a minimum rate of ss. per shift all the year round, when it was resolved that the Gasworkers' Union be informed that the Gas Committee have had no intimation or complant from any of the men employed at the Gas Works, and that, if there are any such complaints, the committee will consider the same when brought direct to their notice by the men as in the past. FUNERAL OF LATE MRS. FORSTER.</p>
        <p>The late Mrs. Forster was interred at Earlestown on Monday. A Requiem Mass was celebrated at Burton- wood, previous to the funeral proceeding to Earlestown, the Rev. Father Ainscough officiating, assisted by the Revs. Dr. Kenny, Dr. Bennett, Dr. Carney, Father O'Reilly and Father Crook. t The chief mourners were:— Mr. James Forster, Mr. Louis Forster, Miss Forster (sons and daughters), Mrs. James Forster, Mrs. Louis Forster (daughters-in-law), Masters E. and H. Forster (grandsons),Mr. Ephraim Aultland (brother),War ring ton; Mr. F. H. 4kukland and Miss Auklaad (nephew and niece), Mr. and Mrs. W. Aukland and Master Aukland, St. Helens; Mrs. Toft, Mr. Laithwate, Wigan ; Mr. Healey, Mr. Swales, Wigan ; Mr. R. Forshaw, Mr. Hague, Mr. Mehers, Burtonwood ; Mr. E. T. Evans, Mr. Robert Evans, Mr. Thorpe, Mr. G. Moon, Mr. Higham, Mr. Unsworth, Mr. Charlton, Mr. McMahon, Mr. Siddeley, Mr. Kenny and Mrs. King. A large number of wreaths from relatives and friends of the deceased lady were placed on the coffin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>BURTON WOOD PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>BURTON WOOD PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this council was held on Monday, Mr. R. Forshaw presiding. There were also present : Messrs. J. T. iMehers, J. G. Thompson, W. Fairclough B. J. Radford. T. Hewitt, and J. Fairclough (clerk).-- Two applications for allotments having been before the Couneil for several meetings, and negotiations having been made for land adjacent to the village, it was found that the price asked by the owner was £3 per statute acre per annum, it is estimated that when the interest on the outlay for fencing, &amp;c., is added, the rent would be £3 10s. per statute acre per aunnm.—One of the applicants stated that he considered the price excessive. —Mr. Richard Forshaw was again re-appointed School House trustee as representative of the Parish Council. —A precept of Id. in the £ on the ratable value was signed on the overseers to meet expanses which it is estimated will be incurred before the 31st March next under the Burial Acts.--41 vote of condolence was passed with Mr. Thomas Forster and family in the sad bereavement they have sustained in the the death of Mrs. Forster. Mr. Forster was for many years a member of the Council.—The motion was received in silence, all the members standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>TRAGIC AFFAIR AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A tragic affair is repotted from St. Helens. At a house in North John-street there, during the early hours of Saturday morning, Robert Greeze, aged twenty-one, took some poison. He then inflicted severe wounds on each side of his throat and in the lower part of the abdomen. Dr.Challoner was called in and police assistance was reniered.The injured man was afterwards taken to the Providence Hospital in a very serious state. It is stated he had previously been an Inmate of an asylum. He was a glassmaker, and for some time had been out of work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RAILWAY LINE BLOCKED.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY LINE BLOCKED.</p>
        <p>Shortly after 9 a.m. on S iturday, a waggon of a goods train got off the line at Huyton Junction,causing much delay to the business community of Huyton, rescot, and St. Helena. The trains on the North line were delayed forty minutes. To relieve the situa- tion at Huyton Station the nine o'clock Manchester to Liverpool express was stopped by signal to take up the local passengers. At a quarter-past ten the waggon was got on the rails and shunted to Huytod Quarry siding. The ordinary traffic was then resumed.</p>
        <p>SISTERS 'FALL FROM A TRAIN. EXTRAORDINARY ACCIDENT AT Sr. HELENS. When the 6,20 train from Lime-street, Liverpool, was approaching St. Helens Station on Monday night, speed was reduced, and the train brought practically to a standstill. In one of the compartments were Misses Alice and Mary Pilkington, daughters of Mr. John Pilkington, 81, Hardshaw-streett who had been shopping in Liverpool. Thinking the train was at the platform, they gathered up their parcels, and shook hands with the lady with whom they had been conversing. Miss Alice opened the door of the compartment, and both of hem fell. The right side of Miss Alice's forehead</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>BRABAZON SALE OF WORK.</head>
        <p>BRABAZON SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>Pleasant Employment for Workhouse</p>
        <p>Inmates.</p>
        <p>The Brabazon Employment Society, which does such a noble work in providing light and interesting employment for infirm inmates of the Workhouse, held its annual sale of the articles thus made, at the Winmarleigh Cafd on Thursday afternoon. Great praise is due to the Brabazon ladies for their wholehearted exertions in this cause which sheds a ray of sunlight into many a life which would otherwise be exceedingly drab and monotonous. Their names are :—Mrs. Frost (president), Miss Ethel Appleton, Miss Lister Kaye, Miss Fairclough, Mrs. Furness, Mrs. Carruthers, Mrs. Hankey, Mrs. B. Roberts, Mrs. Smith, Miss Margaret Broadbent and Miss Constance H. Broadbent (hon. secretary). There was an encouraging attendance at the opening of the sale by the Hon. Mrs. Willis. Mr. George Mather, vice-chairman of the Board of Guardians, presided. Miss CONSTANCE BROADBENT read letters of apology for non-attendance from Mr. William Houghton (who was announced to preside), the Rev. Father Walsh, Dr. Hodgkinson, and Mr. J. Billing°, all of whom expressed their admiration for the work of the Society. Con- tinuing, Miss Broadbent said that for six months, owing to circumstances into which she need not enter, there was practically no work done in the hospital. She was sorry at the time, but now felt really glad that they had passed through that experience. It had made the people thoroughly appreciate the interesting employment the society gave them, and she thought they realised as they never did before, that the Brabazon Society , existed only to give them pleasure. On the recommen- dation of Mr. Lowry, the Local Government Board Inspector, the Guardians had given permission for infirm inmates in the house, not otherwise employed, to work for the Brabazon Society instead of restricting its sphere to the hospital as before. Certainly no-one who stopped to think about it could wish to condemn any human being to idleness who might be happily employed. They bad now nine old men busily at work, three of whom were blind. The master was still a good friend to the Society, and did all he could to help her. She wished to emphasise once and for all, the fact that no work of any kind went into the consumptive ward. Miss Broad- bent than submitted the balance sheet, which showed an income of £57 15s. 2d., and a balance in hand of £2 12s. 4id. in asking the Hon. Mrs. Willis to open the sale, Mis° Broadbent said they welcomed her not only for her own sake but for the sake of her uncle, the beloved Rector of Warrington. (Applause). The Hon. Mrs. WILLIS said she could assure them it gave her very great pleasure to open the sale and to help in a small way the good work which was being done by the Brabazou Society. It was very gratifying to think that all the varied work displayed around them had been made by the aged and infirm inmates of the Warrington Union and the greatest credit was due to all the members of the committee who took such an interest in it, and worked so hard, and to the willing' hands which had done so much to make the sale a success. (Applause). Mr. M.tTfiEll, in moving a vote of thanks to the Hon. Mrs. Willis, said the rates would be higher than they were, if it were not for the Brabazon Society obtaining employment for people who would probably otherwise be life-long inmates of the Workhouse. They heard a great deal about Socialism, but to his mind the work of that Society represented the real Socialism they should look forward to. (Applause). The vote was heartily accorded and Mr. Richard Willis briefly returned thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>NEW PRINCIPLE IN MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>A NEW PRINCIPLE IN MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>The Direct Treatment of Throat and Chest Troubles.</p>
        <p>Uselessness of Liquid Cough Mixtures.</p>
        <p>The crying need for a new principle in medicine I for throat and chest troubles was indicated both by Ithe all-too-obvious uselessness of ordinary medicines I taken into .the stomach, and by the physiological impossibility of introducing liquid medicines into the iiroat and lungs. That or ad has at last been fully satisfied by the introduction of Peps, a breath-able medicine in handy tablet form, which has already achieved world-wide fame as a sterling remedy for all ailments of the breathing organs. In order to comprehend the reason why liquid medicines and equally the liquid solution of ordinary cough lozenges, are so useless and dangerous a method of treatment for throat and chest troubles, it is neces- sary to understand that the throat is so constructed that nothing can be sieallotred into the breathing passages. The very 'act of swallowing automatically closes the windpipe, and prevents the entrance of either liquids or solids. But Peps are designed on lines both novel and natural. They provide a potent medicinal agent of the highest remedial importance which can be breathed direct into the throat and lungs. By this unique and novel method not only are all a lments and weaknesses of the breathing organs soothed, relieved, and per manently cured in a fashion which seems almost I miraculous when contrasted with old-time methods of treatment ; but the risks of vitiating the blood, upsetting the digestion, benumbing the nerves and brain, and weakening the heart by taking drowsy narcotics and sedatives into the stomach are all avoided by the natural Peps breathing treatment Peps have so simplified the direct treatment of throat and chest troubles.that ordinary coughs and colds are banished in a few hours, and even the most obstinate and stubborn chronic chest and throat troubles, such as chronic bronchitis, asthmajaryngitis, old-age coughs, wheeziness, loss of voice, and other ailments where the very structure of the bronchial tubes and lungs is weakened, clogged, obstructed, thickened, and debased by long-continued illness, are gradually but surely relieved and finally banished altogether. Yet Peps are so free from all objectionable features such as the laudanum, chloral, morphia, and other deadly drugs and narcotics so commonly found in ordinary cough-mixtures and lung-remedies, and the irritating menthol, cubebs, eucalytus, and the digestion•destroying fish oils and extracts used in common emulsions and lozenges, that Peps can be regularly used by old' or young, and by the frailest invalid.</p>
        <p>Gets Is. 11,d. or 2s. 91. box of Peps noxv :Loin the nearest chemist, and keep them handy for use when- ever the first indications of dryness in the throat, tickling cough, sneezing, huskiness, breathing diffi- culty, or that made-up feeling in the head or chest I point to the onset of throat or lung trouble. Peps have proved unequalled in many cases of chest- weakness and never fail to cure coughs, colds, bronchitis, asthma, sore throat, and all bronchial affections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Convict's Daughter."</p>
        <p>Am enthusiastic audience welcomed the third visit of Mr. Frank Bertram's Company in the powerful domestic drama " The Convict's Daughter ' at the Royal Court Theatre on Monday evening. The British public has a curious fondness for stories of convict life and the more realistically a melodrama depicts what goes on behind the prison walls the better chance it has of succeeding. Mr. Gordon Holmes' play presents the eternal struggle of villainy versus virtue and fora time the powers of evil have matters all their own way. But right triumphs in the end and the villain is made to bite the dust. There are many stirring incidents and excitement reaches fever heat when the convict escapes from piison by leaping on to a passing train which conveys him to a place of safety. The lot of the convict immured in IBlackmoor prison " is well portrayed and the sympathies of the audience are all with the man who has bean wrongfully imprisoned. Mr. Frank Bertram as Weary Willie and Convict 80—a part he has played over 2,000 times—is the dominating char acter and presents a really clever4tudy. While imper- sonating the broken-down but philosophic tramp, his dry humour creates continual merriment, but he is able to rise to fine dramatic heights when occasion demands. Miss Lillian Dean as Edith and Mr. Walter Graham as Harold Trevalyan are an admirable pair of lovers and Mr. Escott Robson achieves a notable success in the unpopular role of Jasper Thorne. The play is excellently mounted.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAIIIIE. THE MUSICAL SOCIETY'S OPERA.</p>
        <p>A triumphant success is confidently anticipated for the performances of "Merrie England" which will be given at the Royal Court Theatre next week by members of the Warrington Musical Society. The scale on which the opera will he presented for the first time in War- rington will be in every respect adequate and complete ' special scenery arnd new costumes having been requisitioned for the occasion. It is interesting t 3 note that the people in the cast, chorus and orchestra will make up 100 performers. Mr. Frank 11. Crossley will occupy his accustomed post as musical director and conductor, and Mr. Foden Williams is again this year the opera company's coach and stage manager. The rehearsals have been entered into with the warmest enthusiasm, and there is every indication that the performances will add to the laurels of the opera company, and afford the highest degree of pleasure to the large audiences which are expected to assemble in the Court Theatre next week. Reserved seats can be Inoked at the theatre, where the plan is on view.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>DAMAGES FOR TRESPASS BY CATTLE</head>
        <p>DAMAGES FOR TRESPASS BY CATTLE</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before ris Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., E. J. Smith, Victoria House,Wiiderspool,sued James Harrison,butcher Stockton Heath for damages for tresspass by cattle belonging to the defendant on a field of mowing grass in the occupation of the plaintiff at Wilderspool.—Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for the plaintiff and Mr, A. Browne represented the defendant. Mr. Steel stated that on the death of the late Mr. R- SV. Francomb, the executors entered into an agreement with Mr. Smith for the tenancy of Victoria House from June 2nd. On June 20th arrangements were made for him to become the tenant of a small field adjoining the house. Defend Int had beers in correspondence with Mr J. Stanley Francomb, solicitor, Manchester, executor of the late Mr. Francomb's will, with a view to beeming the tenant of the field but no definite arrangement had been made. Whilst the negotiations were pending and after the field had-been let to Mr. Smith, the defendant turned his cattle into the field with the result that considerable damage was done to the crop, and the plaintjff now claimed £6. Evidence was gVen by Mr. Francomb that the tenancy commenced on the 20th June and considerable correspondence which had passed between defendant and himself was submitted.</p>
        <p>11fr. Browne contended that on the correspondence, a tenancy had been created between the executors and the defendant, and that consequently he had a perfect right to pasture his cattle in the field. His Honour held that there was no agreement for tenancy between the executors and the defendant and that the only question was one as to the extent of the damage. Evidence was given by the plaintiff and his daughter as to the number of cows and horses turned into the field and Mr. Lightfoot, farmer, Antrobus. who had assessed the damage on behalf of the plaintiff, said that in his opinion it amounted to £6. Defendant stated that the grass was such bhat he would never have cut it for a hay crop, and if he had done so he would not have expected his cattle to eat it. Henry Broady, farmer, Grappenhall, stated that in his opinion }Os. would cover the damage done, and his view was corroborated by John Yarwood, farmer, Appleton. Cross-examined by Mr. Steel, they admitted they had not examined the field within the last few days, and that the grass, after the heavy rains, would look con- siderably worse than it did immediately after the damage was done.</p>
        <p>Eventually his Honour gave judgment for the plaintiff for £1 and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME,</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME,</p>
        <p>The great attraction at this popular house of entertainment is Miss Ada Colley, prima donna. Miss Colley has had a splendid reception at each perform- ance and well deserves the enthusiastic encores she has received. Her voice is a soprano of great purity and remarkable range, and she internrets songs like " Nightingale " and " Killarney" with much taste and expression. A pair of comedians with ideas out of the ordinary are Moon and Morris, who not only look funny, talk funnily, but dance funnily. In fact, their dancing is indescribable humour allied to the poetry of motion. The Ellirnan's (Van and Alice) have nothing to do with embrocation so far as the programme indicates, but they appear very successfully as refined musical entertainers. Van plays the cello and the violin with ability, and Alice sings and plays the piano. Both combine in a concertina pof. pourri of popular airs which delights the audience immensely. Leo and Roland display feats of strength and balanc- ing which make one wonder. They are a very clever pair and deserve all the praise generously vouchsafed by the discriminating public. George Acre is the usual light comedian in a " swell " suit. The Three Prestons present "The Liftman's Rise," described as a laughable absurdity. It appeared to us more absurd than laughable. Originality was what the Prestons seemed to lack. D. W. Watson, a gentle- man who is handicapped by the loss of an arm, givea solos on the cornet and coach horn in a style which proves him to be no mean executant. There is plenty of attractive music provided by Mr. Widdop's orchestra, and the biograph never fails to amuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>A novel attraction is announced for next week in the shape of a beauty competition in which the following prizes are offered: —lst prize, £5; 2nd prize, £2 10a.; 3rd prize, £.l ; and numerous consolation prizes, the total value of the awards being £25. There will also be a competition for the best head of hair, and a first prize of 10s, is offered. Each competitor will bear a a number and the members of the audience will be supplied with voting papers. Ladies obtaining the greatest number of votes at each performance (Monday to Thursday inclusive) will enter the final cortest on Friday. Entries will be received and full particulars given by the manager (Mr. G. D. Bradbury). The artistes engaged for the week are: —Bros. t. Clair, comedy cyclists, the world's champion bicycle polo and football players; The Five Broadway Girls, refined singers and dancers; Gladys and Albert, comedy tumblers, ; The Smythsons, with their laughing hit, "Love Germs " ; Pete and Juno, comedians .and dancers; Annie Heath, comedienne and dancer; Steve Bartle, concertina soloist and vocalist.</p>
        <p>Children's Party at the Town Hall, The Town Hall was the scene of a very happy , gathering last evening when the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst) gave a children's party. The guests were the boys and girls who received medals on Empire Day because, by the votes of ' their fellow scholars, they had been acclaimed the most popular children in the elementary schools, I and also children, between the ages of seven and seventeen, of members of the Town Council. , Altogether the attendance numbered about one hundred and all spent a capital time—in fact the evening will rank as a red letter occasion in the lives lof the children. The exterior of the Town Hall was brightly decorated with flags and bunting, while the interior was bright with vane-coloured electric lights ' and well-arranged flowers and plants. Having been heartily welcomed by the Mayor and Mayoress, the guests engaged in games and dancing, the music being supplied by Mr. A. Starkey. Light refreshments were provided and everything possible was done for ' the comfort and enjoyment of the children who,before departing, warmly expressed their appreciation of the hospitality of the chief magistrate and his wife.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RETURNED EMPTY!</head>
        <p>RETURNED EMPTY!</p>
        <p>HOW A CANADIAN EMICRANT CAME HOME.</p>
        <p>[By SIDNEY G. LEIGH.]</p>
        <p>At such a time as the present, when the country is flooded with illustrated handbooks setting forth the innumerable advantages of emigrating to Canada, it may not be uninteresting for a Lancashire man to give a brief account of his visit to, and return from that much advertised colony. I left England in the summer of 1907 with a few pounds in my pocket and an ample supply of hope. I arrived at Montreal in the R.M.S. "Tunisian," and having found lodgings began light-heartedly to look for work. I soon realized that it was useless to continue the search in the city, so I paid five dollars to an employment agent for five introductions to farmers who were willing to employ inexperienced hands. The first four were confederates of the agent and regretted that I was too short, not physically fit, etc. The fifth one engaged me at four dollars per month and " all found.' My railway fare to the farm cost me twelve dollars, but as I thought I should have that "healthy, open-air life, in a beautiful climate" of which the emigration agents are so fond of talking, I did not in the least grudge it. Alas ! the hours were from daylight to dark, the food was pickled pork and black bread, and my lodging was in a loft over one of the cattle sheds. Shorthorns and greenhorns slept together. At the end of the first month I left that farm without beat of ' drum, and for evermore I shall loathe the sight, the smell,the very name of pickled pork. In Hontreal,where Ithere are over 25,000 out of employment, I got various small and unremunerative jobs. I played small partsl with a company of the Vincent Crummles type, I lwashed dishes in a restaurant, and at last was cotn-1 pelled by the pangs of hunger to take a job as brass- cleaner in a low saloon. By this time Thai determined to return to England. As I could not afford to book a berth, I arranged to work a passage as cattleman on one of the many ships which bring over to this country the meat fondly imagined to be "best English." It was haled in England, no doubt. A 3 I went on board the vessel I could not help contrasting the manner of my return with the manner of my going out. Now there was no comfortable state-room, no trunks filled with what Pip's friend would have described as " portable property." A brute in human form —the foreman of one of the cattlemen gangs —showed me my bunk in the dirty " fore-peak." Thirty-two of us ,olept in that black hole for thirteen nights. I say , we slept," but tla rat would selllm lc us. Many of the plossaga workers were polish Jews and other foreigners. Some of them prayed, some of them fought, but all of them talked in a manner which would have put ilabel to shame. Each of us had 25 bullocks to attend to —no easy task for an amateur. Three times a day the pitiable boasts, sweating in the " 'tween decks ' were watered, and each one of them would drink two or three buckets. That meant the drawing and carrying by each man of 225 buckets of water from the tank, up the narrow alley way. Three times a day they were fad and this was by far the hardest task. The bales of hay had to be lowered from the main dock and there opened and shaken out. While the shaking was going on, the hungry animals on either side of the alley way lunged forward, straining at their tethers in their wild desire for food. Accidents were frequent. One man lost a thumb, another was gored and two were severely crushed against the bulk-head by a bullock which had managed to get loose. The average hours of work were from 4 a.m. until 9 or 10 p.m. I was never an epicure, but the food was a disgrace to 3ny ship flying the British flag. Stow and hard tack or hard tack and evil smelline, pickled pork 1 was the ever-recurring menu. But of course any- thing is good enough for a cattleman. Had the ship gone down there would have been reported the deaths of nine officers, 810 head of cattle and "a number of cattlemen." And yet, in one respect, I was lucky. I here managed to get home again. Thousands of those who swallow the glib terminological inexacti- tudes of emigration agents never return. 1 Mr. Punch once gave some excellent advice to those about to marry. I repeat it for the benefit of those , about to emigrate : " Don't " !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>MILITARY TRAINING FOR THE UNEMPLOYED.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRAINING FOR THE UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of Warrington has received the following communication from the War afice as to the special reserve force about to be organized : War Office, London, S.W. ,sth October, 1903. Sir,—lt may be that owing to trade depression the difficulty of providing work will become somewhat acute daring the coming winter, and employers of labour while anxious to retain their men in employment will fled considerable difficulty in employing them during the winter months, owing to slackness of trade. The question is whether the newly-organized Special Reserve can be made use of to provide temporary employment during the winter months. The 51ilitia in the past did not adequately afford the assistance required, as recruits were only drilled on enlistment for 49 days. The substitution of the new arrangements of the Speoial Reserve may, however, meet the di ificalty. The Special Reserve takes recruits for the Royal Field Artillery, the Royal Engineers and the Infantry of the Line, and in Ireland for two units of Garrison Artillery. Recruits for these arms are enlisted to train on enlist- ment for 6 months, unless the Musketry is to be performed immediately prior Mb the Annual Training, in which caw the actual training on enlistment is reduced tO5 months.</p>
        <p>The age for enlistment into tho Special Reserve is from 17 to 30 years or age. The pay varies, according to the arm of the service, and the age of the recruit, from 7s. a week in the case of the Infantry,to Bs. sd. in the Royal Artillery if the recruit is under 18 years ; and if the recruit is 19 years of age on enlistment the pay is from 9s. 9d. a week in o Infantry to 10s. 24d. in the Royal Artillery. On completing the period of di 11 on enlistment the recruit receives a bounty of 30s. Afterwards, as stated below, he receiveslin addition to his pay, four bounties in each year of 41 each. I will be seen, therefore, that employers of labour can, to a certain degree remedy the want of employ- ment by advising the younger of their employes to spend the winter in the spacial reserve, and by this means obtain the weekly pay mentioned above in addition to free food, quarters, bedding, fuel and light, while the men, as a rule, will not be so far distant from their homes that it will be impossible for them Ito return on Saturday afternoon till Sunday evening on leave.</p>
        <p>Tho question will be asked for how long the recruit who takes advaninge of these terms will be com- mitted. The answer is for 6 years, 6 months in the first veer and from 15 to N. days in each subsequent year, but a recruit in ordinary circumstances is allowed to purchase his discharge at any time for £3. If he remains in the service he receives three non- training bounties of RI each, payable in October, December and February, and an annual training bounty of £l.</p>
        <p>--Employers of labour may be afraid that their men may be taken away from them at a busy time, and be called out for annual training. This annual training, however, in the case of the infantry,lasts only for 21 days, while in the case of other corps it is only 15 days, and, as the dates of training are fixed locally by the General Officer Commanding the District, the requirements and conditions of each part of the country are studied in connection with the Wining of its special reserve units.</p>
        <p>Any employer of labour who desires to know more of the conditions of service in the Special Reserve on behalf of any of his employ4s, and also the local units in which vacancies exist, can get full information by applying to the nearest recruiting officer or other local military authority.—l am, Sir, Your obedient Servnnt, E. W. D. WARD. The Worshipful The Mayor of Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>MAYORALTY.</head>
        <p>THE MAYORALTY.</p>
        <p>Seven Gentlemen Approached.</p>
        <p>Alderman Smethurst has declined to accept the Mayoralty of the borough for a third year. His decision was made known at a meeting of the General Purposes Committee held at the Town Hall, prior to the Council meeting, on Tuesday evening, and was received with general regret. The Council then appointed a deputation (consisting of Aldermen Bolton and Evans, Councillors Toole, Singleton, Lord Wilkinson and Rogers) to wait upon the following, gentlemen, in the order of seniority, with a view to making the selection from them : —Alderman Pier- point, Alderman Bennett, Alderman Burton, Alderman Forshaw, Councillor Dr. Joseph, Councillor Fulton- Smith and Councillor W. B. Roberts.</p>
        <p>We understand that all the gentlemen named have been waited upon, with the result that three of them have expressed their willingness to take office if elected, one is considering the matter, and three have declined.</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The only new development to record this week is the selection by the Conservatives of Mr. P. J. Lashford to oppose Councillor James Ball (Socialist) in St. Johrt'e Ward. Mr. Lashford was invited to stand at a meeting. of the Ward Committee held on Thursday evening and consented to do so. Considerable energy is being thrown into the contest in Orford Ward where the candidates are Mr. Peter Peacock (L.), Mr. J. Hampson (S.), and Mr. J. A. Rushworth (C.) Mr. Peacock has a strong workers' committee who are using every endeavour to secure his return in the progressive interest. The following is a list of prospective candi- dates :</p>
        <p>Town Hall Ward Coon. W. Benson (N.T.) Mr. T. Dirties (L). Whitecrass Ward Coon. Jas. Evans (L.) Bewsey Ward Coon. Jas. Dickenson (N.T.) . . . _ _ _. . ,_ .</p>
        <p>Howley Ward Conn. T. Chantler (C.) Latchford Ward Conn. J. Pemberton (C.N.T.) St. John's Ward Coun. Jas. Ball (S.) Mr. F. J. Lashford (C.) Orford Ward Mr. P. Peacock (L.) Mr. J. Hampson (S.) Mr. J. A. Rushworth (C.) Fairfield Ward Conn. J. G. Craik (I.) St. Austin's Ward Conn. Dr. Joseph (C.) NOMINATION DAY.</p>
        <p>Nominations must be handed in at the Town Hall not later than Saturday, October 24th, at 5 p.m. The Mayor will adjudicate on the papers between 2 and 4 p.m. on Mondiy, October 26th, and the elections will take place on Monday, November 2nd.</p>
        <p>LATCEIEORD WARD. At a meeting of the Conservative Ward Comraittee held at the Conservative Ciu' or Afondav evening, Councillor Roberts presiding, Councillor i'emberton was adopted as the Conservative candidate anl he agreed to stand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WHITECROSS WARD.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal Committee of Whitecross Ward was held in Thewlis-street sehuols on Monday evening. Councillor Hawthorn presided over a good attendance, which included Councillor James Evans and Mr. J. Howes, junr.—A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Councillor Evans for his ten years' valuable services as a representative of the Ward, and he was unanimously invited to stand again. Councillor Evans consented to do so, and delivered an interesting speech on various municipal questions.</p>
        <p>St. Johns Liberal Ward committee decided last night not to contest the vacancy. In Orford Ward, Mr. P. Peacock held a crowded and enthusiastic meeting last night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE. Distribution of Shooting Prizes.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE. Distribution of Shooting Prizes.</p>
        <p>the dittribiitic 91 the shooting prizes in connection with the Warrington Branch of the National Service League took place on Thursday evening, at the Lien Hotel. Prior to the prizes being given supper was served and was very much enjoyed, Mr. Lee's catering giving complete satisfaction. Captain Crosfield pre- sided and was supported by Major larshall, Captain Layton, Messrs. Ridgway, 3. 'Newell, and Ball. After supper Captain CROSFIELD proposed the health of the King. The toast was drunk with nrovical honours. Captain CROSFIELD in introducing Major Manual! remarked that he had come from Chester to distribute the prizes. He had been doing his best to arouse the patriotism of the people of Chester. Major Mutsu:am. raid tint Captain Crosfleld had kindly asked him to be there that night and he felt it a great honour to present the prizes to the members of the League. CaptAn Crosfleld also asked him to say a few words on the military establishment. Ile would not dwell very largely upon it ; because time did not. permit. Under certain circumstances the invasion of England was perfectly possible. The Navy, of course, were the defenders of tho coasts. 5.t admirals had joined the National Service League,—an institution which trained people to repel invasion from the country they loved so well. The existence for many years of the Volunteers had led many people to believe invasion was possible, or else there would be no use for a Territorial Army or the Volunteers. Ile proposed to, say to them, what he thought would happen, if they were invaded by a first-class European power, with their defensive forces as they weie at the pre:-.ent time and in order to show them what he thought would , happen ho was going to tell them of a certain portion of the war between Prance and Germany which happened in 1870. There were only two powers possibly who could invade England. America was out Of the question. She had not got a very large army and they might have time to organise their defence before anything serious happened. There were two powers which were ready—France and Germany, lle was in no sense an alarmist, but he -was quite certain that if Germany's people, Germany's government thought it was for their good fortune they would come forward without the least hesitation. The speaker described a portion of the war referred to, and said that it was not always numbers that counted, but trained men. He 'Advised those prezent to take note of what the National Service League said," Do not put off the training of your men until it is too late." (Applause). Major Marshall then distributed the prizes to the successful competitors who were as follows : Competition No. 1 (Civilian).-1, R. Pemberton ; 2, R. R. Bunting; 3, A. Longshaw; 4, R. Burthem ; 5, W. Clayton.</p>
        <p>Competition No. 2 (Military).-1, T. Buttrey ; 2, F. M. Cowley ; 3, W. Aikin ; 4, Qnartermaster-Sett. Wilcock ; 5, Colour-Sergeant Howard. Competition I. 3 (Open).-1, Sergeaist-Drnminer Plinston 2, R. Hoods; 3, F. C. Bennion ; 4, H. Whit- field;l 5, L Thompson ; G. O. P. Daly. Mr. OWEN proposed the health of their president, Field Marshal Earl Roberts. He wished that he could do him adequate justice for the service he had rendered to the State. He was expecting to see Earl Roberts down in Warrington at some future date. The toast was drunk heartily. Captain GEORGE CRO'sFIET,D proposed the health of the President of the Warrington Branch, Mr. Lyon. It was quite true that at one time Lord Roberts practically stated that he was coming to Warrington, but numer- ous engagements made it impossible for him to do so. It was hardly fair for them to ask him to make a long journey to a place like Warrington. He (Earl Roberts) I might be induced to speak at a big place like Manchester or Liverpool and then he might pay them a visit. Earl Roberts had not forgotten the work done in Warring- ton and when he (Mr. Crosfleld) had the good fortune of seeing him, he always asked how the Branch was getting on. What were they as rt League doing? They came year after year for the prizes. Were they getting any " forrarder " and how were they going to attain what they wanted ? At the present time the position was a dii!lcult one. They had Mr. Haldane's scheme buttered up by " tuft hunting " politicians, generals, and the Press. It was thought we were able by training for the space of 14 days, for three years consecutively, to turn an army into the field to defend the nation. lie did not believe himself, and he was sure there were some in the room who did not think they had too much time. They never knew what would happen. It might be over 10 years or so, and it might be to-morrow. They wanted the country to feel prepared. The admirals did not think that the navy was sufficient to guard the shores. What was happening? At the present time this magnificent Empire of ours was bringing its men of war from the far-off seas and putting them is the German ocean. He advised the young members oi the League to go into the ten aerial army, and those who were in it to stick in it. Let that not prevent therno however, from speak- ing their minds. Let them speak out and tell people that the present state of affairs was absurd and dangerous. Let them work towards making a sound citizen army, which was absolutely simple of achieve- ment, and which Would gain the respect of all the other nations of Europe. That was what they spent their time in the National Service Leage for. It was to inculcate this spirit into their neighbours. The time was coming when members of Parliament would not be afraid to talk about compulsory training. They would like to see a National Service League Bill wrssed. They had one introduced by Captain Kincaid Smith but that was an idiotic bill for it wanted to keep a man till he was nearly 70 years of age. The sooner they had a good bill the better. They were much obliged to the honorary members for the help rendered; nearly all the prizes had been given by them. Let them not forget the president of the Branch, Mr. Lyon. He was getting on in years, therefore he could net take such an active part as he would like, but his sympathies were with the League and he was always most ready to help them. The toast was drunk with musical honours. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to Captain Crosfleld far presiding. Croqield's Band, the Apollo Quartette, Mr. Whittle and Mr. Pemberton entertained the company with musical items which proved highly interesting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LICENSING BILL. Forthcoming Parr Hall Meeting.</head>
        <p>THE LICENSING BILL. Forthcoming Parr Hall Meeting.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Citizens Temperance League is going to spare no effort during the ensuing Parliamen- tary Session to strengthen the hands of the Government on the question of the Licensing Bill. A second Parr Hall meeting is announced to be held on Monday October 19th under the presidency of another distin- guished Churchman in the person of Canon Barker. Dean of Carlisle. A most attractive array of com- petent speakers has been secured, including Principal Warman of Liverpool, Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., Rev. J. , Palmer of London, and Rev. F. A. Screeton of Hyde, I formerly vicar of Christ Church, Latchford. Much of the opposition to the Licensing Bill from disinterested parties has given way to argument, and it is hoped that a larger representation of local clergy will this time be seen on the temperance platform. The meeting will commence at seven o'clock, and the doors of Parr Hall will open at 6 p.m,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
