        <p>STREET PAVING. WOR:: FOR UNEMPLOYED SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>Councillor CITANTLER asked the Chairman of the Paving and Sewerage Committee :- 1. Ward is the total length of unpaved streets and passages in Warrington? 2. What is the length of streets and passages paved since the report of unpaved streets and passages was presented to the Council, by his com:nittee, on February 6th, 1906 ? 3. If he will urge upon his committee the desirability of hastening on the paving of those streets and passages that require attention, which would find employment for paviors and labourers during the coming winter, besides carrying out a necessary work recommended by the Medical Officer of Health?</p>
        <p>Alderman liNlitos gave the following replies :- 1. Thirteen and a half miles. That included every street and passage in the town,even those only partially built upon. 2. Three and three-quarter miles. The length of streets and passages passed by the committee, but which were awaiting the sanction of the Local Govern- ment Board, was 51 miles. They would see by the agenda paper that they were going to apply for powers to give notice of the levelling and paving of many other passages, so that there were very few passages and streets iu the town in regard to which the committee had not taken some steps. 3. In his opinion, it was necessary that he should impress upon the committee the desirability of hasten- ing the paving. They were doing all they possibly could, and had the matter so thoroughly under con- sideration that they were losing no opportunity of pushing forward the work. The weather in the winter months made it impossible to do the work in a proper und efficient manner ; it was neither creditable to the contractor nor satisfactory to the committee and the property owners who had to pay for it. However, they would continue to work until Christmas if the weather permitted. SHIP CANAL LITIGATION.</p>
        <p>In proposing the adoption of the Rivers Committee minutes, the DEPUTY-MAYOR said they had got a statement from the traders as to the cost of the action and the money the Corporation had back. It RIM the best the Corporation could do, and the Town4jlerk informed him thtt they could not do better than accept it. Alderman FORSHAW seconded, and the minutes were passed. MOTOR WAGGONS AND WARRINGTON WATER.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Water Committee a letter of explanation was read from Messrs. tiny and Cowdell with reference to a motor waggon with reference to a motor waggon having been supplied with water from their tap. Alderman EVANS said Messrs. Kay and Cowdell innocently broke the regulations, but th..t committee would like it to go forth th tt if motor drivers were discovered taking the town's water they would be pro- ceeded against. Many of them carried stand pipes and, without ceremony, fixed them to the hydrants, and after getting their boilers filled went on their way rejoicing. The DEPI:TY-MATOI2 seconded and the minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>deal with. Councillor BROADHURST : What is it brought here at all for, then? 'the TOWN CLERk : Simply for your information and as a record. The minutes were then agreed to. SANKEY CANAL BRIDGE. SUGGESTED WIDENING. At a meeting of the Street Improvement Committee, the Town Clerk laid before the Committee a letter he had received from the County Bridgemaster with refer- ence to the suggested widening of Sankey Canal bridge, stating that the Town Council's refusal to give financial assistance left no alternative but to abandon the improv- ment. It W 95 resolved that the matter be referred to the General Purposes Committee, aril at the meeting of that committee a sub-committee was elected with power to wait on the County Council. Alderman BENNETT moved the conßrmation of the minutes, and said the action of the County Council on the s.ubject of Sankey Canal Bridge was exceedingly illogical, because when the Corporation approached them about Warrington Bridge they at once fell back upon the argument that the bridge was within the county borough ard if they wanted to widen they must do so at their own expense. He contended that the Corporation ought to make a stand in this matter, and as the bridge was entirely within the county the County Council should pay for the widening. (Hear, hear). LAND FOR SALE. The Street Improvement Committee resolved that the following plots of land be offered for sale by public auction :—Plot of land on the west side of Bridge- street, between Messrs. Lee's and Shaw's premises, 262 square yards ; plot of land on the west side of Bridge- street, between the Feather's Hotel and Messrs. Weavers' premises, 2SI square yards ; plot of land on the north side of Knutsford-road, with the buildings thereon lately known as the Mersey Bedstead Works, 2,034 squire yards ; plot of land on the north side of Knutsford-road, part of the former site of Edelsten's Pin Mill, 1,475 square yards : plot of land on the north side of Liverpool-road, formerly the site of the Sankey Chemical Works, 5,11,W sauare yards.</p>
        <p>Lcdge was rroposecl by P.G. J. Tatham and responded to by P.P.G.M. J. Massey. A vote of thanks was accorded to the artistes. " An Eventful Century." The opening lecture of the winter session was delivered at the Old Academy on Monday evening by Mr. Oswald Lofthouse, whose subject was "Au Eventful Century." He save a most interesting survey of the outstanding events of the fifteenth century, which he stated was an age of exploration; of geographical discovery, by which men's knowledge of the surface of the globe was wonderfully increased. He referred to the voyages of discovery undertaken by Dom Henrique, son of the King of Portugal, Bartolemeo Dray, Vasco da 'lima, and Columbus. The fifteenth century was notable for the development of nationalism. Divisions of various small peoples .were welded into one kingdom. Hitherto there had been competition and strife between small powers and communities. Henceforth the competition was to be between nations on a more gigantic scale It was in the fifteenth century that all the causes were at work whose culminating effects were the Reformation in the next century. There was a desire for light and learning. For centuries the minds of men had been fettered by monkish traditions and the narrowness of Medievalism. The new learning taught them not only the glorious possibilities of life, but it gave freedom to the reason-, and henceforth the decree of the Pope, the dogma of the Church, was not accepted with The implicit confidence and submission which had pre- viously obtained. The most notable event in that ! memorable century was the art of printing. The discussion which followed the reading of the paper was taken part in by the Chairman (Mr. L. Greening), Mr. E. J. Hall, Mr. C. Madeley, Mr. T. E. Gibson,Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, Mr. W. 11. Sherburn and the Rev. H. A. Lester ; Mr. Lofthouse was heartily thanked.</p>
        <p>vocal duet., " My song shall always be Thy mercy,''' Mendelsgoltn. The illustrations of " bad music" included a " battle march " and other short items. Mr. Woods will give the same lecture at two other centres in Liverpool before Christmas. Plasterer v. Builder. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., John Clare, master plasterer, 9, Whalley-street, sued Charles Chappell,builder, Stockton Heath, for £lO 10s. Idamages for breach of contract. There was a counter- claim by defendant for £4 12s. for damage alleged to have been sustained owing to plaintiff having refused and neglected to carry out his contract. —Mr. Browne appealed for the plaintiff and Mr. Steel defended.— Plaintiff's case was that defendant accepted his tender Ifor plastering and lathing four houses in Willis-street at £7 per house. He worked on the job for a week and received 30s. on account. He then did some moulding at home and when he went to the houses again the defendant refused to allow him to resume work unless he took on two men who were there. Plaintiff refused to do that and defendant would not allow him to continue his work. As to the counter-claim, plaintiff had never refused or neglected to carry out the contract; on the contrary he was quite prepared to do the work.—Plaintiff denied that at the beginning of the second week he went on I the job in a drunken condition.—For the defence, it l was alleged that both Clare and his employees came to woik in a drunken state and were quite incapable ! of performing the contract.Chippell told plaintiff that if be liked to come again in a proper condition he could I resume the work, and the onus of breaking the con- tract therefore rested with the plaintiff. Numerous 'witnesses having been called, his Honour gave judge- ment for the plaintiff for £0 on the claim and also for the plaintiff on the counter claim.</p>
