        <p>ELECTRICITY DISCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>The Electricity and Tramways Committee met on September 80th, when a scale of discounts for con- sumers of electric light was adopted, ranging from Is. 2d. in the 2 below 250 units per quarter to 2s. Id. between 10,900 and 12,149 units. The scale of discounts for consumers of power ranged from Is. in the 2 for units between 205,000 and 3,749 to Is. 6d. for units between 205,000 and 234,939. These discounts are only allowed if the account is paid within a month. Councillor WILKINSON, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said that in the past the charge for lighting was 4Lid., but it would now be 4d. with discount, which would bring the cost down to nd. They proposed to make a similar reduction for power. It would mean a loss of profits of £5OO on lighting and £6OO on power, but they hoped this would he the means of encouraging more people to use electricity both for lighting and power. Councillor NIF.Ln seconded. The DEPUT Y-MAYOR : It it a step in the right direc- tion. The minutes were adopted. SMALL HOLDINGS AND ALLOTMENTS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee several applica- tions for allotment in Ilewsey and Lovely-lane districts were read. The Town Clerk submitted replies received from land agents as to lands available for allotments in this district and the committee decided to visit the land. It uas resolved that the tender of Mr. George Waywell for the supply and erection of eight tool sheds at £3O be accepted subject to satisfactory inquiries by the Borough Surveyor.—At a meeting on the 18th of September, the committee inspected land in Winwick- road and the Orford district, and also land behind the Cemetery, which had been offered to the Corporation for allotments.-rAt another meeting the committee considered the offers of laud in Longshaw-street, N%inwick-road, and behind the Cemetery and decided to take no further action in view of the prohibitive prices asked, and it was resolved that the Town Clerk obtain particulars of the land at Orford offered for allotments; also that the Town Hall, Parks, and Baths Committee be requested to let this committee the land behind the Old \Carps and adjoining the River Mersey for the purposes of additional allotments. An application for an allot- ment in Victoria Park was received and the Town Clerk was instructed to inform the applicant that this committee had no other land to let at present. These minutes were agreed to on the proposition of Alderman FORSHiW, seconded by Alderman ELLISON. WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Councillor CRANTLER asked the Chairman of the General Purposes Committee: What progress is being made with the widening or re-building of Warrington Bridge? In the absence of the Mayor the DEPUTY-MAYOR advised Councillor Chantler to wait until the General Purposes Committee's meeting. "I don't know any- hing, prictically," he added.</p>
      </div>
