      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>CALF AS THANKOFFERING.</head>
        <p>CALF AS THANKOFFERING.</p>
        <p>A calf was found to be one of the gifts in connection with the harvest thanksgiving at Pickmere Wesleyan Chapel. The animal was nut present along with the other products of nature, but someone had painted it on a card, and underneath the picture was written "Calf to be sold presented by Mr. A. Platt, VVincham." The calf was given along with fruit, flowers, and vegetables.</p>
        <p>• -</p>
        <p>MY D.</p>
        <p>I must ask You to kindly wait a week or two longer for the result of the " Kindness to Animals" Competition. I am sorry to dis- appoint you, but the essays are coming in so well, and are so good, that I am unwilling to close for a little time. To compensate you, I shall add a few extra prizes of boxes of Fry's Chocolates. I have been most pleased with the essays, as it shows that my members are very keen on being kind and considerate to ani- mals, and also to domestic pets. Always try and stop cruelty whenever possible, and try, all of you, to keep some pet animal to•tend and care for.</p>
        <p>Welcome to Hilda Mary Haigh and Violet Sandy. I shall be pleased to send a dainty little pocket mirror to any of my members on receiving application for same. With love, UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,--Having been a reader of the " C.C." for over two years, I have at last decided to compete in the "Kindness to Animals " Competition. The recent .splendid prizes that have been awarded to successful exhibitors in different competitions have roused my enthusiasm. Hoping my essay will meet with your approval.</p>
        <p>KINDNESS TO ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>At the present time, when everything is being revolutionised, as it were, our poor dumb friends, and the treatment they are often sub- jected to, ought not to be looked upon as a trivial consideration. The reverse of kindness is often to be seen when a heavily-laden vehicle is proceeding up a steep incline. The main object of the driver is to reach the top, despite the disgraceful treatment necessitated in so doing. The strenuous efforts of the poor horse are unworthy of consideration in the mind of the driver, but what a change comes over him when the horse fails to proceed owing to es- h aust ion. Then the driver rues the results of his imprudent conduct. The horse, however, is not the only sufferer at the hands of pease- cutors. The dog, whose invaluable services are felt by all householders, is often unjustly pun- ished, and consequently an indifference springs up between master and dog, whereas, if justice and kindness were substituted for injustice and cruelty, love of an inseparable nature would be the ultimate result. R. OSBORNE.</p>
        <p>We all know that nature made us as well ae the brute. Animals can feel just the same pain as people can. Some people are very unkind to their pets. When a horse is at work, he ought to be able to lower his head, so that he can see the road, and step over all impediments, and can quickly recover himself. The bit in its mouth does not hurt him or make him feel un- comfortable, if the rein is loose. It is also very cruel to harness horses when they are lame. Horses are good, willing creatures, if they are well used and not overburdened with work. If horses could speak, they would, perhaps, say, "Treat me better, good master; do not grudge me wholesome food and rest, nor give me an empty nosebag, and I will serve you well to the end of my life." The stable ought to be clean, dry, well ventilated, and free from danger of fire. People never ought to rob little birds of their nests, and put them in cages. Pirds ought to have free lives of their own, and not to be caught and die of hunger. Dogs are good, faithful creatures. They look up to master as their god, and like to be noticed by a kind man, who will attend to their wants. Do not lose your dog, nor send it without a home. A daily brushing is better than fre- quent washing for dogs. Never have their ears cut, as it is a barbarous custom, as the sand and dirt will get in, which distresses the poor animal. It is cruel fo tie a dog under a cart, especially in warm weather. Dogs and cats are far too numerous, and they are often neg- Jaded and starved, which is dreadful. HILDA MARY HAIGH.</p>
        <p>In the first place, kindness is one of the best things which all of us ought to try to culti- vate. Where there is kindness shown to any animal, we may be sure there is love. It is always well to remember that animals have the sense of feeling, just the same as we have. They like to be petted and loved, and in various ways they show their love in return. I will illustrate this by a short story :—A farmer in the country had a dog who always went out with him to look after the sheep, and trotted after the cart when the farmer went to market, and was always ready at the stable door when it was time to start. One morning his master was ill, and did not come. The dog went to the house and began to bark and whine, and waited all day watching the door, but still his master did not come. The farmer died, and on the day of the funeral the dog had gone away. The mourners came back from the churchyard, and no dog was to be seen. After a time, some one went out to place some flowers on to the grave, and there, lying with his paws stretched across it; was the poor doggie, looking so weak and thin: He would not be driven away, and when some tempting food was brought to en- tice him, he would not touch it, but lay there like a stone image. The next day and the next, he was still there; but when those who came to look at him tried to rouse him, they saw that Ice was dead on the grave of him to whose voice he had listened for so long, and , whom he had loved so well.</p>
        <p>WILLIAM R. FAERAR.</p>
        <p>.--+-</p>
        <p>Between intervals of sobbing, the polished dentist remarked, "My girl, just evacuate into this receptacle the fluid contents of your den- tal cavity." " Mammal " wailed the child, "what does he want me to do?" _</p>
        <p>And with an amused smile, the woman re- plied, " Spit."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Hiram: " And have you any refer. ences " Applicant: " No, mum. Oi toted 'em up! " Mrs. Hiram (in surprise): "Tore them up? How foolish 1 " Applicant: " Yez wudn't think so, mum, if yea had seen 'em!"</p>
