      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <p>, sends my father your paper every week. and I have taken great interest in the " Children's Cor- ner " for a long time now. At last; I am writing to ask you if you will 'receive me as one of' your members. I saw in the " Chil- dren's Corner " this week that a sifter watch is going to be given for the best essay on " Kindness to Animals," and I theught I woul&amp;1 like to have a try for it, as I want a werth very much. Hoping this will find pill in good . _ __ •</p>
        <p>_ . healtro. _ VIOLET SANDY. KINDNESS TO ANL:VI:Air,. Of all the things in human nature, cruelty to animals takes a must .prominent part, and in civilised countries it is cnly checked by punishment, which it rightly deserves. •Ani- mals are sent for the use ot mankind hi very many different ways, more, however, in help- ing and assisting to piovide human livillood. As an instance, a horse is the most industrious animal, and instances are known of where they are treated in a most shameful manner, through being either underfed Cr allowed to be worked until they die of fatigue. These are numerous other beasts of burden which end their lives in the same manner. throarh neg• lect. Of another cruelty, which is also prac- tised in a very large way, is that cf taking the lives of our beautiful feathered creatures by the sportsmen who destroy their lives in tlfeir field, for no other purpose than of a craving for sport. Animals which provide food for mankind should be killed in the most humane way, and, by doing this, a large proportion of them would be saved from that su-called sport, which causes them pain and suffering. The Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals is; perhaps, one of the greatest blessings ever known for the welfare of animals in England, and it is most piteous to see some instances of ill-treatment which they often have to take from our streets, more especially for that of the horse. If there was , not such a society, the sympathy of the 1 majority of the people who deal with animals 1 for various purposes would greatly decrease, and, therefore, they teach great lessons, which remind these people of the fair and proper .. treatment, that they should 'exercise towards their dumb friends. If everyone knew of the great amount of good that the Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals has (lone, it would encourage a great deal more kindliness to our world's precious gifts. In short, if there was no society, human nature would be demoralised, and cease to have that affection for animals which its objects and humanity are to attain. We owe very great gratitude to animals, more especielly to the useful animals, who help us very much, and, indeed, if it were not for them, we should have to go without many necessary things. For instance, if it were not for the horse, we should be obliged to be much more industrious. • If a man wishes to keep a horse, so that it will recompense him by drawing loads, cr by helping him in some way or another, he must attend to all its necessities, such as its bed. This he must be sure to keep clean. Then he must see that the horse has plenty of food. In the morning it must be properly roomed. Before starting on a journey, it it has a load to draw, it should be seen that it is net too heavy for one horse, or, if none are required, the owner or driver should see that the shoes of the horse are safe, and, if they are not, the horse should be taken to a blacksmith. Then, at certain periods of the day, the horse should be given its meals. The flog is ar.other example of one of those creatures whlen should be properly attended to in order to perform its task. The master should feed it and give it a nice sleeping-place. It should ; have regular baths, .because, unlike the cat, it is unable to clean itself. We know that the dog is one of those faithful creatures, . which will not leave its master until such time as he dies, or, as sometimes the ease is, the master is compelled to part with it, for something or other. I once read of a man who kcpt a New- foundland dog. It was a very faithful crea- ture, and every evening the dog would go to meet his master as he came home from busi- ness. One day his master became ill, and in a very short time he died. The dog followed ' the funeral to the cemetery, -and. it remained on his master's' grave until he died of star- vation. (Co be continued.)</p>
        <p>-4. A society has been formed, called, "The Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals." This society has done much good, as, when a person is found ill-treating on ani- mal, or over-loading a cart horse, he is gener- ally prosecuted by the R.S.P.C.A., but there is still much cruelty in the country. An treated with kindness will obey its master's call at once' or do anything it is told, and will follow him anywhere. I have read many stories of a person saving dogs limn death, and are always afterwards so devoted to its rescuer that it will grieve so much when its master dies that it will not cat, and so pine away, mid is often found dead on its mos er's grave; or, in the dog's turn, will save him 'from drowning or from fire. So it. is much better to be kind, as the above will sl:cw. Ul- treating an animal does not only rueon k!ek- ing or beating it, but half-feeding. and not giv- ing sufficient bedding to sleep on. ond un- cteanliness, so that it loses its briF.htne,:s and grows sullen and bad-tempered. G. KENDALL.</p>
