      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>P OSTAL MID SAV MS BANK REGULATIONS</head>
        <p>P OSTAL MID SAV MS BANK REGULATIONS</p>
        <p>For further particulars see " Post Office Guide," at any Post °lice. POSTAGE STAMPS. Books containing 12 penny and 23 halfpenny stamps are now on sale at each post office. Price 2s.</p>
        <p>INL IND CORRESPONDENCE. The term " Inland Correspondence " means cor- respondence posted within the United Kingdom, including the Isle of Man and the Channel Islands. " Inland Correspondence " is divided into the following classes : (a) Letters (d) Newspapers (b) Postcards (e) Parcels (c) Halfpenny packets Not only letters, but any document or article (with a few trifling exceptions) which can be trans- mitted by post at all, may be sent by letter post, the only limitation being that of size.</p>
        <p>LETTER POST. The prepaid rate of postage is as follows Not exceeding 4 ozs. in weight ... Id. For every additionai 2 0z5.... A letter posted unpaid is chargeable on delivery with double postage; a letter insufficiently paid, with double the deficiency. No letter may exceed two feet in length, one foot in width, or one foot in depth.</p>
        <p>POST-CARDS. The prepaid rate of postage on every post-card is one half-penny, and on every reply post-card one penny. A post-card posted unpaid is chargeable on delivery with one penny. Cards used as post-cards must be made of ordinary card-board, not thicker than the material used for the thickest official post-card. They may not exceed 51 inches in length by 3} incises in width, or be less than 3i inches in length by 2} inches in width. A gummed label (not to exceed 2 inches long and 2-inch wide) bearing the address may now be waxed to the address side of card. If any of the rules relating to a post-card be infringed, the card will be treated as a letter. HALFPENNY PACKET POST. The Halfpenny Packet Post can be used only for packets not exceeding 20z. in weight. Printed matter, Scc., not in the nature of a letter, may be transmitted by this method. NEWSPAPER POST. The prepaid rate of postage on a registered news- paper, including any supplement thereto, whether posted singly or with others is id. Newspapers not registend at the General Post Office are treated in the pcst like any other printed matter.</p>
        <p>PARCEL POST. The rates of postage on parcels areas follows: For a parcel ... ... not exceeding 1 lb. 0 3 ff exceeding 1 lb. o 2 lb. 0 4 „ 2 lb. .f/ 3 lb. 0 5 o „ 3 lb. If 5 lb. 0 6 o If 5 lb. , „ 7 lb. 0 7 II ft 7 lb. o 8 lb. 0 8 f f ff 8 lb. 9 lb. 0 9 If o 9 lb. :: 10 lb. 010 o /f 10 lb. . o _ll lb. 011 No parcel may exceed 111 b. in weight. The size allowed for an inland parcel is : Greatest lenmth 3 ft. 6 in. Greatest length and girth combined 6 ft. 0 in. For example—A parcel measuring 3ft. Gin. in its longest dimensions as much as 2ft. 6in. round its thickest part ; a short parcel may be thicker • thus, if it measure no more than 3ft. in length, it: may measure as much as 31t. round its thickest part. The postage on a parcel must be prepaid. Mark each parcel" Parcel Post." A parcel must not be posted in a letter box, but presented at the counter of a post office, and the sender should see that the weight, size and postage are in order before leaving. PARCELS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL). Parcels are accepted for parcel post to most foreign countries under conditions similar to the inland regulations, except that for customs require- ments the contents must be declared. The customs charges can be prepaid or collected on delivery. The postage varies to different countries and must be prepaid. MoNF:Y ORDERS (INLAND.) The postal authorities cannot issue a single money order for more than £4O, and will only cash them at the money order office at which they are made payable, unless they are crossed, when they can be paid through a bank. No fractional part of a id. can be contnined in a money order. The rates of poundage are :—Not exceeding £l, 2d. ; r.,3, 3d.; £lO, 4d.; £2O, 6d. ; £3O, Bd. ; £4O, 10d. By paying an additional 2d. and cost of the telegram of advice, a money order can be telegraphed under certain restrictions. MONEY ORDERS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL.) The maximum amount for which a money order may be issued for places abroad varies in each particular place. The poundage rates are :—Not exceeding £l,3d. ; £2, 6d. ; and above that, 3d. for each additional £2 or portion of it. A fee of 6d. in addition to the ordinary poundage, together with cost of telegram of advice, enables money orders to be telegraphed to certain places abroad. POSTAL ORDERS. . ~. .</p>
        <p>These are obtainable at any money order office in the United Kingdom, and possess the advantage over money orders that they can be cashed at any money order office. If crossed, they can be paid into a bank. They are issued for fixed amounts, rising by 6d., from 6d. to Li Is. The poundage fees are :--id. up to 2s. 61.; Id. UD to 155.; lid. up to 21s. Before parting with a postal order, always fill in the name of the person to whom the amount is to be paid. Each order is numbered, as is also the counterfoil attached, which latter should be retained and name of payee filled in. The Post- master-General will entertain no further claim after an order is once paid. Stamps to the amount of sd. can be affixed to make up odd sums.</p>
        <p>TELEGRAMS (INLAND). The Postmaster-General possesses,almost entirely, the privilege of transmitting,collecting and deliver- ing telegrams in the United Kingdom. The charge for a telegram is a minimum amount of 6d., which covers 12 words, and an additional d. for each extra word. This charge includes delivery within the postal district if sent out for delivery from a head telegraph office, but if from a sub-of rice then within three miles only, beyond which a charge of 3d. per mile is made from the office door. All charges are, as far as possible, prepared by affixing stamps to telegram form. Address of person receiv- ing a telegram has to be paid for, but sender's name if written on back of form need not be. Five figures count as one word. The cost of a reply may be prepaid. Telegrams can be repeated on payment of half the original charge, which extra payment is refunded if error is detected. Telegrams may be posted in wall or pillar boxes, or handed to rural postmen, and are wired as soon as received at the nearest telegraph office. Late fees secure attention, in certain cases, to telegrams handed in after the usual hours. The cost of registering an abbrevi• ated address, which must consist of two words, is £1 Is.</p>
        <p>TF.LEGRAMS (FOREIGN AND COLONIAL) Must be written on special forms and are accepted in the language of any of the principal European countries. The rates vary, and are too numerous to detail here. POST OFFICE SAVINGS BANK.</p>
        <p>These are established at all money order °Mmes. In ordinary cases the annual deposit must not exceed £5O, and the total amount £2OO. No person can have more than one account. Amounts placed in the bank on behalf of children under seven years of age cannot be withdrawn uatil the child reaches that age, and then only under his or her own signa- ture. The smallest amount received at a time is Is., and this may take the form of 12 penny stamps affixed to a special form. Each depositor receives a bank book in which the amount of each deposit should be entered at the time of paying in. If the deposit is £1 or over, a special acknowledgment is sent from the head London odice. Interest is allowed at the rate of 21 per cent. Any amount up to and including £1 may ha withdrawn without previous notice, on presenting a bank book. The bank book should be sent each year, postage free, on the anniversary of the date when the account was opened, to the Controller of the Savings Bank Department for purposes of checking and adding interest. The withdrawal of money can bee xpedited by telegraphing, the depositor paying cost of telegrams. P.O. TELEPHONE AND TRUNK LINR. CHARGES.</p>
        <p>The fees for the use of the Trunk lines are as follows : For 25 miles or under ... ... ... 3d. „ 50 „ fl, ... ... ... 6c/. 11 75 )1 t 9 „ 100 , „ ... ... 1/- „ every additional 40 miles or fraction thereof... ... _ ~. ... _ ... 6d.</p>
        <p>..„ _ Between the hours of 7 p.m. and 7 a.m., six minutes conversation is allowed for the same price as three minutes between 7 a.m. and 7 p.m., and a reduced charge is made for three minute calls.</p>
        <p>MOTOR CAR AND CYCLE REGULATIONS. Each one must be registered with the council of a county or county borough, the fee being £1 fored motor car, and ss. for a motor cycle. A register a number is assigned to each, which it is an offence not to keep allixed when driving. Each driver Must possess a license, costing 55., to be renewed every 12 months, and obtainable from the county authorities, which may be endorsed of suspended for reckless driving or certain other nilences. The highest speed at which a car msy he driven on the public highways is 20 miles an hour, which is reduced to 10 miles under special local regula- tions. It is an offence under the Motor Car Act (1903), to drive a car in such a way on a public highway as to be dargerous to the public, having regard to all the circumstances of the case. In the event of an accident to any person due to ho presence of the motor car on the road, the river must supply his name and address, and the twner and number of the car, under heavy penal- odie The driver must produce his licence at the sequest of a police constvtble.</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday October 10, 1908. RUGBY FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday October 10, 1908. RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S NIATCHES. Goal of any description 2 points. Try 3 points. Australians</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP. Lancashire</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. I'. P. Hunslet ,slVakefleld Trin .. 2 519 Batley I+llllll *Huddersfield 7 735 Merthyr Tydvil 1 1 5 *Dewsbury 5 422 Bradford *Halifax *Widnes Treherbert *Leigh 4 420 Wigan 2 1 7 1 *Swinton 3 2 12 Runcorn '*St. Helens 4 626 Barrow Ebbw Vale 0 4 12 *Barry *Keighley *Salford Oldham 2 1 7 *Rochdale H.... 1 1 5</p>
        <p>Warrington Beaten at Salford. The Warrington men proved unequal to the task of beating Salford at the Willows on Saturday, though they made a plucky fight. Brooks' and Taylor were absent, while Salford were short of Lomas and Warwick. Six thousand spectators assembled in beautiful weather and were rewarded by witnessing a tine game. Teams : Salford.—Cochrane, back ; Hvsm, Mesley, Thomas and Cook, three-quarter backs ; 'Preston and John, half backs; Thom, Curzon, Brown, Harris, Spencer, and Coates, forwards. Warrington,—Tilley, back; Malt, Dickenson, Cart- wright, and Jordan, three-quarter backs; Jenkins and Lloyd, half backs; Dowell, P. Boardman, A. E. Board- man, Shortt, Naylor and Becton, forwards. Referee, Mr. R. Robinson (Bradford) ; touch judges, Messrs. C. Platt (Swinton) and C. Simm (Wigan.) Warrington opened in very vigorous fashion and had the better of matters for a time. The backs comkined well and in three minutes from the start Jenkins scored a fine try, Fish landing a goal. Salford then resumed the upper hand for the remainder of the first half, and it was not long before good work between Hyam and Mesley ended in the latter scoring, for Brown to land a goal. Close on the interval another five points were credited to the Salfordians through a try by Cooke, and a goal by Mesley, who was playing a flue game. Half- time score :—tialtord 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points), Warrington 1 goal and 1 try (5 points). In the second half some very keen play was witnessed, and Warrington had several opportunities of scoring, but failed. Mesley landed a penalty goal for the " Reds," and in the closing stages Fish landed a penalty goal for the visitors, but Mesley kicked another fine place goal on behalf of Salford. Result :—Salford 1 goals and 2 tries (14 points), Warrington 2 goals and 1 try 7 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>. WIDNES v. BROUGHTON RANGERS: A DRAWN GAME.</head>
        <p>. WIDNES v. BROUGHTON RANGERS: A DRAWN GAME.</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes, before 3,000 spectators. The ' Rangers won the toss, and Widnes started against a bright sun. Hogg made several good attempts to break through, and then a cross-kick by Hicks gave Taylor possession, and he scored under the posts. G. Aspey improved. The Rangers then made a determined attack on the home line, and after a good round of passing llogg scored, Barlow improving. Half-time score : Widnes, 1 goal and 1 try (5 points) ; Broughton, 1 goal and 1 try (S points). The first portion of the second half was very evenly contested. Then son e faulty play on the pert of Hicks let in Claude James, who passed to Hogg, and the latter scored. Barlow again improved. Towards the finish Widnes attacked, and following a scrambling movement S. Aspey crossed the line. G. Aspey placed a goal from a difficult position. Result :—Widnes, 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points) ; Broughton, 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>RUNCORN'S FIRST DEFEAT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN'S FIRST DEFEAT.</p>
        <p>Swinton were at borne to Runcorn before 5,000 spectators and Jones opened the score by kicking a goal from a mark. This early success acted as a stimu- lant to Swinton, end splendid passing amongst the backs ended in Gartrell scoring a try, Jones improved. and half-time arrived with Swinton leading by seven points. On the resumption, Swinton once more took up the attack, and were rewarded by Jones, who throughout had played a great game, dropping a goal when sur- rounded by his opponents. At this stage Runcorn were a thoroughly keaten team, and from a scrummage Carrell scored his second try. Following this reverse Runcorn took play to the Swinton end, and Flanagan scored. Result :—Swinton, 3 goals 2 tries (12 points) ; Runcorn, 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>ST. HELENS BEAT BARROW.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS BEAT BARROW.</p>
        <p>The Saints had an easy win against a weakened Barrow team, and their form greatly pleased 2,000 spectators. Greenwood, late of Wigan, played for the Saints at centre and did some smart work. In the first half, McKenny of Barrow broke the fingers of his left hand and bad to retire. Final :—St. Helens, 4 goals, 6 tries (2G points) ; Barrow, 1 goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " v. SALFORD.—These teams met before a large crowd at Wilderspool on Saturday, the Warringtonians gaining an easy victory. The home team soon evidenced superiority both in forwards and backs, and after Daintith had led to the line Forstar secured from the loose and scored. Still attacking, Warrington came near scoring repeatedly, and after the three-quarter rear line had all handled,Bradshaw dashed over at the corner, Daintith failing at the goal kick. Salford were prectically outclassed, and eventually a fine individual effort by Landor ended in a splendid try, which again Foster failed to convert, Warrington con- tinued to have tho best of matters until faulty fielding gave Salford their first opportunity and M`Whirter was just pulled down in time. Salford now improved, and APlntyre showed admirable defensive qualities for Warrington. The "Wirepullers " again attacked strongly, and Bradshaw, Kay, and Landor were pulled up in succession on the Near the interval a smart effort by Mclntyre ended in Bradshaw scoring. Half-time : Warrington, 12 points ; Salford, nil. Warrington again attacked on resuming, and smart forward work ended in Harmer crossing, Landor ran over from half-way. Forster next secured in midfield and defeated the Salford backs, whilst immediately after Oakes made an opening for Harries, who got in at the corner. Another try to Forster was followed by Harries again scoring, but in all the cases the goal-kicking was very weak. A smart movement by the Salford's back ended in Isherwood scoring. Brad- shaw put on a fine try and Forster kicked a goal, and in the last minute Harries dropped a goal. Final : War- rington,two goals eleven tries (37 points) ; Salford, one try (three points).</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION. LANCASHIRE CUP DRAW. A meeting of the Lancashira County Union was held in Manchester on Tuesday evening. The following officials were eppointed for the first round of the Lancashire Senior Cup, to be played on Saturday, November 7th : Oldham v. Salford; referee, IL Robinson (Bradford). Widnes v. Wigan; referee, IL Renton (Hunslet). Runcorn v. Broughton Rangers; referee, J. Chumley (Leeds). St. Helens v. Warrington; referee, H. Farrar (Reighley).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>PENKETit ALBION_ V. WELLFIELD.</p>
        <p>, These tea-11-s-iii-e.4 -cOnt;l-tiSiCit-is..c.on-thi-a-g-r-o-und of the former on Saturday. Webb started for the home team and a combined movement ended in Knovles scoring three minutes after the start. The Albion again took up the attack and Forster added a second. WeMeld then pre,sed and Monks shot wide. Knowles and Forster on the left took play once more into the visitors' territory, the ..latter adding a third. Penketh led at half-time by three goals to nothing. On the restart the home team again pressed and from a corner Webb scored a fourth. Not to be denied Wellfleld went into the fray and a foul was given against Penketh. and Farrington, making no mistake, put the ball well into the corner, and Booth added a second. Final : Penketh Albion, four goals; Wellfleld, two goals.</p>
        <p>ELEMENTARY SCGOOLS' LEAGUE. RESULTS TO DATE.</p>
        <p>P. W. D. L. For Agst. P. —Goals Parochial Heathside Wycliffo 4 3 0 1 6 . 3 6 St.Barnabas' 3 .. 2 ... 0 ... 1 ... 6 ..• 5 ... 4 Ar p 1 ey-street 4 ... 2 ... 0 ... 2 ... S ... 14 ... 4 Trinity Hamilton st 3 ... 1 ... 1 ~. 1 ... 6 ... 6 ... 3 St. James' 4 . 1 1 . 9 3 . 4 3 St. Mary's 9 1 0... 1 4 . 6... 2 Christ Church 4 ... 0 ... 1 ... I ... 5 ..• 19 ... 1 Beamont Silver street 2 ... 0 ... 0 ... 2 ... 2 ••• 8 ... 0 Eit. Alban's</p>
        <p>Mathside mounted another rung of the League ladder as a consequence of their match with Beamont. Eventll played a good game for the losers, while the two full-backs did all that was possible to kcep their</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>A NEW RUBBER-YIELDING TREE.</p>
        <p>Dubard and Everhardt give an account in " Comptes Rendu" of the discovery in Tonkin of a new species of Bleckrodea called B. ton- kinensis, belonging to the Urticacew, the latex of which contains a large proportion of caoutchouc of the highest quality. B. ton- kinensis is a tree attaining to a height of ten to fifteen yards, much branched, leaves ob- long, stipulate; flowers monceeious, forming cynics more or less condensed into heads. The male flowers are often grouped around a central female flower. This is the finest rubber-producing tree discovered in Indo-China.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WHY PAPER DECAYS.</head>
        <p>WHY PAPER DECAYS.</p>
        <p>The brownish spots which appear in old books are really due to the ravages of bac- teria, says Popular 21Ier/waifs. The tiny de- stroyer is especially fond of starchy mate- rial, and its propagation is promoted by damp. It has been well understood that damp produced discolouration and decay, but the share of the microbe in the operation has not hitherto been suspected. Tiny Lin- gua or mould is responsible for grey and black marks upon old papers. In spotting the surface the fungus helps to break down the fabric and hasten the process of its destruction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>METEORS AND STEEL.</head>
        <p>METEORS AND STEEL.</p>
        <p>A study of the great collection of meteorites in the Natural History Museum of Vienna leads Dr. F. Berwerth to the interesting con- clusion that meteoritic iron, as it falls from the sky, and the various steels produced in our modern steel works are the result of essentially similar chemical and physical causes. One of the most striking charac- teristics noted in meteoritic iron is the pre- sence of a considerable quantity of nickel. But the mechanism, by means of which nature, operating in celestial space, has pro- duced what is virtually nickel steel, remains to be explained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>BLUE LIGHT TO PRESERVE FRUIT.</head>
        <p>BLUE LIGHT TO PRESERVE FRUIT.</p>
        <p>Tests with red, green, blue, and white light during the last two years have shewn a French experimenter that colours have effects on vegetation that may prove of great practical importance. Under red light, for instance, the growth of vegetables was fifteen times as rapid as under blue light. Blue light, on the other hand, had a remarkable preservative power on growing vegetation, and two years after planting the first leaves of an oak tree were as fresh and vigorous as ever. Of even greater economic value seems the discovery that ripe fruit keeps remark- ably under a blue screen, sliewing no decay after several weeks.</p>
        <p>HOW WE COME TO BE RIGHT-HANDED. The problem of right-handedness, Dr. George M. Gould concludes, is solved by two military and social facts: (1) From the first use of implements of offence and defence, the left or heart side has been protected by the shield and the left hand has been called the shield hand; (2) and the free or right hand was not only employed for the spear, but for counting in primitive barter and other purposes, becoming necessarily domi- nant in all active operations. The pecu- liarity seems to be entirely absent in lower animals. Squirrels that hold nuts in their fore paws, cats that strike insects or play with mice, and even monkeys and gorillas, 'give no suggestion of a preference for paw or eye of either right or left side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>TIIE GREAT CENTRAL ORB.</head>
        <p>TIIE GREAT CENTRAL ORB.</p>
        <p>In the course of his lecture, entitled " A Look Through the Universe," Sir Oliver Lodge called attention to the sun, and spoke of its amazing temperature a temperature much hotter than any artificial temperature which could be produced, and supposed to be about 6,ooodeg. At the enormous distance of 92,000,000 miles we found the heat of the sun oppressive during summer. It was instructive to hold out the band and feel the heat of a fire so many millions of miles away, and to realise how important that hot body was to the earth; for without it there could be no life on this planet or on any of the other planets. Its heat was probably produced by the shrinkage of its own substance. It was so enormous that what on the earth were simple earthquakes were on the sun of such violence as to keep it at its enormous temperature, in spite of its constantly cooling and radiating its heat away into space. Sunspots, as Sir Oliver pointed out, were a sign of great solar activity which was not always favourable to the British Islands. They wiira however, good for the earth as a whole.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>POWER OF A JET OF WATER.</head>
        <p>POWER OF A JET OF WATER.</p>
        <p>In the neighbourhood of Grenoble there is a manufactory which employs the water held in a reservoir situated some 1,600 ft. up on the mountain. The water is brought to the manufactory by a vertical pipe of this length and somewinet less than lin. in diameter, the force of the falling jet being employed to drive a turbine. If, now, by means of a lateral pipe, says the Strand Magazine, the jet be allowed to escape, it spurts up with such force into the air, owing to the velocity imparted to it by its long previous fall, that a strong man armed with a sword may hack at it until he is exhausted or splinters his weapon into fragments without making the slightest impression upon it. The -weapon is checked as effectually as. if it had struck against a bar of iron. And yet this jet of water which is so invulnerable to the, keen- edged steel is but of the thickness of a man's thumb. The speed at which the jet moves, moreover, is, relatively speaking, by no means great; it does not much exceed 100 yards a second—that is to say, about the tenth of the speed of a cannon ball.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>SHIPS STRUCK BY LIGHTNING.</head>
        <p>SHIPS STRUCK BY LIGHTNING.</p>
        <p>Ships remote from the land are seldom damaged by lightning, despite the fact that some of the most awe-inspiring displays of at- mospheric electricity are frequently witnessed by those on board of them. Standing rigging, and even parts of the running gear, are now made of steel wire, and this substitute for the old-fashioned hemp serves the purpose of a lightning conductor when the ship is not fitted with such an aid to safety. The electric current is conveyed down the wire rigging, and reaches the sea through the vessel's metal hull. Damage only occurs, says Know- ledge, if the current be interrupted on its way to earth. In a comparatively large propor- tion of instances the fore-royal truck is struck by lightning, that of the main less fre- quently, and the mizen least of the three. Very serious casualties under this bead oc- curred to warships and merchant vessels in the days of wooden hulls and hempen rigging. Many 'vessels are now fitted with lightning conductors of approved types, lest the wire rigging should fail to carry oft the electric current.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>MAKING OZONE.</head>
        <p>MAKING OZONE.</p>
        <p>A plant for the manufacture of ozone nas been built at the • Homeopathic Hospital, Pittsburg. The result of the experiments is, says an American paper, likely to prove of great value, enabling cities everywhere to furnish a water supply from which all organic matter has been removed. The ' plant was erected at a cost exceeding 10,COOdol. The idea is the creation of Professor Girard, of Belgium, who also has been making experi- ments. There is only one other plant of the kind in the world, that one. being fh Holland. The ozone, whk is manufactured on a scien- tific basis, is exp?fcted not only to kill organic matter in water, but to destroy bacteria of every kind. Ozone is</p>
        <p>MADE BY ELECTROLYSIS, the process decomposing a chemical core. pound by the passage of an electric current through it. Electrolysis decomposes the air and makes the ozone. This is done first by passing air in its natural state through lime. Its passage through the lime takes all the moisture out of it. The air in its dry state is then forced into a glass tube. There is a tube a tube. One is used for the dry an and the other for °zoning, and by the time the au' reaches the water tanks it has become ozone. Men who have worked about the plant at the hospital, and have inhaled the nmeous substance, assert that it has cured a .ioid in twenty minutes. 'lnc tests already atafle have been sufficiently successful almert co assure that pure water can be sa.;ured treatinz it with the ozone proees3.,</p>
        <p>_ •: .</p>
        <p>, - .</p>
        <p>HOW TO MAKE LIGHT BUNS. By using Eiffel Tower Bun Flour the most inexperienced person can make light Buns and Cakes with certain success. Try a id. packet.</p>
        <p>MERRYWEATRE R ON WATER SUPPLY &amp; FIRE PROTECTION</p>
        <p>County Mansions; Experts sent to all parts to Report on Existing Arrangements.</p>
        <p>Write for Pamphlets Merryweather &amp; Sons 63, LONG ACRE, LONDON, W. C.</p>
      </div>
