      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT. THE BUDGET INTRODUCED. Increased Death Duties and Graduated</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT. THE BUDGET INTRODUCED. Increased Death Duties and Graduated</p>
        <p>Income Tax.</p>
        <p>The members of our local Parliament engaged in the pleasant pastime of juggling with millions on Thursday evening. It was Budget night and the Chancellor of the Exchequer (Mr. E. England) unfolded to an expectant House his proposals for providing the where- withal to carry on the Government's work of reform during the coming year. Curiously enough, the pros- pect of an important debate did not arouse any great enthusiasm, and there were large gaps in many parts of the House. The Premier (Mr. John Upson) was again an absentee, and his place was taken by the Chief Secre- tary for Ireland (Mr. J. F. Awbery). QUESTION TIME. In answer to Mr. RIGBY (Scotland division of Liver- pool), Mr. E. WYNNE-JONES (Chester) stated that the Government were quite prepared to give the same liberty to Roman Catholics as to anyone else, and they only wished that in other countries Roman Cakelics would extend a like freedom to men of different faiths. (Applause). Replying to Mr. F. A. JENXINS (Walton Division of Liverpool) Mr. WILLIAM CLEGG (Halifax) on behalf of the Home Secretary, said there was no such thing of imprisonment for debt. With regard to women obtain- ing credit frcm pachmen and money-lenders, be could assure the House that the Government would do all in their power to make that process difficult, but they felt ' that to bring in a Bill to emend thelaw would interfere somewhat with the liberty of the people, and at present they did not intend to take any such step. Mr. H. HOPE (Cambridge), asked the Prime Minister whether it was the intention of the Government to pro- vide temporary pensions for widows left with large families of young children instead of lowering their "caste" by forcing them into alms houses. Mr. J. F. AwDERY said the Government had very little to add to what was said the previous week with regard to meddling with the Poor Law machinery until they had the report of the Poor Law Coparaission now sitting. The idea of the member who asked the question seemed to be that a fixed pension should be awarded to the unfortunate widows who were left with large families whereas the Poor Law machinery at present at their disposal allowed an amount which varied accord- ing to the necessities of the recipient and the number of children. An amusing question stood in the name of Mr. Hawthorne (E. Belfast) who asked the chief Secretary for Ireland if he would plaae on the table of the House the result of his late 'secret" journey to the West of Ireland. Mr. AwEERY : It is not my intention to charge the hon. member with undue inquisitivensss. My object in going to Ireland, as I hope all the members of the Cabinet will in future, was to acquaint myself with the real needs and necessities of poor old Ireland. (Laughter and applause.) THE BUDGET. -</p>
        <p>- - The CH V.CCELLOR OF THE EXCHEQUER (Mr. E. England) had a hearty reception from all parts of the House on rising to introduce his first Budget. He began with an allusion to the growing wealth of the country and deplored the fact that owing to certain systems in our midst the great bulk of the people had not relatively benefited by the increasing riches, the people being used too much for the building up of material wealth instead of that wealth being used for the building up of a great and noble nation. After a few words of congratulation on the substantial reduc- tion of the national debt by the Liberal administration —a matter of great moment to every citizen—the Chancellor passed on to his financial proposals. First of all, he said, the Government proposed to cheapen the food of the people. For that purpose they would take off the whole of the duty on sugar—(applause)— and that would not only reduce the weekly expenditure in all the houses of all classes but would assist many of our iiidustries and create more employment. Further, as the Gov_prnment did not believe in half measures they propored to drop the whole of the duty on ten— I in fact they had decided to go further and propose what was the aim of that truly great financier and statesman, Mr. William Ewart Gladstone—(cheers)—to abolish the whole of the taxes on food. The Govern- ment, through the King's Speech, notified their inten- tion of taking in hand the unemployed problem which was a most pressing one, and he proposed to allocate out of public funds the sum of £1,000,000 on account of the money they felt would be necessary. The House had already passed an Old Age Pensions Bill and the Goyernmest had pledged itself to find the money. They were therefore called upon to find and re-impose the following sums :</p>
        <p>Old Age Pensions (which would absorb £15,000,008 but towards which they had in band £2,250,000 ear-marked for the purpose) 12,750,000 Abolition of sugar duty 6,707,809 Tea duty Coffee Chicory 44,600 Cocoa and chocolate Currants Raisins 215,859 Figs, prunes, Sc. ---</p>
        <p>£27,199,983</p>
        <p>That was a large sun, of money and he was faced with the problem of financially arranging it, but he wished them to bear in mind that the increased expenditure for the year would be only £13,750,000 on account of old age pensions and unemployment. The balance of £13,449,983, the amount of the food taxes, was simply a matter of readjustment. "Now where shall I obtain this money ? " asked the Chancellor. "Well, at one time I had seriously thought of imposing a tax upon bachelors. (Laughter.) I think they deserve to have a heavy tax placed upon them, for they certainly do not do their duty to the State. (Laughter.) Yet I felt I could not do so unless I placed at the same time a tax upon spinsters. (Laughter.) This, in face of what the suffragists are doing at the present time, needs more courage than I as Chancellor of the Exchequer possess, so I hasten away—quickly—from tapping such a source as that. (Laughter.) Some would further tax motors, but they overlook the fact that not only would they be interfering with an industry which now employs a great number of people, but under our present system of national finance the additional tax would not go into the National Exchequer but into the hands of the local authorities,and the com- paratively small amount thus obtained would not be worth serioully entertaining." Continuing the CHANCELLOR said be preferred to obtain what he wanted by tapping sources where it would least be felt and which would not in any way be prejudicial to tine best interests of anyone. In the first place he proposed to increase the death or estate duties on a graduated scale. He did not know of any better or more equitable way of Obtaining revenue. When people had passed over to the majority they certainly had no use for their money, and what better use could made of it than returning a portion to the State? (Laughter). "A person dying " said Mr. England " would then have the comfort of knowing that he had in some measure assisted the community, and as some people appear to be anxious to die for their country, they will now have this pleasure afforded them." (Laughter). About 620,000 people died every year in the United Kingdom worth nothing and 80,000 people died worth £300,000,000. As a rule the State only obtained from estate duties about £15,000,000, which was a ridiculously small sum, and therefore he proposed to obtain a further £15.000,000 by increasing the duties on a graduated scale, commencing on estates of the value of £lO,OOO. As he required a little over .£12,000,000 more he proposed looking for part of it to the Chancellor's old friend—the income-tax payer. He should iequest those who enjoyed incomes of from , £lO,OOO to £20,000 to pay an additional tax of Is. Gd. in the £ ; those xrho had an income of from £20,000 to £10,C60 an additional tax of 2s.in the ; and those who had incomes of £40,000 and upwards, an additional 3s. in the £. This he estimated would realise £10,375,000. To obtain the balance of £1,321,983 he proposed to go to a new source which had too long been left alone—he referred to the mining royalties. (Applause.) The aver- age royalty paid on each ton of coal raised was SM. The remarkable thing about these royalties was that the whole of them, which amounted to £8,000,000 per annum and kept on incrensing, went into the pockets of a few who did nothing In connection with the working of the coal. He proposed that the private owners, who were few, should be requested to share with the people, who were many, and for that purpose a fourth of the royalty should in future be paid into the Exchequer. He should raise by that means about £2,600,000 per annum which would leave him with a balance in hand after providing £12,000,000 for old-age pensions, £1,000,000 for unemployment and taking off over £13,000,000 by the abolition of food taxes. When the hon. members grasped the effect of the proposals they would find that they meant that more than nine-tenths of the people would have £29,000,000 more purchasing power in their midst, most of which would be spent on the necesseries of life and would foster our industries to a considerable degree. In conclusion he claimed that the proposals were simple in practice but effective in purpose. (Cheers). Mr. F. G. T mon (Petersfield) congratulated the Chancellor of the Exchequer on the extremely lucid manner in which he had put forward his proposals—on paper. (Laughter.) He did not, however, think they would prove quite so easy in practice. As to the reduction of the national debt by many millions during the term of office of the Government it was news to him that it was a matter for congratulation in one way, because he always understood as a matter of economy and politics that the larger the national debt was the greater the eredit of the country. (Laughter.) "Hon. members may .smile," retorted Mr. Taylor, "but they will find my view confirmed on their own side of the House by their greatest leaders." The Chancellor of the Exchequer had abandoned Mr. Gladstone's views xvhen he clai med the right to increase the income tax,</p>
        <p>for there wee no doubt that Mr. Gladstone and other Liberal leaders of the time claimed that the income tax should be abolished if possible and that it should only be resorted to in times of extremity. The Chancellor of the Exchequer last session told them that 261 millions would be required for old age pensions, but now he calmly told them that he would only need 12 millions. Why was there no provision in the Budget for the £2,750,000 which was promised as a grant to Ireland and the .£1,000,000 which was to be ' set apart for education? The Chancellor of the Exchequer had altogether forgotten that at the end of the year be would have to face a deficit which could not be less than £6,000,000. How did he propose to provide for the additional expenditure on the building of battleships to which the Liberal Government was pledged during the coming year ? As to the abolition of food taxes, he was struck by the easy way in which the Chancellor escaped the question by saying it was merely a matter of adjustment. Criticising the proposal re death duties, Mr. Taylor said tho Chancellor must know that the more he taxed in that direction the less likelihood was there of the additional sum he required being forthcoming, because people possessing large incomes would make gifts to their families before they died, instead of leaving the money by will, and thus avoid the duty. If the income tax were increased, the taxpayer would have to economise and more unemploy- ment would be produced. Against all the unemploy- ment, the Government only provided a beggarly one million. (Laughter.) He failed to see how the general population would benefit by the taxation of mining royalties, because the price of coal was sure to go up. Mr. WARDMAN (Westhoughton) strongly urged that all mining royalties should go into the Exchequer, but until that was achieved he was in full accord with their taxation. Had the Chancellor taken his courage in both hands and diverted the royalties to the State, he would have earned the gratitude of all true reformers and progressives. (Applause.) The £1,000,00 allocated to the unemployed was totally inadequate. The Govern- ment had been legislating in a panic in this matter, instead of foreseeing the problem and providing for emergencies. The Chancellor had missed a golden opportunity in not allocating anything to the feeding of school children, as the local rates were becoming too great a burden. Until they dealt drastically with the land question, all their efiorts to increase the purchasing power of the people would be futile, as the landowners would reap the frnits. Mr. IV. CLEOG (Postmaster General) said the party opposite ought to be the last to talk about being pre- pared for war. (Hear, hear.) After the South African war experience, the Tories ought to hide their heads in the sand for the next two generations. (Laughter and applause.) The Budget was undoubtedly the best that could be introduced under the circumstances. Mr. W. H. STANSVIELD (leader of the Opposition) said the Chancellor of the Exchequer had confessed that he was going through the country seeking hen roosts to rob. "I think" he went on "that the Chancellor has found some very good hen roosts— (Ministerial applause)—and he is going to rob them very effectually. (Hear, hear.) But if you go about robbing all the hen roosts people will give over keeping hens—(laughter)—and if you are going to put these outrageously excessive duties on capitalists, you will find that capital, which is very timid, will take wings and fly away." (Laughter and a Voice" Where to? ") He maintained that the heavy income tax pro- posed would not encourage men to invest their money in icdustries in this country. Higher death duties would simply provoke people to act almost dishonestly and at any rate, dishonourably. If the Government took it out of them by income tax and death duties, they were going to kill enterprise altogether. The £1,000,000 devoted to unemployment was ridiculous. (A Voice : "It is more than the £200,000 granted by the previous Government.") Mr. Stansfleld worked out the grant to Is. Bd. per week per family and expressed regret that the Government had not faced the question courage- ously. He also taunted them with doing nothing towards the taxation of land values and said he doubted if the Ministerial views on the question were ever meant to be translated into action. Mr. Stansfield propounded some original ideas with regard to food taxes. He believed in taxing what was injurirms to the people, and therefore he would increase the tax on beer, wines and spirits. (Ministerial cheers.) He might stand alone on his side but he would increase the tax on anything that was injurious. He, however, believed that tea was quite as injurious as beer and further that , sugar and "pop " were harmful and therefore they ought all to be taxed. (Laughter.) The Government had treated financial questions in a half-hearted fashion and appeared to be catering for a low-down sort of popularity. The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK (Ashford, Kent) said it was delightfully refreshing to find a member of the Opposition favouring a tax on beer. He took it as a tribute to what the Government were endeavouring to do with regard to the drink question. (Applause). Alluding to death duties, the speaker said that if those who possessed large incomes chose deliberately to play the dishenourable, the Government would tax the dis- honourable. (Applause). He appealed as a matter of justice and righteousness, not because he was a parson but because he was a man, whether it was fair that the great bulk of the money should be in the hands of 5,000,000 people while 28,000,000 had not a tithe of it. ("No.") If the gentlemen with millions did the dishonourable, the Government would do the drastic, because the law must be upheld. If a gentleman on the other side had £lO,OOO a year was he not sufficient of an Englishman to be willing to pay 4s. in the £ towards his brother who was down? (Applause.) Were the wealthy going to store their money in their pockets and see their brothers go to thei devil? The whdle question seemed to move in a wrong atmosphere. They had better have less of what was , called wealth in the sense of money and more of the I wealth of honest, straight and clean-living people. (Applause.) Mr. M. G. He.tseNa (Kilkenny South) suggested that if the Government wanted to get to the root of things they must reduce the national expenditure. Dealing with Mr.Stansfield's argument that sugar was injurious, particularly to the teeth, he said the natives of the East and West Indies practically lived on a sugar diet and had the finest teeth in the universe. (Laughter.) Mr. G. H. W. STEEL (Northwich), startled the House by opening his speech with the words "My heart bleeds." When the laughter had subsided, he added 1" For this progressive Government—this party which was progressing faster than the great socialist party, and which has to go hack and adopt Sir William Harcourt's scheme of 1891 re death duties. I had hoped the Chancellor of the Exchequer would have come with something original. I gave him ideas week after week last session." (Laughter.) Mr. Steel said he had shown bow 21 millions could be raised by the taxation of bookmakers, but this Puritanical Govern- ment said they would never think of taxing vice. He was surprised that the Government, being such temper- ance reformers, were still prepared to continue accepting the revenue from the drink trade. "I can supply the gentlemen opposite with ideas went on the hon. member " but unforennately I cannot supply them with brains." The House rocked with laughter at this announcement but Mr. steel was quite unmoved and said he could not suppy the Government with brains to put forward the idea. He went on to express the hope that the time would not be far dOstant when they would have on the Government benches men with ideas and with brains to work them out. Protesting against the proposed increased taxation of the capitalist, Mr. Steel saiel that if the Government suggested the taxation of parsons (The Rev. Harvey Cook : " We should pay it ") they would have heard the voice of the member for Ashford rising in larger volume than it had already done. (Laughter.) Mr. P. O'BRIEN (St. Pancras) complained that the Opposition in the zeal of partizanship appeared to lose their common sense. As to the alleged injurious effects of tea drinking,he said that when the day came that the majority of our judges declared that most of the crime was &amp;tie to excessive tea drinking, the same men who had been working against the liquor evil would take in hand the redaction of tea drinking. (Applause.) He believed in all mining royalties going to the State. At this stage the debate was adjourned until Thursday next, on the proposition of Mr. E. WYNNE JONES (Chester),</p>
      </div>
