      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>FRIENDS' SCHOOL—On Thursday evening a lantern lecture, which was the first of the season, was given by Mr. C. E. Thompson, of Birkenhead, in the school lecture hall. The lecture was entitled "The Gorges of the river Ardecho." There was a good audience. Mr. W. E. Brown presided. An excellent selection of views was thrown upon the screen, all of which were the results of the lecturer's own efforts.—Mr. J. Thompson proposed a vote of thanks to the lecturer, which was seconded by Miss Lizzie Harrison and cordially passed. Mr. Thorpe manipulated the lantern. .</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.—The above Bible class held their usual open service on Sunday at Penketh Lodge, the chapel being occupied by the Sunday school children. Rev. R. E. Letheren, of Warrington, addressed the gathering and Mr. Druet occupied the chair. The Bible class choir sang the anthem "When I survey the wondrous Cross." Mr. Herbert Plimpton also sang two solos. Mrs. Parker cffciated at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Separation Order Granted.</head>
        <p>Separation Order Granted.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor and other magistrates yesterday, Margaret E. Boardman applied for a separation from her husband.—The case had been adjourned for a month to see if the defendant would amend his ways. Mrs. Boardman said the defendent was much better the first week, but she was sorry to say he bad treated her most cruelly the second week.—Defendant said it would be better for them if they had a separation.— Mr. Firth, the police court missionary, said that on going to defendant's house on Thursday, he found the defendant and his wife quarrelling —A separation order was granted, allowing the wife 15s. weekly with the custody of the three children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 10, 1908.</p>
      </div>
