eenall was won by E. North who also received a gold VOTES OF THANKS. medal (the gift of Councillor Fulton-Smith). The Bro. JaataS PARR moved a vote of thanks to the other winners were :-2, W Leigh ; 3,11 Fairbrother ;4, donors of prizes, and Incidentally mentioned that the Harry Twiss ;5, J Atkinson ;6, John Parr; 7, H Fair-I Order had nearly 1,000 members in Warrington. brother ;8, Herbert Twiss ; 9, J Weaburton ; 10, II I Bro. JOSFPIE ADaals seconded, and the vote was Higham ; 11, John Hussey ; 12, J. Mather, jun. ; 13, heartily passed. Fred Lowe ; 14, W Lawler ; 15, Joseph Lawton ; 16,1 Mr. S. N. GREEN responded, and offered in future to Edwin Twiss ; 17, Joseph Ashton ; 18, John Lockett • supplement the medal which accompanied the cup with ' 19, J Atherton • 20, S Massey ; 21, Thomas Miller ; 22, a prize of £2. (Applause.) H Lawton; 23, Peter L Edgar ; 24, T Baskerville, sen, :1 A vote of thanks was passed to the artistes, on the 25, D Harris ; 26, W J Daintith : 27 R H Holford : 28, ' proposition of Bro. R. IL Hoaeorto, seconded by Bro. W J Wakefield ; 29, J T Harrison; 30, W E Clegge ; 31, 'T. C. DucKwonni, and the Chairman was thanked on II Bennett; 32, T Baskerville, jun. the proposition of Bro. PETER EDGAR, seconded by MR. CROSPIELD AND PENSIONS. Bro. T. BASKERVILLP</p>
        <p>A capital musical programme was submitted, includ- in,, songs and duets by Mr. E. Hughes (tenor), and Mr. G. Ratcliffe (baritone), songs by Mr. A. Pemberton (taritone) and humorous items by Mr. G. Carter. Mr. G. Forster was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>DISTRIBUTION OF BOWLING PRIZES. --- Speech by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.</head>
        <p>DISTRIBUTION OF BOWLING PRIZES. --- Speech by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.</p>
        <p>OLD AE PENSIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, 111. P., said it gave him very great pleasure indeed to propose a vote of thanks to his old friend Sir Gilbert Greenall. He had the privilege of proposing a vote of thanks about a week since to Lady Greenall and in speaking of her lady- ship's activity and the great interest that she always showed in those things which made for the good of the townspeople of Warrington, be also alluded to Sir Gilbert's well known energy and, very briefly, to his success in that great department of England's activity —agriculture. (Applause.) The Chairman had just thanked Sir Gilbert for leaving his agricultural pursuits to come to that pleasant musical gathering and he (Mr. Crosfleld) would liked to take the opportunity of saying that be was quite certain everyone in Warrington, belonging either to the Conservative, the Liberal or any other party,took a personal interest and felt a pride in the successes which Sir Gilbert had achieved in so many branches of agriculture. (Applause.) People talked sometimes as if British agriculture were one of the so-called decaying industries. He did not think it was often realized that agriculture was still the industry which provided employment for a larger number of people in Great Britain than any other of the great industries that we had, not even excepting that vast industry of the cotton trade at ourown doors. He was not going to allude in any detail to</p>
        <p>THE COTTON TRADE but he would jpst take that opportunity of saying in passing that they must all hope that no misguided spirit of bitterness might be allowed to prevent proper mediation, assisting in bringing to a close a strife in tkat great industry which affected as did so many other trades in the country and which, if it were allowed to continue very much longer, must have little less than a disastrous effect on the condition of things in England and aggravate the unfortunate state of the unemployment which was exercising the minds of all thoughtful people at the present time. Let them hope that that unhappy strife would very soon be ended and that the second great industry of Britain would con- tinue to find employment for the millions who now had to look to it practically for their daily bread; and also that agriculture might continue for the benefit of Bngland to hold the premier place as that industry which provided still the largest amount of employment, to the physical benefit of the whole nation. (Applause.) Hating alluded to the very active and successful part , which Sir Gilbert had played in Connection with agriculture, on which he (Mr. Crosfield) in common with everybody else in Warrington, most heartily con- gratulated him, might he say what great pleasure it afforded him to be allowed to propose ,that vote of thanks in THE CONSERVATIVE CUM —(applause)—and how much he appreciated the kindly spirit which had prompted them to invite him to attend the gathering. "it is not the first time " con- tinued Mr. Crosfleld that I have enjoyed the hospitality of the Conservative Club. (Applause). I have amused myself on the billiard table here in years gone by when I occupied a position of greater freedom and less responsibility. (Laughter). I dare say there are a good many in this club who think that in those days I was very much better employed. (Laughter). But that is rather a delicate topic, so I don't think I will pursue it much further. (Laughter). Speaking of billiards, and without trenching upon politics, I am reminded of what occurred very close to Walton Hall in years gone by, when another member of our family—my late uncle` Mr. George Crosfield—addicted, lam afraid like the rest of the Crosfield family, to the game of billiards, was playing with Richard Cobden. In the old days Cobden used occasionally to come and see him and my uncle was rather useful at billiards, and gave him a jolly good slating. (Laughter.) But Cobden was not the least bit down in the mouth at being beaten—not at all. He turned to my uncle and said Eh, Crosfield, what a deal of time von must have wasted at this game !" (Loud laughter.) Continuing, Mr. Crosfield said he had just promised not to trench upon politics, and certainly it would be exceedingly bad testa to do so. He would not dream of doing anything of the kind except- ing in the same impartial spirit as the Chairman showed in the very wise and proper remarks he made on that most difficult question of</p>
        <p>fuovlsloN FOR OLD PEOPLE, He entirely agreed with what Councillor Fulton-Smith had said as to the extreme difficulty of that problem, and while he could not honestly under-rate the import- ance of a measure that would find provision for 500,000 or 600,000 aged people, beginning next year, he admitted at once that what the Government had done was only a preliminary step. Perhaps the Government ought to have done more—he would not go into that—but they did as much as they felt they were able to do as a first step—(hear, hear)—and while, perhaps, it would not be strictly accurate to say that we were within measurable distance of a system of contribution that would cover those circumstances, N%hether of physical disability, illness, or accident, which prevented a man, long before he reached the age of seventy, con- tinuing his active work and earning his livelihood, Lhe was sure that both Conservatives and Liberals recog- nized that provision in one way or another—and like the Chairman he spoke very guardedly—would be made in the near future. The Chairman had said it was a matter of great complexity, and that everybody must recognize. It was plainly occupying the minds of our I statesmen at the head of both parties in the State, and I he for one looked forward hopefully to the time when a solution would be found. (Hear, bear.) He was sure that those who belonged to that ancient Order would be considering a problem of that kind not {less and probably more closely than anybody else in the country. They must all subscribe to what the Chairman said about maintaining the independence, virility and self- reliance which had helped to make Britain, at home and abroad, what she was, and would still in the future enable us to maintain our great position in the world, and he did not believe</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>WORK AS EXERCISE.</head>
        <p>WORK AS EXERCISE.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Compensation Case.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Shires s Will, K.C., Mr. Mc Cleary, barrister, Manchester (instructed by Messrs. Crofton, Craven and Co., Manchester), applied on behalf of the Castle Rubber Company, Warrington, for the termina- tion or diminution of an award under the Workmen's Compensation Act made to James Sigge,l,Clifton-street, Latchford. Mr. Noel B. Go'die, barrister, Liverpool (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel), appeared for the respondent. Mr. Mc Cleary explained that the accident happened on May Bth, 1907, when the respondent while washing rubber, received a blow from an axe on his right wrist. On December 18th, 1907, an agreement was filed, the amount payable to Sigge being 91. 8d per week. The grounds of the present application were that the incapacity had ceased or considerably diminished and that the applicants were and had been ready and Ito provide the respondent with suitable employment*. The first offer of employment was made on February 15th, 1903, when the firm offered him the job of filling and emptying vulcanising pans and carrying rubber to and from the callendars. The, offer was refused, as was also a second made on June 29th, the suggested employment being rubber washing, which had previously been done by a man who could not use his right hand. The respondent, who was about 40 years of age, was in receipt of an army pension, and his wife let lodgings. Although the application was stated to be for "termination or diminution " he (Mr. Cleary) did not ask his Elonour to terminate the award, but he brought it forward as a case where, unless the man was induced to do some work, there was very little chance of his ever doing any. He thought the doctor's evidence would convince his Honour that it was one of those injuiries which never would be better without some sort of exercise. Therefore he asked for diminu- tion of the award for the purpose of inducing the respondent to do some work, as much in his own interests as that of the firm. The respondent had been in receipt of compensation for eighteen months. Edward Burdett, cashier, and Thos. O'Brien, fore- man, gave evidence as to respondent hawing been offered the work mentioned. The latter stated that appl leant had been employed by the firm ten years and had only once been suspended. He bore an excellent character. At rubber washing he could earn about 30s. a week. . . .</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph state'd that he examined the respondent on March 14th, and he formed the opinion that the exten- sor tendons of the right hand were seriously damaged. He again examined him on August sth and did not find much improvement. He had not much grasping power in the right hand and would not be able to lift heavy weights but he had some use in the hand. Exercise in the form of light work would be beneficial.—By Mr. Goldie: Respondent might become used to his old employment in time, but he did not think he would be able to do it straight away. He would have to be , educated to it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Goldie submitted that Mr. McCleary had failed to make out a case, in view of the fact that the only issue between the was whether the man was fit or not to return to the identical work he was doing at the time of the accident. The medical evidence was conclusive against the applicants. His Honour : No other work is offered, but I think I had better see the man and hear his evidence,</p>
        <p>- James Sigge, the respondent, was then called. He stated that he was an es-soldier but as he joined the army under the old system of enlistment he did not receive a pension. Hs had been employed by the firm ten years but had only worked as a washer for a week when the accident happened. He maintained that he was quite unable to do the work in his present condition.</p>
        <p>*Mc Cleary : He had not played bowls with his right hand since the accident. Dr. Collins stated that the extensor tendons at the back of the wrist were matted together and the conse- quence was that respondent could not grip with the right hand. It would be an impossibility for him to do the work offered. From a workman's point of view the wrist was useless and he considered its condition was permanent. His Honour said he was perfectly satisfied that the respondent was unable now to do the work that had been offered to him. It was possible that he might be fitted for some kind of light work but that was not the question he (his Honour) had to decide on that occasion. The issue had not been raised and he had no evidence before him on which he could find how many shillings a week the respondent could earn at some kind of light work. He therefore refused the application with costs on scale 8., and qualifying fee for Dr. Collins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>MANCHESTER'S Garr TO MR. CHURCHILL.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER'S Garr TO MR. CHURCHILL.</p>
        <p>The silver centrepiece which Mr. Winston Churchill is to receive nest Wednesday as a momento of his palitical connection with Manchester, weighs over 400 dances. The inscription mentions the "great victory he won on January 13th,1900." £2OO FOR MOTORIST'S ARREST.</p>
        <p>The Carmarthenshire County Council offers a reward of £2OO for information securing the conviction of the motorist who recently killed one of their road labourers and drove away.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. -- " By Ovalist." October has not, solar, broight about the dawn of batter prospects for the Warrington Club. They were well-beaten on Saturday at Salford, though they made a determined struggle and compiled seven points to fourteen by the Reds. The scoring margin was not great and the fact that Salford only scored two tries to one shows that they had their work cut out to win. Up to Saturday both sides had only won one match, but the odds were always on the Reds, as they were operating at the Willows. If Warrington had repeated last season's feat and halved the points they would have accomplished as much as I, for one, expected.</p>
        <p>On the second Saturday of the season, when. Widnes gave Warrington such a shock at Wilderspool, it was the forwards who were mostly blamed for the defeat. They were shockingly slow and wanting in dash, it is true, but the fault was not wholly their's. Recent events have proved this. The front rank has been strengthened, and in the last three matches the forwards have more than held their own. Certainly they are not yet as strong as tkey might be, but they have not been beaten in scrummagA work since the Widnes episode. The worst trouble now is in the back division, and it is unmistakably apparent that a considerable amount of new blood is required at three•quarter. The Salford men demonstrated the fact that Warrington's weakest point was in the third line, which has for so many years held its own in the best company. The stalwacts of old are not capable of the pace which is so necessary to-day, and this alone is preventing the combination which still exists among the men, from producing the required points. Warrington handled the ball as well as Salford on Saturday, but when it came to running and putting on the finishing touch they were badly left. In addi- tion to a good, fast wing, the club could do with a tip-top centre, and unless Fish displays better form they will need two wings. He had the distinction of being easily the worst three-quarter last week.</p>
        <p>The problem before the committee and officials is a serious and pressing one. They find themselves all at once with a second-rate team instead of one fit to do battle for the N.U. Cup, and the deficiencies cannot be remedied at five minutes notice. I have sufficient confidence in the men concerned to believe that they will leave no plan untried or stick at any expanse to secure the players required. But they want finding first, and capturing afterwards !</p>
        <p>Salford seem to have turned the corner quicker than Warrington, and on their displays against Broughton and Warrington they should go far this season. B. Alesley,the Twickenham youth,has proved a lucky find indeed. He has polled the back division right into shape, and the way he strode through the Warrington defence suggests that in him the Reds have got a coming Wilson. He is just the man Salford have low,' required to help Lomas, and future visitors to the Willows may look out for squalls. Luckily for Warrington, the burly Lomas was engaged elsewhere on Saturday, or the points against would have run into the twenties for sure.</p>
        <p>Warrington missed Brooks, of course, and it is satisfactory to learn that he acquitted himself well against Cumberland. With him and Jenkins the half-back line of the Wirepullers will stand com- parison. All they want is speedier men behind them, and the return of Shugars, Jenkins and Thomas to the front rank. Perhaps the Welsh trip this week-end will be productive of good. The league table up-to-date is as follows : For Ao.st. Per _ _</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. D. T. P. G. T. P. centage Halifax 5 5 0 0 19 21 110 2 5 19 10000 Wakefl'ldT 5 5 0 0 16 21 104 9 8 42 10000 Oldham 5 5 0 0 16 19 89 3 4 18 10000 Wigan H'ddersfild 7 6 1 0 20 27 121 13 11 50 85.71 Keighley 5 4 0 1 19 24 110 3 4 18 8500 Hunslet Runcorn 5 4 1 0 11 13 61 9 6 36 8000 Hull 5 5 2 0 22 23 128 13, 12 62 7142 Barrow 5 3 2 0 8 8 40 8 11 49 6000 Leigh Treherbert 5 3 2 0 4 9 35 5 11 43 60.00 Merthyr Swinton Leeds Batley Salford 5 2 3 0 13 7 47 16 18 83 4000 St. Helens 5 2 3 0 16 19 89 18 24 103 4000 Ebbw Yale 5 2 3 0 4 10 33 13 17 77 4000 Bradford 6 2 4 0 12 16 72 19 17 89 33.33 Hull K.R 5 1 3 1 13 13 65 14 16 70 30.00 Widnes 5 1 3 1 5 6 23 7 9 41 3000 Broughenß 5 1 3 1 8 7 37 14 12 61 3000 Barry Rochdale H 5 1 4 0 3 6 21 8 9 43 2000. Bramley Warrington 5 1 4 0 6 8 36 6 9 :89 2000 Dewsbury 5 1 4 0 11 10 52 14 16 76 0000 York 5 0 5 0 9 7 39 18 21 99 00-01 Mid- Rh'da 4 0 4 0 4 4 20 7 9 41 0000 Aberdare... 4 0 4 0 3 4 18 21 27 123 00'00</p>
        <p>Warrington" A " team played the S3lford second string to a standstill at Warrington on Saturday, and it really looked as if the Reds are worse off for reserves than Warrington. They must have had all their eggs in one basket at the Willows. Warrington ran up 2 goals and 11 tries to a try, their display having an appearance of remarkable excellence, due mostly to the weakness of the opposition. I don't think the kind of back play shown by Kay, Daintith, Landor and Oakes would have succeeded against Wigan" A." There was too much straight passing and too little cutting in by the halves and centres. Damtith makes a mistake in not going through more. Bradshaw made a very promising debut on the right wing. He has speed and trickiness which, with perseverance and training, may earn him early pro- motion. Mclntyre, the young full-back, has a good deal to learn, but he has football in him. There is some raw material of excellent quality in the "A" team, which is a hopeful augury for the future.</p>
        <p>The matches for to-day are : Merthyr v. Warrington Keighley (Y.C.) v. Hunslet Halifax (Y.C.) v. Leeds Kingston R. (Y.C.) v. Batley Dewsbury v. Hull York (Y.C.) v. Bradford Wakefield (Y.C.) v. Huidersliald Ebbw Vale v. Brarnley Broughton v. St. Helens Widnes v. Salford Swinton v. Mid Rhondda Oldham v. Leigh Rochdale v. Australia</p>
        <p>The " Kangaroos "have not, so far, played up to their reputations but they have bea ten Mid Rhondda and Bradford. They had an easy journey in \Vales, but at Bradford on Wednesday they were lucky to win by 12 points toll. Only poor goal-kicking lost the home side the match. The visitors were indebted for about half their points in both matches to Messenger who seems to be playing with even greater brilliance than on the last tour. He is about the most resourceful threequarter back and the best goal-kicker playing the game today. The Australians had another man injured, Hennessy, one of the forwards, sustaining a broken jaw. The remaining fixtures this month are Rochdale Hornets to-day, York on the Nth, Salford on the 17th, Runcorn on the 21st, Cumberland on the 24th, Leigh on the 28th, and Dewsbury on the 31st. They are hardly likely to be an unbeaten side when November dawns.</p>
        <p>Warrington left for their Welsh tour yesterday, the journey being broken at Hereford. The team will be strengthened by the return of Ike Taylor, and much disappointment will be felt if they do not gain a couple of victories at the expense of Merthyr and Ebbw Vale to-day and Monday. • --</p>
        <p>_ The players selected to make the jonrney are:— Tilley ; Fish, Dickinson,Taylor, Cartwright; Brooks, Jenkins; Dowell, P. Boardman, A. E. Boardman, Belton, Naylor, and Hewitt. Reserves, Lloyd, Jordan, and Harmer. The -Combination team to meet Pemberton at Wilderspool will be : 21`Intyre ; B. Bradshaw, Oakes, Landor, Harries; Dairrtith, Kay; Forster, Fairhurst, Harrison, Cheaters, Bradshaw, and Lunt.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lanoashire League up-to-date is as follows : For Agst. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts. Runcorn 3 3 0 ... 8 8 40 43 17... 6 Lefgh Widnes Wigan.. St. Helens 1 0 1 ... 1 1 5... 3 5 21 ... 0 Warringt'n 2 0 2 0 ... 2 2 10 ... 1 4 11 ... 0</p>
        <p>. _7</p>
        <p>To-day Winnington Park are engaged, the first team being at homs with the following players :—W Unsworth, goal; C. Plinston and T. Dixon, backs ; J Albinson, J. B. Wilkinson (Capt.) and F. Tole, halves; W. Spensley, R. J 01203, 11. Scott, E. Banks, F. Newell, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. B. Cobb. Bully-off 2.15 p.m The second team is as follows :—Goal, J. Appleton; J. Wilkinson and W. EI. Pickles (Capt.), backs ; S. R. Rurnney, A. Knight, A. Newton, halves ; H. Booth, H. Parker, W. H. Minards, C. Hackney, A. Starkey, for- wards. Referee, Mr. Harry Warburton. 'Bus le ives Bridge Foot 1.45 p.m.</p>
      </div>
