      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>UNITED KINGDOM ALLIANCE. ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE UNITED KINGDOM ALLIANCE. ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Fight for the Licensing Bill. In the annual report of the United Kingdom Alliance which is to be submitted to the General Council in Manchester next Tuesday, it is stated that the history of the organization for the past year resolves itself into an account of the arduous and prolonged struggle over the Licensing Bill. Though unfortunately that struggle is not yet carried to a successful issue, it goes on to say, a Government measure which embodies principles for which the Alliance contends, has been carried for the first time through its second reading in the House of Commons and into its Committee stage. We have to record the main stages of the conflict, so far as it has gone, in the confident belief that it will end in victory." The campaign on behalf of Local Option during the months preceding the opening of Parliament and _ • INT MOM:MONT OF THE LICENSING BILL . . _</p>
        <p>on February 27th is described, and the various and Parliamentary efforts which have led to and made possible the present Licensing Bill are recounted. The Bill from its first appearance, it is stated, received the whole-hearted support of the organization—especial appreciation being shown of the application of the principle of local option to new licences—the time- limit being regarded as unduly long. All friends of Temperance who desired the promotion of national sobriety and well-being were honestly exhorted to support the Bill. While the tone of opposition to the Bill in the House of Commons has ever been violent, in the country a carefully engineered opposition was carried on with extreme vehemence. As far as possible, the purely trade character of the opposition was masked. The case of the widow and the orphan, or the assumed danger to 'capital and investments, were put forward by the Com- mittee of Debenture-holders in Breweries, headed by Mr. Stanley Bonito?, who remembered that he was the Chairman of the Law Debenture Corporation, but forgot his Chairmanship of the North Worcestershire Breweries, Ltd. The allied traders including occupations as remote from liquor- selling as those of accountants and engineers, were duly marshalled. The ingenious and versatile talents of the protean Mr. Ernest Williams were laid under contribu- tion, the press was exploited, public-houses were ablaze ith posters and misrepresentations, petitions were -xtensively signed in licensed premises.</p>
        <p>- TPLF STRA.TEGY WAS OBVIOUS, —the ministerial majority was to be reduced by reverses at by-elections. But in spite of all this and of the fact that delay in the progress of the Bill assisted the liquor party, the Report expresses satisfaction that this attack has so far hopelessly failed. Such attack, the Executive had always foreseen was inevitable whenever a satisfactory Bill was introduced. In the first place it was met by n counter-agitation on the part of Temperance Reformers, and what especially discon- cerned the efforts of the liquor trade was the splendid loyalty and unity of the Temperance organizations. The Bill at the outset united all sections. No discord- ant note of dissent was heard from Temperance reformers in the House of Commons It is not too much to say that party politicians have been surprised by the steadiness, the unity, and ;the statesmanship of Temper- ance reformers who have not always been credited by their critics with the possession of those qualities. The support given to the Bill by the Churches, especially the Church of England, is dwelt noon ; as also is the support rendered by the Labour M.P.'s. It is admitted that the unity of Temperance reformers, the enthusiasm of the Churches, the support of the Labour men, and an energetic campaign in the country would have proved of insufficient avail had it not been for the resolute determination of the Government. Friends and critics will equally admit the unflinching courage of Mr. Asquith and his colleagues. The splendid utterances of the Government leaders bore their fruit among the followers. When the three days second reading debate took place, the whole financial argument of the liquor trade was formally put forward by the liquor trade'schampions. But it was felt that the Government had a solid and substantial defence for the measure. The ranks of the defenders of the Bill remain firm and unbroken. The hostile amendment to the second reading was defeated by a magnificent majority of 230. Not more than eight dissentient Liberals voted against the Bill. Their loss was offset by the support given to the Bill by two Scottish and five Irish Unionists. The attack of the liquor traders had failed. They almost avowedly gave up the House of Commons as unassailable from their point of view. A new stage opened, in which they concentrated their forces on the by-elections in the hope of influencing the House of Lords.</p>
        <p>Supporters of the Bill are urged to slacken none of their efforts. Earnest work is indispensable right up to Christmas. The liquor trade counts on</p>
        <p>THE TREND- OF THE BY-ELECTIONS against the Government to encourage the Lords to reject or wreck the Bill. The adverse trend of the by-elections began some months before the Licensing Bill was introduced. Twenty-five by-elections have taken place in Great Britain during the last twelve months—that is, since the Government definitely announced their intention of proposing a Licensing Bill for this year. If we are told that we must takel the verdict of the contests as a plebiscite on the Bill, a striking fact emerges. The votes polled by candidates in favour of the Bill showed a decided marjority over the votes polled by candidates opposed to it. In 17 contested by-elections in Great Britain (ignoring eight for reasons given) the votes in favour of candidates supporting the Bill were 100,881, against 82,853—majority, 17,925. A careful study of the ministerial reverses at by-elections shows that they have been largely due to the split between the Liberal and Labour vote. It is the belief then of the Executive Committee that the House of Lords will pass the Bill, which it is held is demanded by the best thought of the nation. Rumours to the contrary effect are from time to time circulated with the obvious motive of inducing the Government to weaken the Bill in the Houses of Commons. They can be disregarded. It is not believed that the Lords will refuse to listen to the earnest and serious appeal of the best part of the nation, praying for a tardy deliverance from gigantic evils." With regard to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>TH-E. SUGGESTED HICH-LICENCE SCHEME</head>
        <p>TH-E. SUGGESTED HICH-LICENCE SCHEME</p>
        <p>in case of the rejection of the Bill by the Lords, it is declared the Temperance reformers are not to be bribed or lured by the chance of a scheme of the kind, into any acquiescence at the thought of the possible wrecking of the Bill. The loss of this Bill would be a supreme disaster to the nation, but it would not be the end of the fight. In spite of the check, Temper- ance reformers would continue their crusade with unabated energy ; and doubtless before the proposed fifteen years time limit were over,a chance would coma again without the necessity of giving the present exorbitant notice to the liquor trade. Sections of the report contain particulars as to the extent and character of an unprecedentedly arduous campaign throughout the country on behalf of the Bill, a summary of the Parliamentary debates on the Bill, with certain valuable Government returns, a full account of the by-elections, the year's history of the Movement in Wales, Scotland and Ireland, as well as in America and Canada. The National Bill, the deaths of the year, and a reference to the memorials to the late Sir Wilfrid Lawson, comprise other parts of a comprehensive document which concludes by observing that nothing so thoroughly educates opinion on any subject as the public discussion of a Government Bill. The doctrines and-hopes of Temperance reformers have become the commonplaces of ordinary talk. An entirely new situation has been created. " But this state of affairs imposes upon your Executive, and upon the whole organization, fresh and important duties. First, it is an urgent duty, as we have seen, to keep the existing agitation alive, and to direct attention to two principal points. The time limit must not be lengthened under any circumstances, and it must be made manifest to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>HOUSE OF LORDS</head>
        <p>THE HOUSE OF LORDS</p>
        <p>that the national support the Bill fs fully sustained. But further, if the Bill passes without serious mutila- tions—and of this your Executive repeats the expression of its confident hope—it will be necessary for your Executive to take instant counsel with its principal supporters, and to draw out a plan of vigorous and decisive action. The passing of a Bill which enlarges the discretion of the local Justices, which subjects all "new licences" to the veto of the local voters, and which promises within a fixed period to make Local Veto of universal application, is a piece of legislation which enormously increases the resp-msibility of the people. Enlarged power means always increased responsibility. The democracy is asked to save itself, to employ the law, to secure its own liberty. A mighty weapon of self-defence is placed in the bands of the people ; it will be the task of the United Kingdom Alliance to teach them how to use it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-10-10_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>(Continued fora page 3.) ST._ CLEMENT'S MISS lON.</p>
        <p>The harvest celebrations at the above mission com- menced on Thursday evening last. The church was beautifully decorated with flowers, fruit and corn and presented a striking appearance. The Rev. F. C. Timmins, vicar of Smeeth, Kent, occupied the pulpit and took his sermon from the 28th chapter of Isiah, 28th verse:—" Bread corn is bruised." Solo.; were effectively rendered by Mr. Smith and Mr. Davies. The collections were in aid of the church funds.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH. The harvest thanksgiving services were commenced at this church on Thursday evening when a large congregation assembled. The preacher was the Rev. A. Wood, of Packington, who took for his text "And a man shall be as an hiding place from the wind, and a covert from the tempest ; as rivers of water in a dry, place, as a shadow of a great rock in a thirsty land. (Isaiah xxxii., 2). lhe choir gave special music most creditably, Mr. E. Wilkes presiding at the organ. The church waq lavishlir decorated with the usual harvest products and presented a very beautiful appearance.</p>
        <p>EMHANUEL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival was celebrated at this church on Sunday and the services were largely attended. The Rev. F. Vaughan preached morning and evening. At the morning service the choir sang an anthem entitled "Clap your hands." A children's flower service was held in the afternoon, the main feature being the repetition of passages from the Scriptures from memory by the children. In the evening there was a large gathering which showed a marked increase upon previous years. The choir rendered the anthem " While the earth remaineth," very creditably. Two handsome solid brass reading desks have been pre- sented to the church by two members of the congrega- tion and the church was also newly carpeted. The collections were in aid of the church funds.</p>
        <p>Marriages. DicKlNSON—PovEv—October 4th, at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev. T. W. Barber, John Thomas Dickinson, to Mary Reddish Povey. HUGHES—WELLS—October 4th. at the Parish Church, Warrington, by the Rev. T. W. Barber, John Hughes, to Alice Ann Wells. IN MEMORIAM. In Loving Memory of Henry Hewitt Dutton, who died October 11th, 1893. "Gone, but not forgotten."</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and published by Sons Werarns &amp; Co., at The Examiner General Printing Worka- Bearsey-street, and published by them at the same place, and also at The Examiner Offices, Viotoria-roaa VVIDNIts 32, George-street, ST. HEMS: 3, Market street, EARLESTOWN; Church-street Runowla Oct. 10th, 1908.</p>
      </div>
