        <p>opponents out. The winners were much the smarter side, every buy playing well. Christ Church boys -visited St. Mary's, and though they held their own up to the interval, they were out- played in the second half. Valente, at left hack, played well and Connor shot two good goals. Williams played a good game kr the losers. St. James' went down to Parochial by two clear goals on the latter's ground. The losers probably would have done better if the backs, instead of touch-finding, had fed the forwards. , Wycliffe still kept up their good form and on Satur- day defeated the Trinity team by 2-0. St. Barnabas' lost their first League points, as a result of their meeting with Heathside, to whom they succumbed by 5-2. The centre forward of the winners showed up well, while Hughes at full-back kicked and tackled with rare judgment. The right back of St. Barnabas', Crozier, played wolf, but at times kicked rather wildly. Arpley-street, with a greatly improved team, defeated Silver-street by three goals to one. A feature of the match was the running and shooting of the winners' wing men. For the losers, Nurse played a good game and scored their goal. The Warrington boys are drawn at home to Bootle in the first round of the English Schools' Shield Competition. FRIENDLY MATCH. STOCKTON HEATH CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE A.F.C. V. LATCHFORD WESLEYANS. This match was decided at Stockton Heath on Satur- day. Stockton Heath attacked and scored after 15 minutes' play. The home team pressed, and from a corner scored their second goal. Soon after J. Sankey tidied a third. Gifford, for Latchford, scored from a penalty. The interval arrived with Stockton Heath I leading by three goals to one. Resuming J. Sankey raced past the opposing backs and scored the fourth goal, and Miller scored a fifth. Resttlt :—Stockton Heath, five ; Latchford Wesleyans, one. GOLF. THE WARRINGTON CLUB. The seventh round in the competition for the captain's prize was decided on Saturday. W. S. Dutton qualified in the first division with a return of S 3-9-74 and W Whitely in the second division with 92-18-78. W Whitely took the first sweep and C. H. Unsworth the second. Scores : FIRST DIVISION. Gross. Ircap. Net. 'W. S. Dutton B3 ... 9 ... 74 J. Ellison B9 ... 12 ... 77 C. H. Unsworth 5l ... 14 ... 77 L. Holden B3 ... 5 ... 78 J. M. Murray B5 ... 6 ... 79 H. Kirkconnel W. Greenhouse L. A. Voisey Rev. Lee Jones L. Fai t clough 93 ... 10 ... 83 C. G. Hutchings It. M. Roberts F. S. Horner C. G. Stone lO3 9 ... 91 E. A. Connor SECOND DIVISION. W. Whitely J. E. 13irtles E. J. Hall 95 ... 16 ... 79 W. Richardson Rev. H. A. Lester 9B ... 18 ... 80 M. H. Brady J. Hepherd J. E. 0. Thompson W. H. Ilellian In the September monthly bogey compatition in the first division, F. Pendlebriry qualified with a return of all square with the Colonel, and in the second division, B. S. Newcombe qualified with two down. MERSEY CLUB. Considerable interest was taken in the competition for the Parker Silver Shield, which took place recently on the above links. On Saturday afternoon Mr. C. E, Parker (who together with Mrs. Parker,are the donors), made the presentation to the winner, Mr. T. H. Ambler, Ash Cottage, Penketh. The shield is a new feature, and was open to be played for by all members of the club. I Mr. Ambler is to be congratulated on having the honour I of being the first to hold the handsome trophy. Mr. I and Mrs. Parker entertained the members to an excellent I repast. This over, Mr. W. S. Knowles, of Widnes I (captain of the club), thanked Mr. and Mrs. Parker, for ' the kindly interest they bad taken in the club, and said I he felt sure that so long as they bad their co-operation , the success would continue to attend their functions ' and competitions. (Applause.) Mrs. Parker responded and said that they were pleased a Penketh resident ! member had been the first to win the shield. Mr.l Miniati (secretary) and Mr. Medley (treasurer), also I , made a few remarks concerning the welfare of the club. I</p>
        <p>PADGATE. WESLEY Guit.D.—The Wesleyans of Padgate were on Monday evening favoured with a visit from the Bev. Trevor H. Davies, of Edinburgh, who delivered a lecture entitled "Touring with an English Apostle." The chair was taken by Mr. J. G. Withenshaw, of Penketh. The subject of the lecture was the great founder of the denomination, John Wesley, the materials being skilfully gathered and woven from the famous " Journals "—a , work which, Mr. Davies said, would stand side by side ; with Walpole's " Letters " and Boswells " Johnson "1 as a revelation of the conditions and manners of the English people in the eighteenth century. Referring to Mr. Augustine Birrell's recent laudation of Wesley's Journal, the Lecturer said that he agreed with it heartily in almost every particular, but be could not understand the statement that the book was lacking in humour. The influence of Wesley—wonderful when h was alive—had been immensely greater since his death and would roll on with ever-increasing volume throughout eternity. ' Mr. Davies was heartily thanked, on the motion of the Chairman. Theft from a Pawn-Shop. Elizabeth Vernon, of 17, Athei ton-street, was charged at the Borough Police Court cm Tuesday with stealing from the shop of Thomas Domville, of 31a, Priestley-stret, on Monday, the .sth of September, a pair of carpet slippers valued at Is. Od. She was further charged Nv t h stealing a brass jockey 'oar valued at 7s. Cid. on September Ist ; she was further charged with stealing from the shop of Messrs. Stubbs of 318, Loyely-lane,on September 11th, an alarm clock valued at 2s. 6d. —She pleaded guilty to all three charges.—Detective Meacock made inquiries and it was found that the first two articles had been pledged at Messrs. Stubbs by the prisoner. He received the articles from John Stubbs and in company with Detective Clough visited the prisoner's house. Witness questioned her about the thefts, and she replied, "I have taken nothing." She then went into the back kitchen, and witness heard her toll her husband that she had taken the goods. Sho was brought to the Police Station. After being duly cautioned she con• fessecl to all three charges. Mr. Stubbs, her husband, end his wife was not responsible for her actions Dr. Ferguson said that prisoner had been suffering for a month. She bad attempted to take her life, and had also left home several times. Ho thought it was I caused through drink. When asked if she would go home the replied that she did not want to. —The I Chairman said that she would either have to go to a home or to prisc n, He said he would adjourn the case for a month to see if her husband could find a home I for her.</p>
